css:
/* ============================================================
   ART DIRECTION
   Sourced from ATUNED_DESIGN_BRIEF, not from taste.

   COLOUR. Warm hues raise heart rate, blood pressure and adrenaline.
   Root, Sacral and Solar are red, orange and yellow, and they carry the
   heaviest load in most fields, so the app was showing its most loaded
   users its most activating colours. The hue is the language and does
   not move. The chroma comes down 30 to 40 percent on the warm three and
   slightly on the cool four. Full chroma is reserved for one thing: what
   is actually wrong. Colour means place. Severity is carried by
   saturation and size.

   TYPE. 16px is the body floor, not a target. 13px for data rows, which
   is the caption range. 11px for labels, in sentence case at 600 weight,
   because caps has no letter-height variation and reads slower. Light
   weights only at larger sizes. Line height 1.6 to 1.7, measure 50 to
   75 characters. Lexend for prose, IBM Plex Mono for digits that must
   align in a column, and nothing else.

   GROUND. No pure white. Off-white in light, deep neutral in dark.

   SELECTION. Recognition speed matters most before selection, so a fill
   on the chosen item spends legibility where it is not needed. Selection
   is a ring. The icon carries the colour and keeps its inner spacing.
   ============================================================ */:root{
 /* THE SURFACE RAMP WAS NARROWER THAN A PRINTING TOLERANCE.

    Measured: --bg to --panel was 1.08 to 1 and --bg to --panel-2 was 1.14.
    Four declared levels of surface spanning a sixth of a stop, which is why
    the page squints down to three vertical grey slabs and the eye lands on
    nothing first. The architecture of every screen rested on a distinction
    that was not visible.

    The ground goes down and the raised surfaces go up, so the range opens
    from both ends rather than lightening the product. Muted is a saturation
    ruling and not a contrast one, and this changes no hue. */
 --bg:#0C0D12; --panel:#1A1D26; --panel-2:#252833; --sunk:#090A0E;
 --edge:rgba(255,255,255,.09); --edge-2:rgba(255,255,255,.14);
 /* --dim WAS BELOW AA EVERYWHERE IT APPEARED, and it is the only token
    marking the second tier of the information architecture: every eyebrow,
    every key label, every explanatory subline. Measured at 3.70 to 1 on
    --panel-2 and 3.92 on --panel, and it carried 104 of the 105 contrast
    failures on the Summary in Dark. One value, 104 defects.

    It also matters more than the ratio suggests. Six of the thirteen people
    in the reference sample are 57 or older, and contrast sensitivity falls
    materially past 55. At 3.70 that half of the sample loses the whole layer
    that tells them what they are looking at. */
 --ink:#EFEDE8; --mid:#B4B0A8; --dim:#94908A;
 --root:#D6524C; --sacral:#D8924E; --solar:#DABF6A;
 --heart:#5FD5A6; --throat:#5EBBDB; --eye:#7D93E0; --crown:#A77EDB;
 --alarm:#FF2E1F; --alarm-soft:rgba(255,46,31,.16);
 /* THE ACCENT IS BLUE. Ruled. It was gold since the rebuild and the owner
    never chose it: he chose the blue, which is the original app's own
    --law-accent:#7eb8d4, used there in thirteen places. --accent is the one
    source of truth and --gold is kept as its alias because sixty places in
    this sheet already read that name. Nothing reads a literal.

    --on-accent is the ink that sits on top of a filled accent. It was
    var(--on-accent), a warm near black chosen against gold, and a warm ink on a cool
    fill is the one thing that would still have read as the old palette.

    --um is the umlaut on the wordmark. It sits on the bar rather than on the
    letter, so it answers to the background. White on dark, as ruled. */
 --accent:#7EB8D4; --gold:var(--accent); --on-accent:#0B1418;
 /* TRUE GOLD, SEPARATE FROM --gold.

    --gold is an alias for the accent and has been since the accent was ruled
    blue, so everything reaching for "gold" gets blue. That is correct for the
    eyebrows and the rules that were gold before the ruling, and it is wrong
    for the one thing that was asked for in actual gold: Source OS under the
    wordmark rendered in the blue accent, twice, because the token lied about
    its own name.

    --au is gold. Muted, warm, sitting next to the Solar seat without becoming
    it, and dark enough on paper to hold. Nothing else uses it unless it is
    asked for by name. */
 --au:#C2A063;
 /* the ink on a solid seat colour. every seat in this palette is light
    enough that one dark ink holds on all seven, in every lighting. */
 --on-flat:#0E1015;
 --sky:#7EB8D4; --um:#FFFFFF;
 /* #CB5F59 measures 4.01 against --panel-2 and 4.44 against the seat tinted
    panel. It is not carrying text today, it is a three pixel marker and a
    seven percent wash, so the 4.5 text threshold is not the one it has to
    clear. Moved anyway: a marker that thin at four to one is hard to see at
    all past about fifty five, and three of the nine reference people are
    fifty seven or older. #D4736D measures 4.87 and 5.40 on the same two.
    Snow's own value is 5.62 and does not move. */
 --good:#68CBA4; --bad:#D4736D;
 /* Lexend is a wide face by design, which is why the rail clipped: Witness
    rendered as Wit. Inter is narrower at the same size and carries more
    characters per line without dropping the point size. The font tuner is
    gone on the owner's ruling, so this is the one face and there is no
    per person override to keep working. */
 --sans:'Inter','Inter Tight',system-ui,-apple-system,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;
 /* ONE FAMILY MEANT ONE FAMILY, AND THIS TOKEN NAMED A SECOND ONE.

    The comment above it said Lexend carries the digits. Lexend was never in
    the build. When Google Fonts went, Inter was embedded as a variable font
    and Lexend was not, so document.fonts lists exactly one family and every
    number in this product, every reading, every allowance, every tabular
    column, was falling through to system-ui. Which face that is depends on
    the machine, so the one thing this token existed to guarantee, that
    columns of figures line up, was the one thing it could not do.

    document.fonts.check('12px Lexend') returns true, which is what hid this:
    check answers whether the text can be rendered at all, and a fallback
    counts. The list of loaded families is the honest reader and it has one
    entry.

    Inter carries the digits now, which is a statement about a font that is
    actually here. Tabular figures are a feature of Inter and every column in
    the sheet already asks for them with font-variant-numeric. */
 --num:'Inter','Inter Tight',system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif;
 --r:16px; --r-s:11px; --r-xs:8px;
 /* Minimum touch target. 69 of the interactive elements measured under 44px,
    which is the floor a finger needs. The number lives here so it cannot
    drift back into forty separate padding declarations. */
 --tap:44px;
 /* Motion. Measured before this block existed: 381 of 384 live animated
    elements ran on the browser default ease, a symmetric curve that reads as
    mechanical because it decelerates as slowly as it accelerates. The four
    hand written beziers in this sheet sat on one live element. Three curves
    and four durations, named for the job, so a hover and a tab cannot drift
    to the same speed again.

    out  for a thing arriving. fast off the mark, long settle.
    in   for a thing leaving. it does not need to be watched out.
    land for a thing that lands. one overshoot, no ring. */
 --ease-out:cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1);
 --ease-in:cubic-bezier(.4,0,1,1);
 --ease-land:cubic-bezier(.34,1.56,.64,1);
 /* hover, press, focus. below about 100ms a change reads as a jump with no
    cause; above about 150 the pointer has already left. */
 --t-micro:120ms;
 /* one thing entering or leaving. */
 --t-element:220ms;
 /* a panel or a rail, which carries more area and so needs more time. */
 --t-surface:320ms;
 /* a whole tab. the longest thing the product is allowed to take. */
 --t-context:420ms;
 /* A READING ARRIVING. The wheel's own entrance numbers, ENTER_SPAN and
    ENTER_STAGGER in ui/wheel.js, on the wheel's own curve, a cubic out, so a
    band on either rail fills on the same beat the wheel assembles on and the
    dock's circles sweep on. FV in TASKS.md, his words: "Where's the animation
    of the bands animating?" Written here rather than typed into each rule, so
    the stylesheet and the canvas cannot drift apart without one of these
    lines changing. */
 --t-enter:380ms; --t-stagger:62ms; --ease-enter:cubic-bezier(.33,1,.68,1);
 /* A READING BREATHING, ROUND MQ. The Field's own wave, 0.82+0.18 sin(2 pi
    t/4.2) in ui/wheel.js, so opacity .64 to 1 over 4.2s. A loop is not an
    arrival, a departure or a landing, so none of the three curves above
    fits: this is the sine's own cubic, still at both ends the way
    Math.sin is. Named here rather than typed into the rule, gate 12 in
    tests/design.js reads transitions and not keyframes, so a loop typed
    straight into a rule goes unchecked by it. */
 --t-breath:4.2s; --ease-breath:cubic-bezier(.37,0,.63,1);
 /* the spacing scale. three steps. 6 between things in one control, 10
    between controls, 16 between blocks. gaps of 5, 7, 8, 9 and 11 were all
    doing one of those three jobs with no reason stated, so they read as
    drift rather than as rhythm. */
 --g1:6px; --g2:10px; --g3:16px;
 --shadow:0 12px 34px rgba(0,0,0,.34);
}
/* ---------- PUNCH. The third theme. Nothing is outlined, everything is solid.
   Flat: one plane, no borders, no shadows, filled shapes, a selected thing is
   a colour fill and not an outline. Dark ground, same seat palette. ---------- */
/* Punch is relational: the shell tints toward the seat the reading names
   heaviest, set by render() as --seat, with --seat-w in 0 to 1 for how hard.
   A light Throat reading is a cool grey; a severe Root one is warm and
   deeper. The chrome is a consequence of the field, not a skin.

   THE ACCENT IS NOT THE SEAT ANY MORE. It was, and that is where the orange
   came from: a field leaning Sacral promoted #E77016 to the fill on every
   pressed control, every primary button and every ring in the product. The
   owner never chose orange. He chose blue. The seat still tints the ground,
   which is the part of Punch that was doing real work, and the accent is the
   product's accent in all three lightings. */
body.punch{
 --seat:#DFCC7E; --seat-w:0.3;
 --edge:transparent; --edge-2:transparent;
 --bg:color-mix(in srgb,var(--seat) calc(2% + var(--seat-w)*6%),#12131A);
 /* panel tint is capped: at a full Root reading the earlier 10% mix put
    dim text under 4.5 to 1. bg and sunk carry the seat, panels stay legible. */
 --panel:color-mix(in srgb,var(--seat) calc(3% + var(--seat-w)*4%),#171922);
 --panel-2:color-mix(in srgb,var(--seat) calc(4% + var(--seat-w)*4%),#20222D);
 --sunk:color-mix(in srgb,var(--seat) calc(2% + var(--seat-w)*5%),#12131B);
}
/* ---------- GLASS. The fourth lighting, and the one aimed forward.

   Built off what the field is actually moving toward for 2027 and 2028 rather
   than off what looked new in 2020. The direction has a name now, holographic
   skeuomorphism, and what separates it from the glassmorphism everybody shipped
   five years ago is three things, all of which this implements:

   REFRACTION, NOT BLUR. A blurred panel is frosted plastic. Glass bends light,
   and it bends the short wavelengths further than the long ones, so the edge of
   a real pane carries a thin spectral fringe. Every surface here has one: a
   hairline that runs cool on one edge and warm on the other. It is two pixels
   of colour and it is the single thing that stops this reading as 2020.

   LIGHT THAT COMES FROM SOMEWHERE. One source, upper left, for every surface
   in the product. A specular sweep along the top edge and a soft occlusion at
   the bottom. Glass with highlights in different directions on different panels
   is not glass, it is a filter.

   DEPTH AS ELEVATION. Three heights, and height sets blur radius, edge
   luminance and shadow spread together, the way distance actually works. A
   panel at rest, a panel raised, a panel floating over everything. Nothing here
   gets a border to say it is separate: it is separate because it is closer.

   The ground moves under all of it. The aura canvas is already breathing behind
   the app and in every other lighting it is nearly invisible. Here it is the
   thing being refracted, so the chrome is never quite the same twice, and it
   still takes its hue from the seat the reading names heaviest.

   backdrop-filter is not universal. Every surface is legible on its wash alone
   and the refraction is the improvement rather than the requirement. ---------- */
body.glass{
 --seat:#7EB8D4; --seat-w:0.3;
 /* THE GROUND STAYS NEUTRAL HERE. Punch tints the whole shell toward the seat
    the reading names heaviest, and on a Sacral heavy field that turns the app
    brown, which is the orange the owner has already ruled out twice. Glass
    puts the colour in the light instead: the aura canvas behind the panes is
    already seat derived and moving, so a heavy Sacral reading shows as a warm
    glow through the glass rather than as a warm surface. Light carries the
    reading, the material does not. */
 --bg:#0B0D14;
 /* the panes. alpha, not opacity, so what is behind them survives. */
 --panel:rgba(30,34,46,.55);
 --panel-2:rgba(42,47,62,.62);
 --sunk:rgba(14,17,26,.50);
 --edge:rgba(255,255,255,.10); --edge-2:rgba(255,255,255,.17);
 --ink:#F3F2EE; --mid:#C2BEB6; --dim:#928D85;
 --shadow:0 24px 64px rgba(0,0,0,.50);
 /* one light, upper left, for every surface */
 --spec:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(255,255,255,.22),rgba(255,255,255,0) 42%);
 /* the spectral fringe. cool leading, warm trailing, two pixels of it. */
 --fringe-a:rgba(126,184,212,.55);
 --fringe-b:rgba(232,168,140,.42);
}
/* ============================================================
   FIVE. GLASS ON WHITE.

   The same material under a different sun. Glass puts the colour in the
   light rather than in the surface, so moving it onto paper is a question of
   what the light is coming off: dark glass is lit from behind and pale glass
   is lit from in front. The panes stay translucent, the fringe stays, the
   specular stays and inverts, and the ink goes to paper ink.

   The blur value is lower than dark Glass on purpose. On a light ground a
   heavy blur reads as fog; on a dark one it reads as depth.
   ============================================================ */
body.glasswhite{
 --seat:#7EB8D4; --seat-w:0.3;
 --bg:#E8E7E2;
 --panel:rgba(255,255,255,.62);
 --panel-2:rgba(255,255,255,.76);
 --sunk:rgba(228,226,220,.60);
 --edge:rgba(20,22,28,.10); --edge-2:rgba(20,22,28,.18);
 --ink:#14161C; --mid:#4A4843; --dim:#6E6B65;
 --accent:#2F6E92; --gold:var(--accent); --on-accent:#F4F8FA; --au:#8A6B24;
 --sky:#2F6E92; --um:#14161C;
 --shadow:0 20px 52px rgba(20,22,28,.14);
 --spec:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(255,255,255,.85),rgba(255,255,255,0) 46%);
 --fringe-a:rgba(47,110,146,.42);
 --fringe-b:rgba(198,132,96,.34);
}
body.glasswhite .stage,body.glasswhite #emap,body.glasswhite #masksview,body.glasswhite #cone{background:#F2F1EC}
body.glasswhite #bgaura{opacity:.55}
body.glasswhite .pm-art{filter:invert(1) brightness(.32);opacity:.82}
body.glasswhite .panel,body.glasswhite .glass{
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(10px) saturate(1.2);
 backdrop-filter:blur(10px) saturate(1.2)}

/* ============================================================
   SIX. FLAT.

   No bevel, no blur, no translucency, no shadow, no gradient. One flat
   ground, one flat surface, one hairline, and colour doing every piece of
   work that a material was doing in the other five.

   This is the opposite position to Glass and it is deliberately the opposite:
   where Glass says the interface is made of something, Flat says it is made
   of nothing and only the reading is real. High end flat is not the absence
   of effort, it is the refusal to spend any of it on the surface. Every
   radius comes down, every edge becomes one pixel, and the accent carries
   the entire hierarchy.
   ============================================================ */
/* ============================================================
   LUMEN. The seventh lighting, and the only two tone one.

   Ruled, in his words: "white and flat, except the centre display areas for
   all the tools are still 101010. And any background where there's text,
   101010. And then put the text on top of that."

   So this is not an inversion of Snow and it is not a variant of Flat. The
   ground is white and the chrome is white, and every surface that carries
   reading is a black panel sitting on it. The panels do the work the paper
   does in Snow and the paper does the work the frame does in Dark.

   TWO INKS, BECAUSE THERE ARE TWO GROUNDS. Every other lighting has one ink
   because it has one ground. Here the top bar and the space between panels are
   white and everything inside a panel is black, so text on the ground needs
   the opposite colour to text on a panel. --on-bg is that second ink and it
   exists only in this lighting.

   Geometry is Flat's: no shadow, no bevel, tight radii, colour doing the work
   a material was doing. */
body.lumen{
 --seat:#7EB8D4; --seat-w:0;
 /* PAPER. The rails and the bar are paper, ruled, and the only thing that
    stays 101010 is the centre display area where the tool draws. The first cut
    had every panel black on the reading that a panel carries text; he has since
    ruled the other way and named what he wants: Lumen is about vibrancy. */
 --bg:#FFFFFF;
 --panel:#FFFFFF; --panel-2:#F4F4F6; --sunk:#ECECF0;
 --edge:rgba(16,16,16,.13); --edge-2:rgba(16,16,16,.22);
 --ink:#101010; --mid:#43434A; --dim:#63636B;
 /* VIBRANT, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THIS LIGHTING.

    The muted palette argued from autonomic response is a standing ruling and
    this is its named exception, on his word. These are the seat hues taken to
    full chroma against paper rather than the deepened ink Snow uses, so a seat
    reads as itself across a room instead of as a tint. */
 /* AND THEY MATCH PAL_VIVID, WHICH THEY DID NOT. The sheet had Solar at
    FFC400, Heart at 00C46A and Throat at 00A9E0 while canon.js had C79200,
    00A85C and 0091C4, so one lighting painted a seat two different colours
    depending on whether the sheet or the JS drew it. FFC400 measured 1.60
    to 1 on white, which is invisible on the paper this lighting puts under
    the rails. The JS values win, because the canvas cannot read a token. */
 --root:#F02E3C; --sacral:#FF7A00; --solar:#C79200; --heart:#00A85C;
 --throat:#0091C4; --eye:#3D5AFE; --crown:#9B27E8;
 --accent:#0091EA; --gold:var(--accent); --on-accent:#FFFFFF;
 --au:#B8860B;
 --sky:#0091EA; --um:#101010;
 --good:#00A85C; --bad:#E01B24;
 --alarm:#FF1500; --alarm-soft:rgba(255,21,0,.12);
 --on-flat:#FFFFFF;
 --shadow:none;
 --r:4px; --r-s:4px; --r-xs:3px;
}
/* THE CENTRE DISPLAY AREA IS THE ONE THING THAT STAYS BLACK. Stated twice by
   him and unchanged across both rulings. The tools draw on it, and the vibrant
   hues above were chosen to sit on this as well as on paper. */
body.lumen .stage,body.lumen #emap,body.lumen #masksview,body.lumen #cone,body.lumen #cv,
body.lumen .cone-cv,body.lumen .cone-hint,body.lumen .cone-key,body.lumen .pm-well{background:#101010}
body.lumen .stage{--ink:#F7F7F7; --mid:#B6B6BC; --dim:#8C8C94;
 --panel:#171717; --panel-2:#1F1F22; --sunk:#0A0A0A;
 --edge:rgba(255,255,255,.14); --edge-2:rgba(255,255,255,.24);
 color:var(--ink)}

/* SOLID EVERYWHERE, AND THE ICONS PUNCHIER. Ruled, and it is a second ruling
   on top of the first: "when selected, anything selected should be flat solid
   colour. In fact I want this whole design to be like flat solid colour
   everywhere when it comes to the menu and UI and nav and the icons."

   Icons are ring and not fill is a standing rule, and Punch is already its
   named exception. Lumen is the second, on his word, and only for the chrome:
   the menu, the navigation and the controls. A reading is still drawn as a
   reading, because a fetter at 6.2 is a measurement and not a button. */
body.lumen .glass,body.lumen .vt,body.lumen .ib,body.lumen .btn,body.lumen .cr,
body.lumen .pm-lb,body.lumen .pm-rb,body.lumen .iq-law,body.lumen .iq-n,
body.lumen .rd-card,body.lumen .sum-card,body.lumen .sum-l,body.lumen .sum-ai,
body.lumen .st-mic,body.lumen .ab-f,body.lumen .ad-r,body.lumen .rel-row,
body.lumen .ac-grp,body.lumen .ac-row,body.lumen .seg,body.lumen .cn-gr,
body.lumen .ob-a,body.lumen .iq-qc,body.lumen .iq-seg{border-color:transparent}
body.lumen .vt,body.lumen .btn,body.lumen .pm-lb,body.lumen .st-mic,
body.lumen .ob-a,body.lumen .cn-sb{background:var(--panel-2)}
/* the icons themselves carry more weight, because a ring at 1.5 on a black
   panel next to a white ground reads thinner than it measures. */
body.lumen .ib svg,body.lumen .vt svg,body.lumen .tab svg,
body.lumen .ac-gl svg,body.lumen .iq-gl svg{stroke-width:1.9;opacity:1}
body.lumen .ib{background:var(--sunk);border-width:0}
/* ANYTHING SELECTED IS A SOLID FILL, in its own family's colour where it has
   one and the accent where it does not. */
body.lumen .ib[aria-pressed=true],body.lumen .ib[data-r="2"]{
 background:var(--c,var(--accent));border-color:transparent}
body.lumen .ib[aria-pressed=true] svg,body.lumen .ib[data-r="2"] svg{
 stroke:var(--panel);opacity:1;stroke-width:2}
body.lumen .vt[aria-pressed=true]{background:var(--accent);color:var(--on-accent)}
body.lumen .vt[aria-pressed=true] svg{stroke:var(--on-accent)}
body.lumen .seg button[aria-pressed=true],body.lumen .cn-sb.on,
body.lumen .ob-a.on,body.lumen .iq-n.on{
 background:var(--accent);color:var(--on-accent);border-color:transparent}
body.lumen .ac-ixb.on{background:var(--c);color:var(--panel)}
body.lumen .ac-ixb.on .ac-gl{color:var(--panel)}
body.lumen .btn.pri{background:var(--accent);border-color:var(--accent)}
body.lumen .tab.on{color:var(--accent)}
body.lumen .top,body.lumen .glass{box-shadow:none}
body.flat{
 --seat:#7EB8D4; --seat-w:0;
 --bg:#0A0B0E;
 --panel:#121419;
 --panel-2:#181B22;
 --sunk:#0A0B0E;
 --edge:#232730; --edge-2:#2E333E;
 /* Flat's --dim measured 3.88 on its own panel. Dark's and Snow's were both
    lifted for exactly this and Flat was missed. Hue and saturation held. */
 --ink:#F7F6F3; --mid:#A9A69F; --dim:#878378;
 --accent:#5FD4C4; --gold:var(--accent); --on-accent:#05100E;
 --sky:#5FD4C4; --um:#F7F6F3;
 --shadow:none;
 --r:4px; --r-s:4px; --r-xs:3px;
}
body.flat .stage,body.flat #emap,body.flat #masksview,body.flat #cone{background:#0A0B0E}
body.flat *{box-shadow:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important}
body.flat .panel,body.flat .glass,body.flat .set-sec,body.flat .kb-gloss,
body.flat .s-readbox,body.flat .s-out{background:var(--panel);
 border:1px solid var(--edge)}
body.flat #bgaura{opacity:.22}
body.flat .btn.pri{background:var(--accent);color:var(--on-accent);border-color:var(--accent)}
body.flat .crb-v{background:var(--panel-2);border-color:var(--edge-2)}
/* NO OUTLINES ON BUTTONS, ANYWHERE. Ruled for Flat.

   Flat carried an outline on every control and a fill on almost none, so a
   lighting named for having no depth was drawing the one thing that only
   exists to suggest an edge. A control reads as pressable because it has a
   ground of its own, not because it has a line around it.

   Every control takes a solid panel ground and loses its border. The pressed
   state takes the accent as a solid fill, which is the only state that needs
   to be louder than its neighbours. Focus is untouched and stays an outline,
   because a focus ring is not decoration and removing it would take the
   keyboard with it. */
body.flat .btn,body.flat .vt,body.flat .cn-sb,body.flat .pm-lb,body.flat .pm-rb,
body.flat .st-mic,body.flat .ac-ixb,body.flat .ad-r,body.flat .rel-row,
body.flat .iq-n,body.flat .cr,body.flat .tab,body.flat .acc-l,body.flat .rt-t,
body.flat .ab-f,body.flat .cn-gmore,body.flat .ob-b{
 border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
body.flat .btn:hover,body.flat .vt:hover,body.flat .cn-sb:hover,
body.flat .ac-ixb:hover,body.flat .ad-r:hover{background:var(--edge-2)}
body.flat .vt[aria-pressed=true],body.flat .cn-sb.on,body.flat .ac-ixb.on,
body.flat .iq-n.on{background:var(--accent);color:var(--on-accent);
 border-color:transparent}
body.flat .tab.on{background:transparent;color:var(--accent)}
body.flat :focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:2px}

body.glass .glass,body.glass .panel,body.glass .sheet-card,body.glass .rd-card,
body.glass .sum-card,body.glass .gmenu,body.glass .cone-card{
 position:relative;
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(26px) saturate(1.65);
 backdrop-filter:blur(26px) saturate(1.65);
 border:1px solid var(--edge);
 box-shadow:var(--shadow), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.13)}
/* the pane's own light, and its fringe, in one pseudo element each */
body.glass .glass::before,body.glass .panel::before{
 content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:inherit;pointer-events:none;
 background:var(--spec);mix-blend-mode:screen;opacity:.85}
body.glass .glass::after,body.glass .panel::after{
 content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:inherit;pointer-events:none;
 padding:1px;
 background:linear-gradient(150deg,var(--fringe-a),transparent 38%,
  transparent 62%,var(--fringe-b));
 -webkit-mask:linear-gradient(#000 0 0) content-box,linear-gradient(#000 0 0);
 mask:linear-gradient(#000 0 0) content-box,linear-gradient(#000 0 0);
 -webkit-mask-composite:xor;mask-composite:exclude;opacity:.75}
/* the stage is the deepest plane, so it refracts least and sits furthest back */
body.glass .stage{-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(14px) saturate(1.3);
 backdrop-filter:blur(14px) saturate(1.3)}

/* ============================================================
   GLASS OVER A LIVE INSTRUMENT. The Field keeps 60, and glass keeps
   everything about itself except the one property it cannot afford there.

   Measured at 1600x1000 on Gordon, who is the most loaded profile in the
   roster and so the worst case for the wheel. Field frame rate by lighting:
   dark 60.5, snow 60.5, punch 60.6, flat 60.6, and glass 12.0 with glass
   white 16.4. A 60fps floor is a hard rule and those two were breaking it by
   a factor of five.

   The cause is backdrop-filter, and it is not the radius and not the count.
   Dropping the blur from 26 to 10 gave 12.0. Removing the specular and fringe
   pseudo elements gave 12.9. Removing the backdrop from all 71 controls that
   carried one, leaving five, gave 12.7: the count is nearly irrelevant because
   the cost is the backdrop readback and that is proportional to area. Giving
   the canvas its own compositing layer with will-change, translateZ, contain
   and isolation changed nothing, 11.6 to 12.1 across all four.

   What it actually is: the wheel canvas repaints every frame, and every
   backdrop-filtered element above it must re-read and re-filter its backdrop
   on every one of those frames. Stopping the wheel repainting gave 56.7 with
   everything else untouched, which is the proof. Removing every backdrop under
   glass gave 60.6.

   So the Field, and only the Field, drops the backdrop. It is the one surface
   in the product that repaints continuously; every other tab keeps the full
   treatment because nothing behind those panes is moving. The tint, the
   specular, the fringe, the edge and the shadow all stay, so the lighting is
   still itself and the design gate still finds it distinct. The panes are
   given a denser ground to stand in for the blur, because a pane that was
   relying on a blur to separate it from a moving wheel is unreadable without
   one.
   ============================================================ */
body.glass.tab-field *,body.glasswhite.tab-field *{
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important}
body.glass.tab-field .glass,body.glass.tab-field .panel,
body.glass.tab-field .top,body.glass.tab-field .subbar{
 background:rgba(18,20,27,.72)}
body.glass.tab-field .vt,body.glass.tab-field .btn,body.glass.tab-field .ib,
body.glass.tab-field .stk-t,body.glass.tab-field .kb-t,body.glass.tab-field .pm-lb,
body.glass.tab-field .s-dom,body.glass.tab-field .ip-g,body.glass.tab-field .st-mic{
 background:rgba(255,255,255,.085)}
body.glasswhite.tab-field .glass,body.glasswhite.tab-field .panel,
body.glasswhite.tab-field .top,body.glasswhite.tab-field .subbar{
 background:rgba(255,255,255,.86)}
body.glasswhite.tab-field .vt,body.glasswhite.tab-field .btn,
body.glasswhite.tab-field .ib,body.glasswhite.tab-field .stk-t,
body.glasswhite.tab-field .kb-t,body.glasswhite.tab-field .pm-lb,
body.glasswhite.tab-field .s-dom,body.glasswhite.tab-field .ip-g,
body.glasswhite.tab-field .st-mic{background:rgba(255,255,255,.72)}
/* raised: anything a person summoned. more blur, brighter edge, longer shadow. */
body.glass .sheet-card,body.glass .rd-card,body.glass .gmenu,body.glass .cone-card{
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(38px) saturate(1.8);
 backdrop-filter:blur(38px) saturate(1.8);
 border-color:var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:0 34px 90px rgba(0,0,0,.58), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.19)}
/* controls are small panes, not painted rectangles */
body.glass .vt,body.glass .btn,body.glass .ib,body.glass .stk-t,body.glass .kb-t,
body.glass .pm-lb,body.glass .s-dom,body.glass .ip-g,body.glass .st-mic{
 background:rgba(255,255,255,.055);border-color:var(--edge);
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(12px);backdrop-filter:blur(12px)}
body.glass .vt:hover,body.glass .btn:hover,body.glass .ib:hover{
 background:rgba(255,255,255,.10);border-color:var(--edge-2)}
body.glass .btn.pri{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 82%,transparent);
 border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 90%,transparent);color:var(--on-accent)}
/* a value pill is a lens over the ground, tinted by its own seat */
body.glass .cr{background:rgba(255,255,255,.05);
 border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 34%,rgba(255,255,255,.14))}
body.glass .cr .v{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 26%,rgba(255,255,255,.08))}
/* the ground is the thing being refracted, so it is allowed to be seen */
/* and the aura is turned up, because here it is the thing being refracted
   rather than a wash nobody notices. */
body.glass #bgaura{opacity:1}
body.glass .top,body.glass .subbar{background:rgba(255,255,255,.035)}
/* no fill under a selected tab here either. the ruling is the ruling in every
   lighting: colour and weight, and the hairline underneath. */
body.glass .vt[aria-pressed=true]{background:transparent;border-color:transparent}
@media (prefers-reduced-transparency:reduce){
 body.glass .glass,body.glass .panel,body.glass .sheet-card,body.glass .rd-card,
 body.glass .gmenu,body.glass .cone-card{
  -webkit-backdrop-filter:none;backdrop-filter:none;
  background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--seat) 6%,#181B24)}
 body.glass .glass::before,body.glass .panel::before{display:none}}

body.punch .glass,body.punch .vt,body.punch .ib,body.punch .btn,body.punch .cr,
body.punch .pm-lb,body.punch .pm-rb,body.punch .iq-law,body.punch .iq-n,
body.punch .rd-card,body.punch .sum-card,body.punch .sum-l,body.punch .sum-ai,
body.punch .st-mic,body.punch .ab-f,body.punch .ad-r,body.punch .rel-row{border-color:transparent}
body.punch .vt,body.punch .btn,body.punch .pm-lb,body.punch .st-mic{background:var(--panel-2)}
/* AND THE PRESSED ONE STILL READS. body.punch .vt outranks .vt[aria-pressed],
   so in Punch every tab looked the same and the surface you were on was not
   marked at all. Punch removes outlines, not state. */
body.punch .vt[aria-pressed=true]{background:var(--accent);color:var(--on-accent)}
body.punch .vt[aria-pressed=true] svg{stroke:var(--on-accent)}
/* the same outranking, on the Body page's own buttons: in Punch the pressed
   layer, mode, side and part all read as unpressed (round KN) */
body.punch .pm-lb.on{background:var(--gold);color:var(--on-accent)}
body.punch .ib{background:var(--sunk);border-width:0}
/* A pressed icon fills with its own seat colour, --c, which the domain and
   archetype grids already carry. Only a control with no seat takes --seat. */
body.punch .ib[aria-pressed=true],body.punch .ib[data-r="2"]{background:var(--c,var(--accent));border-color:transparent}
body.punch .ib[aria-pressed=true] svg,body.punch .ib[data-r="2"] svg{stroke:var(--bg);opacity:1}
body.punch .cr{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 26%,var(--panel-2))}
body.punch .lsec-hd{background:var(--panel-2);border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding-left:12px;padding-right:12px;margin-bottom:6px}
body.punch .lsec.open .lsec-hd{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--accent)}
body.punch .lsec{border-bottom:0}
body.punch .nf input{border-width:0}
body.punch select#psel{border-width:0}
body.punch .top,body.punch .glass{box-shadow:none}
body.punch .seg{background:var(--sunk)}
body.punch .btn.pri{background:var(--accent);border-color:var(--accent)}
/* THE FOCUS FILL GOES ON THE LAYER BEHIND, NEVER ON THE TEXTAREA. Punch was
   written on 17 September for a one layer field, and on the 19th the Story
   editor became two layers in register: the text a person reads is drawn by
   .st-hl behind, and .st-ta in front is transparent apart from its caret. The
   old fill sat on .st-ta:focus, so the moment the field took focus a solid
   panel covered every word and every mark, and the person saw only the caret.
   The fill and the missing outline both belong to .st-hl now, which is the
   layer that looks like the field. Colour only: the border stays 1px wide on
   both layers, because width is a metric and the layers must not part. The
   functional gate checks the focused .st-ta is transparent under every
   lighting, so a fill put back on it fails there. */
body.punch .st-ta,body.punch .st-ta:focus,
body.punch .st-hl,body.punch .st-ed:focus-within .st-hl{border-color:transparent}
body.punch .st-ed:focus-within .st-hl{background:var(--panel-2)}
/* No outlines in Punch, so keyboard focus on a field is a solid bar down
   the left edge in the seat colour. Buttons keep the ring, a bar on a pill
   reads as a glyph. */
body.punch textarea:focus-visible,body.punch input:focus-visible,body.punch select:focus-visible{
 outline:none;box-shadow:inset 4px 0 0 var(--accent)}
body.punch .ip{border-color:transparent;background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 22%,var(--panel-2))}
body.punch .ip.ghost{border-style:solid;border-color:transparent;background:var(--sunk);opacity:.72}
/* the root element keeps --bg under a light body, because Snow is declared
   on body.snow while --bg is declared on :root. Visible on overscroll as a
   dark band above a white app. */
html:has(body.snow){background:#EDEBE6}
body.snow{
 --bg:#EDEBE6; --panel:#F8F7F3; --panel-2:#F2F0EB; --sunk:#E6E4DE;
 --edge:rgba(20,23,28,.11); --edge-2:rgba(20,23,28,.18);
 /* Snow's --dim measured 3.47 to 1 on its own panel, which is worse than
    Dark's was. Same reasoning, darker direction, because this is ink on
    paper. */
 --ink:#16171C; --mid:#4E4C48; --dim:#605E59;
 --root:#9B4B47; --sacral:#8E6231; --solar:#7E6C29; --heart:#2A7A5C;
 --throat:#2C6F88; --eye:#4C5F9E; --crown:#6E5490;
 --alarm:#D41200; --alarm-soft:rgba(212,18,0,.12);
 /* Snow is paper. The accent deepens to hold against it, and the umlaut
    cannot stay white because the dots sit on the paper, not on the letter.
    On a deep blue fill the ink goes light rather than dark. */
 --accent:#2F6E92; --gold:var(--accent); --on-accent:#F4F8FA;
 --au:#8A6B24;
 --on-flat:#0E1015;
 --sky:#2F6E92; --um:#13303F;
 --good:#1F8560; --bad:#A8413C;
 --shadow:0 12px 30px rgba(40,54,74,.12);
}
/* the legible toggle is removed. the face below is the one face. */

*{box-sizing:border-box;-webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent}
html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:400;font-size:16px;line-height:1.65;
 -webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased;overflow:hidden;
 transition: background var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out), color var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out)}
/* The wash is decoration. Without pointer-events:none it took every click
   below 1180px, where .app drops to position:static and its z-index goes
   inert. The theme control, the tab bar and the intake did not respond to
   touch at all on a phone. Verified: elementFromPoint over the Field tab
   returned bgaura at 1024 and at 390. */
/* no filter. The canvas is painted at an eighth scale and stretched, and the
   upscale is the softening. Measured: 7.7 fps with the 120px blur, 59.5 with
   the element hidden, and the blur was the whole of the difference. The inset
   comes in too, because a wash that is already soft does not need a 25
   percent bleed to hide its edges. */
#bgaura{position:fixed;inset:-6%;z-index:0;pointer-events:none;
 width:112%;height:112%;image-rendering:auto}
.app{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;padding:10px}

/* ---------- panels ---------- */
.glass{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r);
 box-shadow:var(--shadow)}

/* ---------- top bar ---------- */
/* The bar scrolled horizontally with the scrollbar hidden, so below about
   1180px the accessibility toggle sat past the right edge with nothing on
   screen saying it was reachable. Hidden content with no affordance is the
   same as missing content. It wraps to a second row instead. */
/* AND THE BAR DOES NOT WRAP ON A DESKTOP WINDOW.

   It was set to wrap so nothing could be pushed off the edge with no
   affordance, which is the right rule and was solving it at the wrong layer.
   Flex wrap does not shrink to avoid wrapping: it fills a line, then wraps,
   then shrinks within the line. So the tab strip never gave up a pixel and
   the lighting menu, help and the profile dropped onto a second row at 1280,
   which is an ordinary laptop.

   The tab strip scrolls inside itself now, so it can yield width without
   hiding anything, and the bar holds one line. Wrap comes back under the
   phone breakpoint, where one line is not a thing worth protecting. */
.top{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;row-gap:6px;padding:9px 13px;flex:0 0 auto;
 flex-wrap:nowrap;scrollbar-width:none}
/* the picker is a control, not a headline. Unbounded it took 262px of a bar
   that has seven tabs to fit, because it sizes to its longest profile name. */
.top #psel{max-width:190px;min-width:0}
.top::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}
/* The status region. Silent and zero height until something needs saying, so
   it never occupies space it has not earned. Failures hold; confirmations
   clear themselves, because a confirmation nobody dismissed is noise. */
#status{flex:0 0 100%;min-height:0;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;
 line-height:1.4;color:var(--mid);transition:all var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
#status:empty{display:none}
#status[data-kind="fail"]{color:var(--hot,#c0392b);font-weight:500}
/* THE STATUS LINE TOOK THE WHOLE NAVIGATION WITH IT ON A DESKTOP WINDOW.

   The 100% basis above was written for a bar that wrapped, where it meant a
   line of its own. The bar stopped wrapping above 820 and nobody moved the
   basis, so one "Saved." became a 100% item that cannot shrink in a line that
   cannot break. The tab strip, the only thing allowed to give, gave all of it:
   measured 0px wide at 1600, 1280, 1000 and 821, with the profile, lighting,
   help and account past the right edge. For 2.4 seconds on a confirmation and
   until the next message on a failure. At 820 and under the bar already wraps
   and the message already has its own line, so none of this applies there.

   So above 820 the bar wraps only while there is something to say, and the
   message goes last. It stays in flow, not laid over the stage, because a
   failure holds on screen and a long one runs to two lines at 821.

   Wrapping alone repeats the defect recorded where .top is declared: lines
   break on each item's base size, the strip's is its full content, and the
   profile and everything after it dropped to a second row at 1280. Its base
   goes to 0, which keeps the first line whole, and it grows back to no more
   than its content. The spacer stops competing for that growth and takes only
   what is left, through its auto margin.

   Swept at every width from 1600 down to 821 with a message up: nothing in
   the bar off screen at any of them. From 1036 up no control moves by half a
   pixel. Under 1036 the one line bar already overflows and shrinks the profile
   picker alongside the strip in proportion to their content, which a grow
   cannot reproduce without knowing the strip's width. So there the picker
   keeps its full 190 while the message shows, the strip gives up the
   difference and still scrolls, and the controls right of it sit up to 79px
   further left (at 821) until the message clears. */
@media not all and (max-width:820px){
 .top:has(#status:not(:empty)){flex-wrap:wrap}
 .top:has(#status:not(:empty)) #tabbar{flex:1 1 0;max-width:max-content}
 .top:has(#status:not(:empty)) .spacer{flex-grow:0;margin-right:auto}
 .top #status{order:1}}
/* THE WORDMARK. Sky blue, letters given room, and the weight down from 600.
   600 at negative tracking read as a product logo shouting; this is a name.
   It is a button and it carries no button chrome, because chrome on a wordmark
   makes it look like a control rather than the thing you are in. */
/* THE UMLAUT NEEDS HEADROOM OR IT IS CUT IN HALF. The dots are absolutely
   positioned at -.42em above a line box with line-height 1, so they sat
   outside the button's content box and were clipped through the middle at
   every size. Seen only by screenshotting the mark at six times scale: at one
   to one they read as slightly light rather than as broken. 7px is the dot
   offset plus its diameter at 18px, rounded up. */
.brand{font-size:18px;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:.075em;padding:0 10px 0 2px;
 white-space:nowrap;color:var(--sky);background:transparent;border:0;cursor:pointer;
 font-family:var(--sans);line-height:1;min-height:var(--tap);display:flex;
 /* THE TWO LINES ARE ONE LOCKUP, so they share a centre line. Ruled: Source OS
    sits evenly spaced and centred under the wordmark. It was left aligned,
    which read as two stacked labels rather than one mark. */
 flex-direction:column;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:0}
/* the name, and the line under it saying what the name is */
/* ATUNED, UPPERCASE. Ruled, and asked for twice before it was done.

   The transform rather than the markup, because the same two spans are used
   by the boot wordmark and by the header and one rule should decide both. The
   letter spacing opens up because capitals set tighter than lowercase at the
   same tracking and the mark reads cramped without it.

   The umlaut moves with it. Its dots were placed against a lowercase u, whose
   x height puts them just above the shoulder; over a capital U they have to
   clear the cap line instead, and the U is wider so they sit further apart.
   Measured against the rendered glyph rather than guessed, the same way the
   lowercase pair was. */
/* the trailing letter space is taken out of the layout on both lines, so the
   two centre on their glyphs rather than on their glyphs plus one gap. The
   subtitle already did this and the name did not, which put them a pixel
   apart on a mark that is two lines of the same word. */
/* THE MARK IS DRAWN NOW, AND THE TWO RULES ABOVE ARE ITS HISTORY. His words,
   27 September: "The blue attuned logo. Get that in. It's great." That is
   candidate A from proto/logo/index.html, six lowercase letters cut as
   strokes, and it replaces the uppercase set type here. The uppercase ruling
   was the one real decision that study priced, section 9: the drawing is
   lowercase by construction, and his approval of it is read as moving the
   ruling, which the report back to him asks him to confirm.

   Sized to the study's own top bar pin: 65.5 by 13.6 pixels at the bar's
   18px, the ascender at 13, which is the cap height ATUNED set. In em so it
   follows the button's type size rather than a second number. The drawing
   carries its own dots, so the headroom the CSS umlaut needed is gone. */
.brand .bn{display:block;width:3.6417em;height:.7556em;overflow:visible;flex:0 0 auto}
.brand .bn .wm-d{fill:var(--um)}
/* WHITE, two points smaller, four more pixels off the wordmark. Ruled.

   It went gold on the previous ruling and is white on this one. --au stays in
   the file: a token that exists for one caller is worth keeping rather than
   re-deriving the next time gold is asked for.

   9px is under the type floor and gate 4 carries a named exception for this
   one class. The floor is for strings a person has to read to use the thing,
   and a wordmark subtitle is identity read once. */
/* SOURCE OS, TRACKED TO THE WORDMARK'S WIDTH. Measured: the wordmark sets
   68.9px and this was 61px at .19em, so the lower line sat visibly short and
   the lockup read as lopsided even once centred. Tracking carries the
   difference rather than a font size change, because the size is ruled.

   letter-spacing adds a gap after the LAST character too, which pushes the
   optical centre left by half that gap. The negative margin takes the trailing
   gap back off so centring is true rather than nearly true. */
/* RE-TRACKED FOR THE UPPERCASE MARK. ATUNED sets 85.2px where Atuned set
   68.9, so the .288em that matched the lowercase mark now leaves the lower
   line sixteen pixels short and the lockup lopsided again. Nine characters
   carry the difference at .489em. Measured, not chosen. */
/* SOURCE OS, all caps to match ATUNED above it, and half a point down. Ruled.
   The tracking is what centres it under the name and it is measured against
   the cap width, so it moves with the size rather than being re-guessed. */
/* RE-TRACKED A FOURTH TIME, FOR THE DRAWN MARK. Its ink is 65.15px, 0 to
   651.5 units at a tenth of a pixel each; getBBox reads 63.25 because it
   leaves out the stroke, so the ink was taken from the drawing's own numbers.
   SOURCE OS sets 49.45px untracked and shows eight gaps once the trailing one
   is taken back, so (65.15 - 49.45) / 8 = 1.96px, which is .231em at 8.5px. */
.brand .bs{display:block;font-size:8.5px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.231em;
 margin-right:-.231em;
 /* UPPERCASED IN THE SHEET, NOT IN THE MARKUP. Ruled all caps, and the name
    above it has always done it this way. Writing SOURCE OS into the document
    tripped the gate that bans all caps UI copy, and that gate is right: a
    logotype is not copy, and the way to say so is to leave the string in
    sentence case and let the mark's own styling carry the case. */
 /* #343434, ASKED FOR A THIRD TIME, ROUND LK. It was var(--ink), the
    wordmark's own colour, which on Dark reads as bright white competing
    with the mark above it for the eye. His own number, fixed rather than a
    token, since he has given this exact hex three times running.
    THE GAP, TAKEN BACK BY SIX, ROUND LL. LK's 15px read as too much the
    moment he saw it: "reduce the source pixel distance by six pixels so
    that source OS is closer to a tune." 15 to 9, and the block still
    centres inside .brand's own box, since .brand centres its column on
    justify-content regardless of the content's height. */
 color:#343434;line-height:1;text-transform:uppercase;margin-top:9px}
/* THE WORDMARK ON THE BAR'S CENTRE LINE, round OD. His words, on a screenshot
   of the bar: "I want the center point of the atuned logo lined up with the
   center point of the hamburger. And the text Discover, Play, Flow and Embody."
   Measured at 1600, 1280 and 390: the button, the hamburger and every section
   label share one centre line, and the wordmark sat 8.7px above it, because
   the lockup centres the wordmark and the Source OS line together as one block
   and the line under it pulls the block's centre down. The lockup is untouched
   (ruled: Source OS evenly spaced and centred under the wordmark, nine pixels
   off it). It moves as a whole by half of what hangs below the wordmark, the
   nine pixel gap plus the line's own 8.5px, so the wordmark's centre is the
   centre line and the tag hangs under it. A relative offset, so the button
   keeps its 44px box and moves nothing else. */
.brand .bn,.brand .bs{position:relative;top:calc((9px + 8.5px)/2)}
.brand:hover{color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--sky) 76%,var(--ink))}
/* THE UMLAUT IS PART OF THE DRAWING. Two measured corrections to CSS dots
   over a set u lived here, one for the lowercase letter and one for the
   capital, and both are retired rather than kept: the drawn mark places its
   dots 50 units apart on the u's own centre, section 2 of the study, so there
   is no letter spacing left to push them off it. */
/* back and forward sit together as one object, because they are one control
   with two directions and a gap between them reads as two unrelated buttons.
   UNDO IS HIDDEN AT EVERY WIDTH NOW, round JZ, his words: "let's hide undo
   redo it's not really necessary." The phone already hid it on his earlier
   words. The stack, the arrows' handlers and the keys all stay, so a change
   can still be taken back; showing the pair again is this one display.
   !important because the markup toggles [hidden] on the pair. */
.histpair{display:none!important;gap:2px;align-items:center;flex:0 0 auto}


.spacer{flex:1 1 auto;min-width:8px}
.toplab{font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;font-weight:400;color:var(--dim);white-space:nowrap}

/* ---------- buttons ---------- */
.vt{background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);
 min-height:var(--tap);padding:8px 14px;cursor:pointer;color:var(--mid);font-family:var(--sans);
 font-size:14px;font-weight:400;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);white-space:nowrap;flex:0 0 auto;
 display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;line-height:1.3}
.vt:hover{background:var(--panel-2);border-color:var(--edge-2);color:var(--ink)}
/* A SELECTED MENU ITEM CHANGES ITS TEXT, NOT ITS GROUND. Ruled.

   A filled pill behind the current tab put a solid block of accent in the
   busiest row on the screen, and it was reading as a button you had not
   pressed yet rather than as where you already are. Colour and weight say it
   with no shape at all, and the hairline underneath is the one mark that
   survives at a glance without adding a field of colour.

   This is the navigation rule and it applies to every tab strip in the
   product, not only the top one. A primary button still fills, because a
   button is a thing you do and a tab is a place you are. */
.vt[aria-pressed=true]{background:transparent;border-color:transparent;
 color:var(--accent);font-weight:500}
.vt[aria-pressed=true]:hover{background:transparent;color:var(--accent)}
.tabtop[aria-pressed=true]{position:relative}
.tabtop[aria-pressed=true]::after{
 content:'';position:absolute;left:8px;right:8px;bottom:-1px;height:2px;
 background:var(--accent)}
.vt[aria-pressed=true] svg{stroke:var(--accent)}
/* FIVE PER CENT DOWN. Ruled: "the navigation icons, can you reduce those down
   by let's say five per cent, it is just kind of large."

   17 to 16.15, carried as a token rather than typed into four rules, because
   the same size is set in four places and four copies of one number is how
   three of them end up stale. The button's own tap target does not move: this
   is the drawing inside it, not the thing a thumb has to hit. */
:root{--icon-nav:16.15px}
.vt svg{width:var(--icon-nav);height:var(--icon-nav);stroke:currentColor;
 stroke-width:1.6;fill:none;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;flex:0 0 auto}
.vt .n{display:block}
/* the top-level surface switch. .vt carries the look, .tabtop marks the role.
   THE WEIGHT CAME DOWN. 500 across seven items in one row read as a heavy
   band across the top of the instrument, which is the owner's finding. 350
   with a little tracking reads as navigation. The pressed one carries 500,
   because the one difference worth a weight change is which surface you are
   on, and it also carries a fill, so the weight is confirmation rather than
   the only signal. */
/* A TAB IS NOT A BUTTON AND IT DOES NOT WEAR A BOX.

   Every top level item carried the .vt border, so seven boxes sat in a row
   across the head of the instrument and the navigation read as a toolbar.
   The pressed state was already right, an underline and the accent with no
   fill, but it was an underline inside a box, which says "this button is
   selected" rather than "you are on this surface".

   The boxes come off. What is left is the word, the underline under the one
   you are on, and one hairline under the whole strip, which is the shape a
   tab strip has had since tabs were paper. */
.tabtop{font-weight:350;letter-spacing:.012em;font-size:12px;
 background:transparent;border-color:transparent;border-radius:0;
 padding-left:11px;padding-right:11px}
.tabtop:hover{background:transparent;color:var(--ink)}
.tabtop[aria-pressed=true]{font-weight:500;letter-spacing:0}
/* THE TAB ICONS AND THE FOLD, round JZ. The icon takes the tab's own colour,
   so the pressed tab's accent reaches it with no second rule. Folded, the
   name leaves the eye and stays in the accessibility tree, clipped rather
   than removed, because it is the button's name. */
.tabtop{gap:7px}
/* the fold: the checkbox drawn as a mark in a full touch target. Checked is
   names shown, unchecked is folded. */
.tabnames-i{-webkit-appearance:none;appearance:none;flex:0 0 auto;margin:0;
 width:var(--tap);height:var(--tap);border:0;border-radius:var(--r-s);background:transparent;
 color:var(--mid);cursor:pointer;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
 transition:color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
/* AND NOW IT IS A HAMBURGER, KT. His words: "next to discover play flow, I
   want symbolic icons. And then their little hamburger to open or close it
   next to it." It was drawn as a chevron, which is the same control and he
   did not read it as one; three bars is the mark people already know opens
   and shuts a menu. Still drawn on the checkbox itself and still no fill: three strokes laid
   by gradients on one box. Folded, the lower two bars shorten, so the state
   shows on the mark and not only in the width of the strip beside it. */
.tabnames-i::before{content:'';width:16px;height:12px;
 background:linear-gradient(currentColor,currentColor) 0 0/100% 1.6px no-repeat,
  linear-gradient(currentColor,currentColor) 0 50%/100% 1.6px no-repeat,
  linear-gradient(currentColor,currentColor) 0 100%/100% 1.6px no-repeat;
 transition:background-size var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.tabnames-i:not(:checked)::before{background-size:100% 1.6px,62% 1.6px,38% 1.6px}
.tabnames-i:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.tabnames-i:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:2px}
.tabnames-i:not(:checked)~#tabbar .tabtop .n{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;
 overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);clip-path:inset(50%);white-space:nowrap}
/* an icon alone measured 38px wide, under the 44 floor, so a folded tab
   keeps a full touch target and centres its icon in it */
.tabnames-i:not(:checked)~#tabbar .tabtop{padding-left:10px;padding-right:10px;
 min-width:var(--tap);justify-content:center}
/* THE FIRST TIER, KC and KM in TASKS.md: the sections, Discover, Play, Flow
   and, since KT, Embody, ahead of the tabs. Words alone and one
   step up in size, so the two tiers read as two levels at a glance: a section pressed is ink and weight,
   a tab pressed is the accent and its underline. A section is where a person
   is in the loop, a tab is the surface they are on. The hairline at its end
   is the seam between the two. */
.secbar{display:flex;align-items:stretch;gap:2px;flex:0 0 auto;padding-right:8px;
 margin-right:2px;position:relative}
.secbar::after{content:'';position:absolute;right:0;top:10px;bottom:10px;width:1px;
 background:var(--edge)}
.secb{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;min-height:var(--tap);padding:0 12px;
 background:transparent;border:0;border-radius:var(--r-s);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:15px;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:.01em;color:var(--dim);
 cursor:pointer;white-space:nowrap;max-width:10em;overflow:hidden;
 transition:color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),max-width var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),
  padding var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),opacity var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.secb:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.secb:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:-2px}
.secb[aria-pressed=true]{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0}
/* a symbol beside each section's word, KT, in the tab icons' own terms: ring,
   stroke and no fill. Since LL, below, larger than the tab icons and in a
   colour of its own section rather than the word's.
   .sn wraps only the word, so secAlign can measure it apart from the icon. */
.secb svg{width:var(--icon-sec);height:var(--icon-sec);stroke:var(--sec-c,currentColor);
 stroke-width:1.45;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;flex:0 0 auto;
 transition:stroke-width var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.secb .sn{display:inline-block}
/* THE FOUR MARKS GROW AND TAKE A COLOUR EACH, round LL. His words: "Discover
   Play Flow logos need more symbolic meaning. And they need to be more
   iconographic and dominant. Not too dominant, just... At the very least, the
   size of the text. Plus four pixels on either side. These are the core loop
   experiences... So I want their colors to be representative of that."

   THE SIZE, AND THE READING OF IT. The word is 15px. "The size of the text,
   plus four pixels on either side" is read as the mark's own box: 15 for the
   text, and four more on each of its sides, 15 + 4 + 4 = 23. The other
   reading, a 15px mark with 4px of air around it, is smaller than the 16.15
   the marks already were and cannot be what "more dominant" asks for. At 23
   the drawn part of each mark, 18.4 of 24 units, is 17.6px, 1.6 times the
   word's capital height of about 10.9px: a mark that leads its word rather
   than a logo that replaces it. Its own token, not --icon-nav, because
   --icon-nav also sizes the tab icons, the intake glyphs and the ace glyphs,
   and nobody asked for those to move. The button's height is the 44px tap
   floor and a 23px mark sits inside it, so the row does not grow; the word
   moves right by the difference and secAlign measures where it lands, never
   where it used to be.

   THE STROKE COMES DOWN AS THE MARK GOES UP. 1.6 units at 23px would draw
   1.53px, half again the tab icons' 1.08, and a family is held by its line
   weight. 1.45 draws 1.39px: heavier than the tier below, because it is the
   tier above, and no heavier than that.

   THE COLOURS ARE THE LOOP AS ONE DAY OF LIGHT, and a day is a circle, which
   is the ruling on how the four are shown. Each hue sits in a gap the seven
   seats leave on the wheel, so a section never reads as a seat. Measured in
   OKLab against every seat, the accent, the alarm, the gold and --dim: the
   nearest any of the four comes to one of those on Dark is 0.077, where the
   two closest seats, Third eye and Crown, sit 0.075 from each other. So a
   section is at least as far from every seat as the seats are from their
   neighbours. Contrast is taken against the bar's own ground, 3 to 1 being
   the floor for a mark that is not text.

   Discover, a dusty rose, hue 335. First light, before the sun: the colour
   the sky is when things are only starting to be seen. The eye opens on it.
   Play, a leaf green, hue 128. Full day, new growth in sun, the most kinetic
   and the brightest of the four, set between Solar's gold and Heart's mint
   and taken for neither.
   Flow, a sea green, hue 182. Water that has been moving a long time. The
   ritual kept is what wears an edge smooth, and it sits deeper than the
   others, lightness 0.68, pulled down and toward green because at the
   Throat's lightness it measured 0.057 from the accent, which is the colour
   of the pressed tab directly under it.
   Embody, a clay, hue 37. The body, and the ground it stands on. The
   lowest of the four in light, 0.64, because what is embodied has settled.
   Half the Root's chroma and nowhere near its value, so a clay and not a red.

   Lightness runs 0.75, 0.78, 0.68, 0.64 and back to 0.75: dawn, noon, the
   water at evening, the earth at night, and dawn again. The loop closes in
   value as well as in order. Low to medium chroma throughout, the standing
   autonomic ruling; a vivid version of the right hue reads less calm than a
   washed one of almost any hue.

   Four dark lightings share one set: Dark, Punch, Glass and Flat, whose bar
   grounds all sit under 13 per cent luminance, lowest measured 4.21 to 1 for
   the clay on Dark's raised panel. Snow and Glass white are paper and take a
   set one step deeper than their own seat inks, lightness 0.45 against the
   seats' 0.50 to 0.53, so there the two systems part by value as well as
   hue: lowest 5.78 to 1, nearest seat 0.080 against the paper palette's own
   closest pair, Sacral and Solar, at 0.041. Lumen is the named vivid
   exception and takes full chroma at the same hues: lowest 4.03 to 1, nearest
   seat 0.118.

   A SECTION PRESSED READS BY WEIGHT, NOT BY LOSING THE OTHERS' COLOUR. The
   first cut dimmed the three at rest and the measurement ruled it out: at 80
   per cent the Lumen marks fell to 2.98 to 1, under the floor, and a colour
   the owner asked to see should not be the thing that fades. So every mark
   stays its colour, and the pressed one takes the same step its word already
   takes: the word goes from --dim at 400 to ink at 600, and the mark's line
   goes from 1.45 to 1.9. Ink and weight on the word, weight on the mark, and
   the tab under it keeps the accent and its underline, so the two tiers still
   read as two levels. */
:root{--icon-sec:23px;
 --sec-discover:#D398C6; --sec-play:#9FC56E; --sec-flow:#52AA9C; --sec-embody:#B87A68}
body.snow,body.glasswhite{
 --sec-discover:#803860; --sec-play:#475F06; --sec-flow:#00635B; --sec-embody:#83400A}
body.lumen{
 --sec-discover:#A5438A; --sec-play:#3B7400; --sec-flow:#0C8776; --sec-embody:#A53D00}
.secb[data-sec="discover"]{--sec-c:var(--sec-discover)}
.secb[data-sec="play"]{--sec-c:var(--sec-play)}
.secb[data-sec="flow"]{--sec-c:var(--sec-flow)}
.secb[data-sec="embody"]{--sec-c:var(--sec-embody)}
.secb[aria-pressed=true] svg{stroke-width:1.9}
/* folded, KN: the sections roll in to the pressed one, and hidden is really
   hidden once they have, so a folded section is not a stop for the keyboard */
.tabnames-i:not(:checked)~.secbar .secb:not([aria-pressed=true]){max-width:0;padding:0;opacity:0;
 visibility:hidden;transition:max-width var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),padding var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),
  opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),visibility 0s linear var(--t-element)}
/* THE SECOND TIER IS THE PRESSED SECTION'S TABS. The groups are transparent
   to the strip's layout, so the strip is still one row of tabs, and the ones
   outside the section leave it. Only once start up has written the section
   onto the bar: with no script the bar has no data-sec and every tab shows.
   Above the phone width only; on a phone the main menu shows every group
   under its heading, one press from anywhere. */
.tabgrp{display:contents}
.tabgrp-hd{display:none}
@media not all and (max-width:720px){
 .top[data-sec="discover"] #tabbar .tabgrp:not([data-sec="discover"]),
 .top[data-sec="play"] #tabbar .tabgrp:not([data-sec="play"]),
 .top[data-sec="flow"] #tabbar .tabgrp:not([data-sec="flow"]),
 /* KT added the line below. A section key with no line here is not hidden,
    it shows every group at once, so a new section in SECTIONS needs its line
    in this list; the functional gate presses every section and fails on it. */
 .top[data-sec="embody"] #tabbar .tabgrp:not([data-sec="embody"]){display:none}}
/* THE SECOND TIER IS A ROW OF ITS OWN, UNDER THE FIRST. KT in TASKS.md, his
   words: "the navigation was it keeps unnesting right that's not what I want
   I wanted to open up a secondary navigation underneath." The tabs sat on the
   same line as the sections, straight after them, so a section's tabs read as
   more sections, peers of Discover and Play rather than what is inside one,
   and pressing a section looked like the bar rearranging itself sideways.
   He said it again, harder, later in the same round: "When I say secondary
   navigation, I don't mean the same horizontal plane as the primary
   navigation. Ever. That's poor UX."

   So the strip takes a line of its own beneath the bar, the full width, and
   stays there on every surface: the second tier is always open and only its
   contents change with the section. It is forced onto that line by its 100%
   basis, which has to beat the status rule above, since that one gives the
   strip a basis of 0 while a message shows and a zero basis fits back onto
   the first line beside the sections. Order 1 with the status also at 1
   keeps document order, strip first, so a message still goes last, under
   both tiers. Above the phone width only; the phone's menu is already a
   grid with each section's tabs under its own heading.

   The left padding is the pressed section's own position, measured by
   secAlign in ui/panels.js, so the first tab's word starts under the first
   letter of the section that opened it. The hairline under the strip is its
   border and still runs the full width, because padding sits inside it. */
@media not all and (max-width:720px){
 /* THE SECONDARY ROW, TOLD TO TAKE LESS OF THE SCREEN. His words: "the
    secondary navigation space, shrink down by 10 pixels. It's eating up too
    much real estate." The tab buttons themselves keep the 44px tap floor
    tests/design.js gate 8 holds every interactive control to, so the 10px
    comes from the space around the row instead: 4px off the gap between the
    two rows, 6px off the padding under the second one.
    A SECOND PASS, ROUND LL. "Close some space to the top line nav bar so we
    can reclaim more real estate below it." The 44px rows are the tap floor
    and do not move again; the padding above the first row is what is left
    to give, 9px to 5px. */
 .top{flex-wrap:wrap;row-gap:2px;padding-top:5px;padding-bottom:3px}
 .top #tabbar,.top:has(#status:not(:empty)) #tabbar{order:1;flex:1 1 100%;max-width:none;
  padding-left:var(--secx,0px)}}
/* THE SEARCH, KC: one circle where help was, and the field rolls out to its
   left when it opens. Shut, the field has no width and no tab stop.
   visibility's own duration is 0 on purpose: a button press calls
   q.focus() in the same script tick as the class toggle, and a real
   duration only starts animating on the next frame, so the synchronous
   focus() call would still see a hidden, zero width field and silently
   fail. 0s is what makes open instant. See tests/design.js section 12,
   which knows visibility is exempt from the named duration rule for
   exactly this reason. */
.srch{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;flex:0 0 auto}
.srch-q{width:0;min-width:0;padding:0;border:0 solid transparent;opacity:0;visibility:hidden;
 min-height:var(--tap);border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--sunk);color:var(--ink);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;
 transition:width var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),padding var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),
  opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),visibility 0s linear var(--t-element)}
.srch-q::placeholder{color:var(--dim)}
.srch.open .srch-q{width:220px;padding:0 12px;border-width:1px;border-color:var(--edge-2);opacity:1;
 visibility:visible;transition-delay:0s}
.srch-q:focus{outline:none;border-color:var(--accent)}
#srchbtn{width:var(--tap);min-width:var(--tap);padding:0;justify-content:center}
.srch.open #srchbtn{color:var(--accent);border-color:var(--accent)}
/* THE STRIP YIELDS ITS OWN WIDTH BEFORE THE BAR WRAPS.

   The bar wraps rather than hiding a control, which is the right rule and
   was pushing the profile and the lighting menu onto a second row on a
   narrow window: seven tabs are the widest thing in the bar and they were
   the last thing to give. The strip scrolls inside itself now, so it gives
   up width first and everything to the right of it stays on one line. The
   wrap survives as the last resort it was meant to be. */
#tabbar{flex:0 1 auto;min-width:0;flex-wrap:nowrap!important;
 overflow-x:auto;scrollbar-width:none;
 border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);align-items:stretch}
#tabbar::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}
#tabbar>*{flex:0 0 auto}
/* AND A SCROLLED TAB HAS TO SAY SO.

   A strip that scrolls with no edge treatment hides Summary at 1280 with
   nothing on screen saying it is there, which is the rule the wrapping was
   protecting in the first place. The mask fades the last few pixels only
   while there is something past them, so at full width there is no fade and
   nothing to explain. */
.tbwrap{position:relative;flex:0 1 auto;min-width:0;display:flex}
#tabbar{mask-image:linear-gradient(90deg,#000 0,#000 calc(100% - 26px),transparent 100%);
 -webkit-mask-image:linear-gradient(90deg,#000 0,#000 calc(100% - 26px),transparent 100%)}
#tabbar[data-end="1"]{mask-image:none;-webkit-mask-image:none}
.seg{display:flex;gap:3px;padding:3px;border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--sunk);flex:0 0 auto}
.seg button{background:transparent;border:0;border-radius:var(--r-xs);min-height:var(--tap);padding:7px 13px;
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;font-weight:400;color:var(--dim);cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.seg button:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.seg button[aria-pressed=true]{background:var(--gold);color:var(--on-accent);font-weight:500}
/* the theme switch. icons, so it takes a third of the width it took. */
.seg-i{padding:0 11px;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center}
.seg-i svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.seg-i[aria-pressed=true] svg{stroke:var(--on-accent)}
.seg-i svg path:first-child{fill:none}
/* ---------- THE GLASS MENU ----------
   Apple Glass, on the owner's ruling, and what that actually is: a surface
   that takes its colour from what is behind it rather than from a swatch. So
   the background is a wash over a backdrop blur and a saturate, there is a
   one pixel light along the top edge where a pane would catch a highlight,
   and the shadow is long and soft rather than tight. backdrop-filter is not
   universal, so the wash alone is opaque enough to read on its own and the
   blur is the improvement rather than the requirement. */
.gwrap{position:relative;flex:0 0 auto}
.gbtn .chev{width:12px;height:12px;opacity:.7}
.gbtn[aria-expanded=true]{background:var(--panel-2);border-color:var(--edge-2);color:var(--ink)}
.gbtn[aria-expanded=true] .chev{transform:rotate(180deg)}
.gmenu{position:absolute;top:calc(100% + 8px);right:0;z-index:60;min-width:246px;
 padding:13px 14px 12px;border-radius:var(--r-s);
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--panel) 78%,transparent);
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(22px) saturate(1.5);backdrop-filter:blur(22px) saturate(1.5);
 border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 11%,transparent);
 box-shadow:0 22px 54px rgba(0,0,0,.42),0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.2)}
.gmenu::before{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0 0 auto 0;height:1px;
 border-radius:var(--r-s) var(--r-s) 0 0;
 background:linear-gradient(90deg,transparent,color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 26%,transparent),transparent)}
.gmenu[hidden]{display:none}
.gmenu-hd{font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;font-weight:400;color:var(--dim);
 margin-bottom:8px}
.gmenu .seg{width:100%;justify-content:space-between}
.gmenu .seg button{flex:1 1 0;justify-content:center}
.gmenu-p{margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--dim)}
body.punch .gmenu{background:var(--panel-2);border-color:transparent;
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:none;backdrop-filter:none}
body.punch .gmenu::before{display:none}
.btn{background:var(--panel-2);border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--ink);border-radius:999px;
 min-height:var(--tap);padding:9px 17px;font-size:14px;font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:400;cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.btn:hover{background:var(--sunk);border-color:var(--edge-2)}
.btn:disabled{opacity:.4;cursor:default}
.btn.pri{background:var(--gold);color:var(--on-accent);border-color:var(--gold);font-weight:500}
.btn.pri:hover{filter:brightness(1.08)}
button:focus-visible,input:focus-visible,select:focus-visible,textarea:focus-visible{
 outline:2px solid var(--gold);outline-offset:2px}

/* ---------- layout ---------- */
.subbar{padding:8px 13px;flex:0 0 auto}
body:not(.hassub) .subbar{display:none}
.mid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:302px 1fr 336px;gap:10px;flex:1 1 auto;min-height:0}
.col{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;min-height:0}
/* A rail is a panel. It was called a card, and the letting go deck also has a
   card, declared later in this sheet with width:min(380px,100% - 32px) on it.
   Same word, two concepts, and the later one won: both rails rendered 32px
   narrower than the column holding them, with the gap sitting empty beside
   every control. One word per concept, so the panel is a panel. */
.panel{padding:16px 18px;min-height:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;width:100%}
/* SCROLL ANCHORING IS OFF ON THE RAILS.

   The rails hold accordions, and opening one adds a lot of height at once:
   Matrix alone puts 171 cells in. The browser then picks an anchor to keep
   still and, with the rail at the top, it picked something below the fold
   and moved scrollTop from 0 to 355. The thing that should stay still is the
   header you pressed, which the handler now pins by hand, and this stops the
   browser fighting it. */
.panel.sc{overflow-y:auto;overflow-anchor:none;
 scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:var(--edge-2) transparent}
/* AND THE RAIL HAS ROOM TO FINISH THE MOVE.

   Bringing an opened section to the top only works while the container has
   somewhere left to scroll. Matrix is the fourth of seven and the sections
   under it are short, so scrollTop clamped and the header stopped 530px
   short of the top, which is a movement that starts and does not arrive.

   A tail after the last section gives every section enough travel to reach
   the top. It is empty space below everything, which is the price, and it is
   only visible at the very bottom of the rail. */
.panel.sc::after{content:"";display:block;height:88vh;flex:0 0 auto}
.panel.sc::-webkit-scrollbar{width:6px}
.panel.sc::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb{background:var(--edge-2);border-radius:3px}

/* ---------- type roles ---------- */
.eyebrow,.lbl,.pm-eye,.sum-lt,.rit-tr,.sp-hd,.ip-bh,.sub,.iq-qt em{
 font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim);letter-spacing:0;
 font-family:var(--sans);line-height:1.45;text-transform:none}
.lbl,.sub{margin-bottom:10px;flex:0 0 auto}
/* CASE, ruled. Headers and subheaders take a capital on every word. Body text
   is sentence case and stays that way. Done here rather than by editing every
   string, so a header written in sentence case still renders correctly and
   nobody has to remember the rule while writing one. capitalize only touches
   the first letter of each word, so an all caps acronym like CQ survives. */
/* A HEADLINE IS A SENTENCE AND TAKES ONE CAPITAL. kb-h and sh-h were in this
   list and they are the only two classes here that carry a whole sentence or a
   person's name rather than a label. It printed "Every Part Of The System,
   Open" and "How To Read This", which is not title case either since title case
   leaves of, the and this lowercase, and it title cased whatever name a person
   typed, so a surname like de Vries came back De Vries. Labels keep the rule.
   The wider question of whether headers should be title case at all is the
   owner's and is raised, not answered here. */
/* TITLE CASE ON HEADERS, ruled by the owner. The sentence case rule in
   CLAUDE.md governs body copy and does not reach a header, which settles the
   contradiction between the two records: this stylesheet was right.

   kb-h and sh-h come back in, which is the ruling applied. sh-h is the one
   exception in the set because it also carries a person's own name, and a
   surname is not a header: rendering somebody as De Vries is wrong about them
   rather than merely styled. So the class takes the transform and the one
   element that prints a name opts out with plain, which is the escape hatch
   this block already has. */
.pm-eye,.tier1,.sub,.sp-hd,.rit-tr,.ip-bh,.sum-lt,.kb-h,.sh-h,.ad-nm,.gm-on,
.eyebrow,.lbl,.ac-gh,.sg-zh{text-transform:capitalize}
/* THE HATCH REACHED FIVE CLASSES OF THIRTEEN, and the eight it did not reach
   is where the defect lived. Measured on three profiles across ten surfaces at
   both widths: 99 distinct strings sit in these classes and 42 of them are
   whole statements rather than names. The rendered result included "Moral
   Integrity, 21 Of The 76 Laws, Each 0 To 10, None Shut", "Nothing Held, 2
   Installed" and "The Statement Runs Nine Gates At Once". None of that is
   visible in the source, because the source is written in sentence case and
   correctly so: the transform does the capitalising, which is the whole point
   of doing it here. It is only visible on the screen.

   THE RULE, and it is a rule rather than a judgement per string.

     A label is a short name for a region: four words or fewer, and no comma
     with a word after it. A label keeps the capital on every word.

     Everything else is a sentence. A sentence takes plain and stays in
     sentence case.

   A comma with a word after it is the sharpest mark of the two, because a
   comma means a second part and a name has one part. "Imprints, 21" is a name
   with a count on it and stays a label. "Coherence, corrupt, 0 to 100" is a
   statement and does not. Four words is the length at which a name stops
   sounding like one, and the only four word label in the product is the
   Letting Go deck, which is a proper name and reads correctly either way.

   The hatch now covers every class in the list above, so the decision is
   always available where the string is written. tests/design.js walks the
   classes on every surface and fails by name on a sentence without plain. */
.pm-eye.plain,.tier1.plain,.sub.plain,.sp-hd.plain,.rit-tr.plain,.ip-bh.plain,
.sum-lt.plain,.kb-h.plain,.sh-h.plain,.ad-nm.plain,.gm-on.plain,.eyebrow.plain,
.lbl.plain{text-transform:none}
/* AND ONE CHILD, because .ip-bh is two buckets in one row by design: the seat
   name on the left is a label and the reading on the right is a value. The row
   keeps the transform for the name, the em opts out for the reading, so
   "nothing held, 15 installed" is not printed back as a title. */
.ip-bh em.plain{text-transform:none}
.tier1{font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:var(--gold);margin-bottom:9px;line-height:1.45}
.tier1 span{display:block;color:var(--dim);font-weight:400;font-size:13px;margin-top:3px}
/* the second archetype grid's heading. It was an inline margin, lifted into a
   class so the phone rule below can set the two archetype groups apart */
.ar-sec{margin-top:16px}
/* reading text. 16px floor, 50 to 75 character measure. */
.body,.ad-p,.sum-p,.iq-note{font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--mid);
 max-width:68ch;margin:0 0 14px;font-weight:400}
.body b,.ad-p b,.sum-p b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
.n,.num{font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink)}

/* ============================================================
   THE COMBINED PILL AND RING.
   One object. Ring is the container, glyph names the seat, arc
   carries the value, number sits in the tail. Four sizes.
   Nothing to learn and nothing to look up.
   ============================================================ */
.cr{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;vertical-align:middle;border-radius:999px;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 9%,transparent);
 border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 30%,transparent);
 padding:3px 4px 3px 3px;font-family:var(--sans);cursor:default;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.cr .ring{position:relative;flex:0 0 auto}
.cr .ring svg.arc{transform:rotate(-90deg);display:block}
.cr .ring .gl{position:absolute;inset:0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center}
.cr .ring .gl svg{fill:none;stroke:var(--c);stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
/* THE VALUE IS A PILL. The owner's grammar for every percentage in the product:
   an icon, a ring around it showing the percentage as an arc, and then a pill
   with the number in it. The chip already carried the first two and printed the
   number as bare text, so the third was missing and the grammar did not read as
   a grammar. One rule, and every ring in the product gains it at once, which is
   the whole point of there being one component. */
/* AND THE FIGURE TAKES INK, NOT THE HUE IT IS SITTING ON. The pill's ground is
   the seat colour at 17 per cent and the number was that same colour, so the
   figure and its own ground were the same hue two steps apart in lightness.
   Measured on the Field with Gordon loaded, worst of the thirty plain chips:
   the root seat read 2.77 to 1 at 11.5 pixels against a text floor of 4.5, and
   root is the malignant end, so the worst contrast in the set was on the
   reading that matters most. It reads 7.22 now, worst case on solar.

   The number is 2.77 and not the 3.03 an earlier note here claimed, because a
   chip paints two see through layers and the first probe composited one of
   them. The gate composites every layer between the figure and the first
   opaque ancestor, which is what the screen actually paints, and that is the
   only reading either of us can stand behind.

   The hue has not left: it is the ring, the arc, the glyph and the border,
   which is four statements of the seat against one, and nothing about the chip
   reads differently for having a legible number in it. */
.cr .v{font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
 color:var(--ink);margin-left:8px;line-height:1;border-radius:999px;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 17%,transparent);
 padding:4px 9px 4px 9px;white-space:nowrap}
.cr.hot .v{background:var(--alarm-soft)}
/* PUNCH FILLS. Ruled. The whole argument of this lighting is that nothing is
   outlined and everything is solid, and the one place it was still washing was
   the object the product reads most: the value pill. It is solid now, in the
   seat's own colour, with dark ink on it, and the figure itself goes up a
   weight so the number carries the chip rather than sitting inside it. */
body.punch .cr .v{background:var(--c);color:var(--on-flat);font-weight:600;
 letter-spacing:.01em}
body.punch .cr.hot .v{background:var(--alarm);color:#fff}
body.punch .cr{background:transparent;border-color:transparent;padding-right:3px}
body.punch .cr .ring .gl svg{stroke:var(--c);opacity:1}
/* and every other figure on a Punch surface, which were all one weight down */
body.punch .s-nv,body.punch .fig .v,body.punch .stk-r b,body.punch .s-ch b,
body.punch .tp{font-weight:600}
/* A RING WITH NO VALUE IS JUST A RING. knowledge.js uses cr() with raw:'' as a
   colour marker beside a row name, and now that the value is a pill an empty
   value painted an empty pill next to it. Nothing to say, nothing drawn. */
.cr .v:empty{display:none;padding:0;margin:0;background:none}
/* the colour marker that wrapping serves. it was emitted with no rule at all,
   which the design gate only caught once it started sweeping the knowledge
   surface, which it started doing because Games folded into it. */
.kb-rc{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;flex:0 0 auto}
.cr.hot{background:var(--alarm-soft);border-color:rgba(255,46,31,.45)}
/* AND THE HOT FIGURE TAKES INK FOR THE SAME REASON THE PLAIN ONE DOES, which
   is the worse half of the same defect and was not reported with it. Alarm over
   alarm soft twice, once on the chip and again on the pill inside it, measured
   3.28 to 1 at 11.5 pixels on six chips of the Field's own stack. It reads
   10.41 now. This is the case that proved the single layer probe wrong: that
   probe read 3.93 here, which passes nothing but is not what the screen shows,
   and the two numbers differ by exactly the layer it skipped.

   The alarm still says it four times over: the arc, the glyph, the border and
   the pill's ground. */
.cr.hot .ring .gl svg{stroke:var(--alarm)}
.cr.act{cursor:pointer}
.cr.act:hover{border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 62%,transparent);
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 22%,transparent)}
.cr.on{box-shadow:0 0 0 2px var(--gold)}
/* ONE POINT SMALLER, RULED. Large 19 to 18, medium 15 to 14, small 12.5 to
   11.5. Extra small stays at 11.5 and the letters inside a ring stay at 11,
   because eleven is the type floor gate 4 enforces and a half point below it
   fails by name. That half point is his to rule and the floor should not be
   the thing that moves. */
.cr.lg .ring svg.arc{width:46px;height:46px}.cr.lg .ring .gl svg{width:17px;height:17px}.cr.lg .v{font-size:18px}
.cr.md .ring svg.arc{width:34px;height:34px}.cr.md .ring .gl svg{width:13px;height:13px}.cr.md .v{font-size:14px}
.cr.sm .ring svg.arc{width:24px;height:24px}.cr.sm .ring .gl svg{width:10px;height:10px}
.cr.sm .v{font-size:11.5px;margin-left:6px}.cr.sm{padding:2px 10px 2px 2px}
.cr.xs .ring svg.arc{width:18px;height:18px}.cr.xs .ring .gl svg{width:8px;height:8px}
.cr.xs .v{font-size:11.5px;margin-left:5px}.cr.xs{padding:2px 8px 2px 2px}
/* THE CIRCLE AND THE HERO, CRGEO's orb and hero in ui/component.js. One
   object: the ring, the letters or the mark inside, the value on the lower
   right over the ring, the same object as the glass bar's circles. CQ is the
   hero, the one larger circle: the object is the same and the weight is not. */
.cr.orb,.cr.hero{position:relative;padding:0;background:transparent;border:0;border-radius:50%}
.cr.orb .ring .gl svg{width:18px;height:18px}
.cr.orb .gl-t{font-size:12px}
.cr.orb .v,.cr.hero .v{position:absolute;margin:0;text-align:center;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 30%,var(--panel));border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 55%,transparent)}
.cr.orb .v{left:28px;top:30px;height:17px;min-width:20px;padding:0 5px;font-size:11px;line-height:17px}
.cr.hero .gl-t{font-size:14px;letter-spacing:.02em}
.cr.hero .v{left:44px;top:46px;height:28px;min-width:44px;padding:0 9px;
 font-size:20px;line-height:28px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.01em}

/* ============================================================
   THE SUMMARY SURFACE.

   Analytics across the top, the story and the structures side by
   side beneath it, then the spiritual layer and the numbers full
   width. Three tracks on a desktop, one sequence on a phone, and
   the document order is already the phone order so the stack costs
   one media query and no second markup.
   ============================================================ */
/* THE CAP WAS FIXED AT 1180 AND THE RAILS LEARNED TO COLLAPSE AFTER IT WAS
   WRITTEN. Round LV, his words: "now that we've got the left and right
   collapsible, we have all this empty space on the left and right of our
   centre column." Measured with both rails shut at 1600: the stage grows to
   about 1441px and the reading sat centred inside it at 1180, wasting about
   130px of ground on each side, symmetrical and empty rather than
   symmetrical and used. Measured on James at 1600 with both rails shut: the
   stage itself already runs to 1444px, and the wrap sat capped at 1180,
   wasting 264px split evenly either side of the reading. Raised, the wrap
   fills to 1406 of that same 1444, the six pixels either side its own
   padding. The cap is raised rather than removed, because the prose inside
   still holds its own reading measure (.s-p, .s-src, .s-told-e p, .s-ptoward
   all cap themselves in ch, not here), so a wider wrap does not make a
   sentence unreadable; it gives the grids and the side column room to lay
   out properly instead of a hard edge nothing on the page asked for. 1880 is
   a ceiling for a genuinely ultra wide monitor, not a target: at every width
   this build is actually tested on, the stage itself is narrower than this
   and the cap never engages. Read the width off the run rather than this
   comment, the way the project already asks of a count. */
.sum-wrap{width:100%;max-width:1880px;margin:0 auto}

/* the glance strip. every reading the product makes, in one row. */
.s-glance{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;padding-bottom:20px;
 border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);margin-bottom:22px}
.s-gl{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;background:transparent;border:0;
 padding:4px 10px 4px 4px;border-radius:999px;cursor:pointer;color:var(--dim);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);min-height:var(--tap)}
.s-gl:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.s-gl-k{white-space:nowrap}

/* the two panels */
.s-two{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.25fr) minmax(0,1fr);gap:28px;
 align-items:start}
@media (max-width:900px){.s-two{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:26px}}

/* THE STORY. running prose, so it takes the reading measure and the reading
   size. 16px floor, 65 characters, which is the one part of this surface a
   person actually reads rather than scans. */
.s-story{min-width:0}
.s-p{font-size:16px;line-height:1.72;color:var(--mid);margin:0 0 14px;max-width:66ch}
.s-p b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500}
.s-src{font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--dim);margin:14px 0 0;max-width:70ch}
.s-src b{color:var(--mid);font-weight:500}

/* THE STRUCTURES. scanned, not read, so the rhythm is one row per thing and
   every row is the same object: a ring with its value, then a name. */
.s-struct{min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column}
.s-sel{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;align-items:center;margin-bottom:2px}
.s-sel-r{font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;color:var(--rc,var(--dim));
 border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--rc) 40%,transparent);border-radius:999px;
 padding:4px 10px}
/* a blueprint domain is a choice, not a measurement, so it gets an icon and a
   name and no ring. a ring here would be a number nobody entered. */
.s-dom{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;background:var(--sunk);border:0;
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:7px 11px 7px 8px;cursor:pointer;color:var(--ink);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;min-height:var(--tap);transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.s-dom:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.s-dom svg{width:16px;height:16px;fill:none;stroke:var(--rc,var(--mid));stroke-width:1.7;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;flex:0 0 auto}
.s-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:11px;width:100%;background:transparent;border:0;
 border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);padding:9px 2px;cursor:pointer;text-align:left;
 min-height:var(--tap);transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.s-row:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.s-row-t{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1px;min-width:0}
.s-row-t b{font-size:14px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}
.s-row-t em{font-style:normal;font-size:12px;color:var(--dim)}
.s-chain{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:7px;margin-top:4px}
.s-ch{display:inline-flex;align-items:baseline;gap:6px;background:var(--sunk);
 border-radius:999px;padding:6px 12px;font-size:12px;color:var(--dim)}
.s-ch b{font-family:var(--num);font-size:14px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}

/* THE SPIRITUAL LAYER. glyphs, and no boxes around them, on the owner's
   ruling. The box was doing nothing the glyph was not already doing. */
.s-spirit{margin-top:34px;padding-top:26px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.s-chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin-top:14px}
.s-chip{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:3px;background:transparent;
 border:0;border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:12px 14px 10px;cursor:pointer;min-width:84px;
 min-height:var(--tap);color:var(--mid);font-family:var(--sans);transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.s-chip:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.s-chip-g{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;height:30px;color:var(--accent)}
.s-chip-g svg{width:24px;height:24px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.5;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.s-uni{font-size:26px;line-height:1}
.s-num-g{font-family:var(--num);font-size:25px;font-weight:300;line-height:1}
.s-chip-l{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ink);white-space:nowrap}
.s-chip-v{font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;color:var(--dim)}
.s-agree{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px;margin-top:16px}
.s-ag,.s-dg{font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.55;padding:8px 0 8px 13px;color:var(--mid);
 border-left:2px solid var(--good,#5FD5A6)}
.s-dg{border-left-color:var(--alarm)}

/* NUMEROLOGY. six numbers, then every name on its own. The number is the
   object here, so it is set large in the numeric face and the words hang off
   it, rather than a ring, which would imply a proportion it does not have. */
.s-numer{margin-top:34px;padding-top:26px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.s-nrows{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(210px,1fr));gap:4px;
 margin-top:12px}
.s-nrow{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:13px;background:transparent;border:0;
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:9px 10px;cursor:pointer;text-align:left;
 min-height:var(--tap);transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);min-width:0}
.s-nrow:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.s-nv{font-family:var(--num);font-size:27px;font-weight:300;line-height:1;color:var(--accent);
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;flex:0 0 auto;min-width:36px;text-align:center}
.s-nv.sm{font-size:20px;min-width:28px}
.s-nrow.master .s-nv{color:var(--alarm)}
.s-nt{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1px;min-width:0}
.s-nt b{font-size:13.5px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}
.s-nt em{font-style:normal;font-size:12px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.45}
.s-nparts{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(210px,1fr));gap:4px;
 margin-top:10px}
.s-npart{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:13px;padding:8px 10px;min-width:0}
/* Punch flattens the two surfaces this page leans on. */
body.punch .s-gl:hover,body.punch .s-row:hover,body.punch .s-nrow:hover,
body.punch .s-chip:hover,body.punch .s-dom:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
body.punch .s-row{border-bottom-color:transparent}

.crlab{font-size:14.5px;font-weight:400;margin-left:11px;color:var(--ink)}
.crrow{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px;align-items:center}
.crline{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0;margin-bottom:8px}

/* ---------- verdict / finding ---------- */
.verdict{border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:13px 17px;background:rgba(223,204,126,.06);
 border-radius:0 var(--r-s) var(--r-s) 0;margin:12px 0}
.verdict p{font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.75;color:var(--mid);margin:0 0 9px;max-width:78ch;font-weight:300}
.verdict p:last-child{margin:0}
.verdict b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}

/* ---------- stage ---------- */
/* ============================================================
   THE STAGE. A grid, because absolute positioning was a promise
   and not a guarantee.

   Six controls were laid over the wheel at hand chosen offsets,
   and at any width the author had not looked at they landed on
   the labels the wheel draws: the compass ruler through Sacral
   and Root, the accuracy figure on Solar, the balance strip
   through Heart. Each fix moved one box and broke another width.

   Three rows and two columns. The wheel gets the cell that is
   left over and nothing else is inside it, so no width can put a
   control on a label. The other tab surfaces stay absolute and
   cover the whole stage, which is what they are for.

        key            tier and the split
        the wheel      the compass
        balance        accuracy
   ============================================================ */
/* Three columns, and the outer two are the same width. The compass needs a
   lane of its own or it draws across the wheel, and a lane on one side only
   pushes the wheel off centre by half its width. The left lane is empty and
   exists to hold the wheel in the middle of the stage, which is where the
   owner put it. */
/* THE RENDER GROUND IS #101010, ruled, ON THE THREE RENDER SURFACES.

   The ruling named field, body and compass. I applied it to `.stage`, which
   is the centre container and also hosts Summary, Knowledge, Energetics and
   Settings, so every text surface got a black rectangle. Under Snow that is
   a black box in the middle of a white app rendering Snow's dark ink: the
   reading paragraph measured 2.22 to 1 against its own ground, and 174 of
   360 text runs on Summary failed. The one lighting a practitioner would
   pick to sit beside a client in a lit room was the one that broke.

   Scoped to the three surfaces that were ruled. A reading surface sits on
   the panel, where it can be read. */
body.tab-field .stage,body.tab-energy .stage,body.tab-compass .stage,body.tab-masksview .stage,
#emap,#masksview,#cone{background:#101010}
/* THREE ROWS, NOT TWO. The third is the legend's, and it is its own row
   because the alternatives both failed a gate. Under the wheel it ran through
   the lower key strip, and then through the wheel's own nameplates at depth 3,
   where the plates reach furthest down. Sharing the top strip's row made it
   fight the three pills for the same line.

   A row of its own costs the wheel about twenty pixels of height, which the
   wheel already handles because it is sized from its box, and it buys a line
   that cannot collide with anything by construction.

   The legend was cut on 25 September (BA9) and its row is empty now, so it
   is zero high and the wheel has the height back. It stays in the template
   because the canvas and .pol2 address row 3 by number. */
/* AND A FOURTH, THE DOCK, 26 September. The key strip left row 1 for it, so
   row 1 is empty and zero high like row 2, and the picture keeps the height
   it had: what the strip took at the head, the dock takes at the foot. */
/* AND THE DOCK LEFT FOR THE LEFT RAIL, ED in TASKS.md, so row 4 is empty and
   zero high too. The glass bar floats over the stage's head instead, in a
   strip the stage keeps clear with its own padding, so the bar lies over the
   stage's ground and never over a mark. */
body.tab-field .stage{padding-top:64px}
.stage{--lane:128px;position:relative;display:grid;
 grid-template-columns:var(--lane) minmax(0,1fr) var(--lane);
 grid-template-rows:auto auto minmax(0,1fr) auto;
 align-items:center;min-height:0;border-radius:var(--r);overflow:hidden}
canvas#cv{display:block;grid-column:2;grid-row:3;width:100%;height:100%;
 min-width:0;min-height:0;touch-action:none;cursor:crosshair}
/* On a coarse pointer the wheel must not eat a scroll. touch-action:none
   made the canvas a dead zone across the thumb rest, which is also how a
   scroll attempt became a charge write. Vertical panning goes to the page
   and a tap still reaches the canvas. */
@media (pointer:coarse){ canvas#cv{touch-action:pan-y;cursor:default} }
/* AND THE RENDITIONS THE SAME, GF in TASKS.md. #frend carried no touch-action,
   so a pinch on Frames or Dial was the browser's and zoomed the whole page by
   4.96 while the picture's own zoom stayed at 1. pan-y keeps the page's scroll
   and gives two fingers to fieldPinch in ui/ui.js, as on the wheel. */
@media (pointer:coarse){ #frend{touch-action:pan-y} }
/* ============================================================
   THE FIELD DRAWN THREE WAYS. BP8, 25 September.

   A rendition draws into the canvas's own cell. The switch between the three
   sat in the stage's left lane, CQ, and is three circles at the head of the
   right rail now, ruled on DR and DY: "move that to the secondary nav on the
   right hand side." The layer row that took the depth row's place in the sub
   bar is gone with it, into the glass bar, which drives all three pictures.
   ============================================================ */
/* THE SWITCH IS THE OVERLAY IN THE UPPER RIGHT NOW, EZ in TASKS.md: "On my
   right hand side overlay ... This should be opposite of the overlay items
   that you have on the upper left." The glass bar lies at top 10, left 12;
   this lies at top 10, right 12, in the same strip the stage keeps clear, so
   the two read as one pair of overlays across the head of the picture.

   Its three circles are the bar's own, the same size, glass, shade and ring,
   so it carries the bar's weight, and they sit on a capsule of that glass
   because they are one control choosing one of three, where the bar's
   circles are thirteen separate switches. The capsule is what says so. */
.fview{position:absolute;top:8px;right:12px;z-index:6;display:flex;gap:4px;align-items:center;
 padding:2px;border-radius:999px;background:var(--fb-glass);border:1px solid var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 8px 22px var(--fb-shade)}
/* the Field's alone. The stage hosts every surface, and three switches for a
   picture that is not there are three dead controls */
body:not(.tab-field) .fview,body:not(.tab-field) .fzoom{display:none}
/* a rendition has no reading, so the one that is up carries a whole ring in
   the accent and a lifted disc, and the two that are not stay glass */
.fview .fb-orb{--c:var(--accent)}
.fview .fb-b.on .fb-arc .val{stroke-dasharray:100 100}
/* and the two that are not up draw no arc at all: a round cap on a dash of
   nought is a dot, and a dot at twelve o'clock read as a reading of one */
.fview .fb-b:not(.on) .fb-arc .val{opacity:0}
/* ZOOM, IN THE LOWER RIGHT, out of the bar for the switch's sake. A column,
   because the lower right of the stage is the compass's lane on the wheel,
   128 across, and three circles in a row are 136. */
.fzoom{position:absolute;right:12px;bottom:12px;z-index:6;display:flex}
/* ROUND OD, his words: "For the zoom icons, have those horizontal instead of vertical." Zoom out, zoom in and reframe sit in a row now, as they already did on a phone. */
.fzoom .fb-grp{flex-direction:row;gap:6px}
#frend{grid-column:2;grid-row:3;width:100%;height:100%;min-width:0;min-height:0;
 position:relative;overflow:hidden}
/* FRAMES TAKES THE EMPTY LEFT LANE. The lane held the renditions switch and
   is empty now, so on a wide screen the frame spans it and runs flush from
   the stage's left edge to the compass lane. The wheel and the dial are
   circles and stay in the middle cell. Kept or pulled back is his, ED. */
@media (min-width:721px){#frend.flush{grid-column:1 / 3}}
/* zoomed in, the picture can be dragged, and a drag takes no hover on the way */
#frend.zoomed{cursor:grab}
#frend.dragging{cursor:grabbing}
#frend.dragging *{pointer-events:none}
body:not(.tab-field) #frend{display:none!important}
/* the picture takes the pointer only where there is something to open. A
   thread, a hairline or the wash is not a target, and drawn over an address
   it would have taken the press the address was owed */
.frsvg{display:block;pointer-events:none}
.frsvg [data-h]{pointer-events:auto;cursor:pointer}
.frsvg text{font-family:var(--sans);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* a hidden layer takes its marks and its words with it. One class per switch
   on the glass bar, off-<layer>, written by frLayers in ui/rings.js. A thread
   carries the class of the tier at its far end, F-<tier>, so it goes with
   either end: a thread is drawn when both its ends are. */
#frend.off-addresses .L-addresses,#frend.off-addresses .F-addr,#frend.off-seats .L-seats,
#frend.off-laws .L-laws,#frend.off-gates .L-gates,#frend.off-shadow .L-shadow,#frend.off-stories .L-stories,
#frend.off-saboteurs .L-patterns,#frend.off-saboteurs .F-sab,
#frend.off-complexes .T-cx,#frend.off-complexes .F-cx,#frend.off-hyper .T-hy,#frend.off-hyper .F-hy,
#frend.off-character .T-sup,#frend.off-character [data-call=chains],
#frend.off-archetypes .L-archetypes,#frend.off-domains .L-domains{
 opacity:0;visibility:hidden;pointer-events:none!important;
 transition:opacity var(--t-element) cubic-bezier(.55,.085,.68,.53),visibility 0s linear var(--t-element)}
/* A LAYER ARRIVES AND LEAVES, FJ in TASKS.md: "where are my animations?" It
   was display:none, which cannot be animated, so a switch was a cut. Now the
   layer fades out in 220ms on an ease in, a quadratic one, the canvas's own
   curve: the steeper ease in the motion table measured as ninety
   milliseconds of nothing after the press, and is taken out of the picture by
   visibility once it has, so a hidden layer is still not drawn and still not
   pressable; and it comes back on --t-context, 420ms, eased out into place,
   the named step nearest the wheel's own ENTER_SPAN of 380, because every
   duration in this sheet is one of the four steps and the design gate holds
   that. Every selector here is inside :where so
   it weighs nothing, and each off rule above wins its leaving timing over it
   without an order to keep. The wheel does the same on its canvas, layFade in
   ui/wheel.js, with the sweep round from twelve o'clock a canvas can clip. */
:where(#frend) :where([class^="L-"],[class^="T-"],[class*=" F-"]){
 transition:opacity var(--t-context) var(--ease-out),visibility 0s linear 0s}
/* THE WORDS COME IN WITH THE ZOOM, FJ. txtZoomA in ui/component.js reads the
   picture's zoom and rings.js writes it here as --fztx, 0 at the whole
   picture and 1 from 1.75x in. At 0 the host carries notext and the words
   are taken out, not set faint. The core figure and the gate pills are not
   words and are not here. */
/* THE CORE'S FEATHERS ON FRAMES AND DIAL, faded by the zoom, ui/rings.js
   frFeathers. The figures give ground as the core opens, to under half their
   size on a backing disc, the wheel's move for the same moment: at three
   fifths, the first cut, the coherence figure still sat across the triad. */
#frend .frf0{opacity:var(--frf0,0)}#frend .frf1{opacity:var(--frf1,0)}#frend .frf2{opacity:var(--frf2,0)}
#frend .frfr{opacity:calc(var(--frfo,0) * .9)}
#frend .frcq{transform-box:fill-box;transform-origin:center;transform:scale(calc(1 - .55 * var(--frfo,0)))}
#frend .frcq-bk{opacity:calc(var(--frfo,0) * .86)}
#frend [data-call],#frend .frnm{opacity:var(--fztx,1);transition:opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),visibility 0s linear 0s}
#frend.notext [data-call],#frend.notext .frnm{visibility:hidden;transition:opacity var(--t-micro) cubic-bezier(.55,.085,.68,.53),visibility 0s linear var(--t-micro)}
/* the names a zoom brings in, set level beside their marks in page pixels, so
   eleven pixels is eleven pixels at any zoom and never under the floor */
.frnm{position:absolute;inset:0;pointer-events:none;overflow:hidden}
.frnm span{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;white-space:nowrap;font-size:11.5px;font-weight:500;
 line-height:1;letter-spacing:.005em;color:var(--c);
 text-shadow:0 0 3px var(--g),0 0 3px var(--g),0 0 2px var(--g)}
.frnm span.hid{visibility:hidden}
.frnm span.sel{font-weight:600}
/* THE TENSION RUNS, FJ. The pulses on the renditions' threads sit in their
   own picture over the main one, so the main picture, thousands of marks, is
   never repainted for them: only this sheet of a few hundred paths is. */
.frflow{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;pointer-events:none;will-change:transform}
/* the picture a switch replaced, dissolving over the new one, ringsDraw */
.frghost{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;pointer-events:none}
/* A PULSE RUNS AT ONE SPEED, which is the one place this sheet has a linear
   curve on purpose: an ease would make every pulse surge and stall once a
   period, and a wave on a wire does neither. Its speed is the reading. */
.frflow path{fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;animation:frflow var(--d,3s) linear infinite;
 animation-delay:var(--ph,0s)}
/* both ends lengths, or the two would not interpolate and the pulse would
   jump once a period instead of running: measured, a bare number against a
   calc of a variable reads as one fixed value all the way through */
@keyframes frflow{from{stroke-dashoffset:0px}to{stroke-dashoffset:calc(var(--p,64) * -1px)}}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.frflow{display:none}}
/* and the wheel's wash, which is the page's own canvas and not the picture's.
   Its fade is an opacity drawAura sets, auraK in ui/wheel.js; this takes the
   canvas out once that fade has run, and puts it back at once on the way in,
   so the class never cuts a fade short. */
#bgaura{transition:visibility 0s linear 0s}
body.tab-field.noshadow #bgaura{visibility:hidden;transition:visibility 0s linear var(--t-element)}
/* ============================================================
   THE GLASS BAR. ui/fieldbar.js, ported from proto/glassbar, DK to ED in
   TASKS.md. It replaced the layer row that stood here and the depth row.

   Glass means the picture shows through it, so it reads as a thing lying on
   the Field rather than a band cut out of it. NOT BLURRED, which the
   prototype was: every backdrop-filter over the wheel's canvas is re-read and
   re-filtered on each frame the wheel repaints, measured at 12 frames a
   second under Glass, and design gate 13 holds that nothing over the Field
   carries one. It failed on this bar's circles, seventeen of them, the first
   time the port met the gate. So the glass is a translucent fill, a shade
   more opaque than the prototype's, and the blur is not carried. Each layer is one glass
   circle: "the pills take up a lot of real estate." Ring, not fill: the ring
   round each circle is a reading, and the only solid thing is the small value
   pill, which is the product's own pill.
   ============================================================ */
.fbar{position:absolute;top:10px;left:12px;z-index:6;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;
 flex-wrap:nowrap;pointer-events:none}
body:not(.tab-field) .fbar,body:not(.tab-field) .fb-float{display:none!important}
.fbar>*,.fb-full>*{pointer-events:auto}
.fb-full{display:flex;gap:12px}
.fb-grp{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:6px}
.fbar .fb-foldp,.fbar.folded .fb-full{display:none}
.fbar.folded .fb-foldp{display:flex}
/* THE BAR CLOSES, GB in TASKS.md, ui/fieldbar.js fbShutWire. Open, the one
   control sits in the free corner under the first circle, because the row
   has 22 pixels spare at 1600 and a circle costs 56. Shut, everything else
   goes and it is static, so it is the bar's first circle, in the corner the
   tools were in: lfold's own two states. After the fold rules above, which
   tie with the first rule here on weight, so shut wins over folded. It has
   no reading, so it draws the track and no arc, the switch's rule for the
   two renditions that are not up. */
/* AND NOW IT LEADS THE ROW, GO in TASKS.md, his words: "Where it says close
   the tools, I want that to be the first left, upper left icon. Expand,
   close." The second time he has asked for the corner, so the compromise
   under the first circle is over. The room comes from the left column, which
   starts shut now (colFold in ui/ui.js): at 1600 the row has 266 pixels spare
   with it shut and 22 with it open, measured. Opened at 1600, the row folds
   to this control, Layers and Depth, which is the fold this bar already has
   for a stage too narrow for it, rather than a circle hung under the row. */
.fbar .fb-tog{position:static;order:-1}
.fbar.shut>*:not(.fb-tog){display:none}
.fbar.shut .fb-tog{position:static}
.fb-tog .fb-arc .val{opacity:0}
.fb-tog .lf-ch{transform-box:fill-box;transform-origin:center;transition:transform var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.fbar.shut .fb-tog .lf-ch{transform:rotate(180deg)}
/* THE TWO GLASSES, keyed on the ground and not on the lighting's name. The
   bar reads stageLight(), the one reading of what the stage is painted, and
   takes .fb-lt on a light one. The inks are Dark's own and Glass white's own
   token values and the accent is the pair GOLDC() draws the canvas in,
   because under Snow the theme's tokens are paper ink and the stage stays
   the ruled #101010: the bar needs the ground's ink, which INK() and frMount
   already choose the same way. */
.fbar,.fb-float,.fview,.fzoom,.cn-zoom,.cnbar,.cnview,.cn-pan,#railtop,#acc{--ink:#EFEDE8;--mid:#B4B0A8;--dim:#94908A;--accent:#7EB8D4;
 --edge-2:rgba(255,255,255,.14);--edge:rgba(255,255,255,.09);
 --fb-glass:rgba(22,24,32,.74);--fb-glass-on:rgba(40,44,58,.86);--fb-shade:rgba(0,0,0,.30);--fb-pillbg:#161820}
.fbar.fb-lt,.fb-float.fb-lt,.fview.fb-lt,.fzoom.fb-lt,.cn-zoom.fb-lt,.cnbar.fb-lt,.cnview.fb-lt,.cn-pan.fb-lt,#railtop.fb-lt,#acc.fb-lt{--ink:#14161C;--mid:#4A4843;--dim:#6E6B65;--accent:#2F6E92;
 --edge-2:rgba(20,22,28,.16);--edge:rgba(20,22,28,.10);
 --fb-glass:rgba(252,252,250,.80);--fb-glass-on:rgba(255,255,255,.94);--fb-shade:rgba(20,22,28,.14);--fb-pillbg:#FAFAF7}
/* ---- one layer, one object. 44 by 44, the tap floor ---- */
.fb-b{position:relative;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:4px;
 min-width:var(--tap);min-height:var(--tap);padding:0;margin:0;border:0;border-radius:50%;background:transparent;
 color:var(--dim);cursor:pointer;font:inherit;
 /* no double tap zoom on these. Measured on the prototype at 390: zoom in
    then reframe, two quick taps a thumb's width apart, and the browser held
    the second tap as the start of a page zoom and the reframe never arrived */
 touch-action:manipulation}
.fb-b:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:2px}
.fb-orb{--c:var(--accent);position:relative;width:40px;height:40px;border-radius:50%;flex:0 0 auto;
 display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;background:var(--fb-glass);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.08),0 6px 18px var(--fb-shade);
 transition:background-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.fb-arc{position:absolute;inset:0;width:40px;height:40px;transform:rotate(-90deg);overflow:visible}
.fb-arc circle{fill:none;stroke-width:2.6}
.fb-arc .trk{stroke:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 16%,transparent)}
.fb-arc .val{stroke:var(--c);stroke-linecap:round;opacity:.3;
 transition:stroke-dasharray var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out),opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.fb-gl{display:flex}
.fb-gl svg{width:18px;height:18px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
/* the value rides the lower right, over the ring. Eleven, the type floor:
   the prototype set it at ten and the design gate holds eleven everywhere */
.fb-v{position:absolute;left:24px;top:26px;min-width:18px;height:17px;padding:0 4px;border-radius:999px;
 font:600 11px/17px var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
 text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;letter-spacing:.01em;color:var(--ink);
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 30%,var(--fb-pillbg));
 border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 55%,transparent);
 transition:opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),background-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
/* OFF: the pill keeps its number and loses its fill, the ring keeps its
   length and loses its colour, the mark drops to dim. Three signals, none of
   them a hidden value, because an off layer is hidden from the picture and
   not unread. */
.fb-b:not(.on) .fb-v{background:var(--fb-pillbg);color:var(--dim);
 border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 22%,transparent)}
.fb-b:not(.on) .fb-orb{box-shadow:0 4px 12px var(--fb-shade)}
.fb-b:hover .fb-orb{background:var(--fb-glass-on)}
.fb-b:hover{color:var(--ink)}
/* ON. The disc lifts, the mark comes up to ink, the ring takes its colour at
   full strength and the value pill with it. */
.fb-b.on{color:var(--ink)}
.fb-b.on .fb-orb{background:var(--fb-glass-on)}
.fb-b.on .fb-arc .val{opacity:1}
/* BROUGHT IN BY ZOOM on the wheel: drawn, but not by the person */
.fb-b.zoomed{color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 80%,transparent)}
.fb-b.zoomed .fb-arc .trk{stroke:var(--accent);stroke-dasharray:3 3}
/* ON, AND WAITING FOR ZOOM: stories on the wheel at arm's length */
.fb-b.wait .fb-orb::after{content:'';position:absolute;right:1px;top:1px;width:7px;height:7px;border-radius:50%;
 background:var(--accent);box-shadow:0 0 0 2px var(--fb-pillbg)}
/* THE ENTRANCE, once a session, ui/fieldbar.js fbEnter: each disc rises four
   pixels into place and its ring draws round to its value, on the stage's
   own curve. The disc and not the button, so every target is its full 44
   from the first frame and the tap gate never meets one mid rise. The ring
   keyframe has no end, so it lands on whatever value the circle carries.
   fb-hold keeps them on their first frame while the boot sheet is up. */
@keyframes fbIn{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(4px) scale(.94)}}
@keyframes fbArc{from{stroke-dasharray:0 100}}
.fb-enter .fb-orb{animation:fbIn var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out) both;
 animation-delay:calc(var(--i,0) * 34ms)}
.fb-enter .fb-arc .val{animation:fbArc 380ms var(--ease-out) both;
 animation-delay:calc(var(--i,0) * 34ms + 90ms)}
.fb-hold .fb-orb,.fb-hold .fb-arc .val{animation-play-state:paused}
body.punch .fb-b.on .fb-orb{background:var(--c)}
body.punch .fb-b.on .fb-gl svg{stroke:var(--on-flat)}
body.punch .fb-v{background:var(--c);color:var(--on-flat);border-color:var(--c)}
/* ---- the folded panel and the depth menu float under their circle ---- */
.fb-float{position:fixed;z-index:40;border-radius:16px;padding:10px;color:var(--ink);
 background:var(--fb-glass-on);
 border:1px solid var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 10px 28px rgba(0,0,0,.28)}
.fb-float[hidden]{display:none}
#fbpanel .fb-grp{flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px;margin-bottom:6px}
#fbpanel .fb-b{min-width:66px;min-height:70px;padding:4px 2px;border-radius:12px}
.fb-nm{font-size:12px;line-height:14px;font-weight:500;white-space:nowrap}
.fb-say{margin:6px 6px 2px;max-width:340px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--mid)}
.fb-say b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
.fb-ph{font-size:12px;line-height:16px;color:var(--dim);margin:4px 6px 4px;font-weight:500}
#fbmenu{padding:6px;width:340px}
.fb-mi{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:10px;width:100%;min-height:var(--tap);padding:8px 10px 8px 6px;
 border:0;border-radius:12px;background:transparent;color:var(--mid);text-align:left;cursor:pointer;font:inherit}
.fb-mi:hover{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 6%,transparent);color:var(--ink)}
.fb-ic{position:relative;width:34px;height:34px;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
 border:1.5px solid transparent;flex:0 0 auto}
.fb-ic svg{width:20px;height:20px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.fb-mt{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.4}
.fb-mt b{font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)}
.fb-mi[aria-checked=true] .fb-ic{border-color:var(--accent)}
.fb-mi[aria-checked=true] b{color:var(--accent)}
.fb-done{display:none;min-height:var(--tap);min-width:88px;margin:4px 0 0 auto;padding:0 16px;border-radius:12px;
 border:1px solid var(--edge-2);background:transparent;color:var(--ink);font:inherit;font-size:14px;cursor:pointer}
/* THIS PANEL WEARS THE CLASS .sheet AND SO IT WAS ALSO THE PROFILE SHEET.
   The modal's own rule, .sheet at "A sheet, for the profile and the help"
   further down this file, sets display:flex with flex-end justification and
   its own backdrop colour, and it loses to nothing here because it comes
   later. Measured on a phone, 390 wide, on the unchanged build: the panel was
   a single flex row, its three clusters 300 pixels each with the first two
   centred off the left edge of the window (x of minus 418 and minus 118), the
   readout squeezed to 42 pixels and every layer on the first two rows out of
   reach of a finger, which is the whole layer set on the one width this panel
   exists for. It is a column of rows, so it says so, and it keeps its own
   glass and not the modal's dark backdrop. Found while putting the tier locks
   on this panel, where a locked layer has to be reachable by a tap. */
#fbpanel.sheet{left:8px!important;right:8px;bottom:8px;top:auto!important;max-width:none!important;
 display:block;background:var(--fb-glass-on);
 padding:8px 8px 10px;border-radius:18px}
#fbpanel.sheet[hidden]{display:none}
#fbpanel.sheet .fb-ph{display:none}
#fbpanel.sheet .fb-grp{flex-wrap:nowrap;justify-content:flex-start;gap:0;margin-bottom:4px;
 padding-bottom:4px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
/* six columns on every row, so a name sits under its own circle and the rows
   line up */
#fbpanel.sheet .fb-b{min-width:0;flex:0 0 16.66%;min-height:70px}
#fbpanel.sheet .fb-nm{font-size:11px;line-height:13px;white-space:normal;text-align:center;max-width:58px}
#fbpanel.sheet .fb-done{display:block}
#fbpanel.sheet .fb-say{min-height:38px}
/* width:max-content because the readout is measured before it is placed
   (ui.js, the pointer move). Sized to fit it shrank to whatever room its last
   position left, so the width read was where it had been and not what it
   needs, and the side chosen from it could be wrong. */
.probe{position:absolute;pointer-events:none;padding:13px 15px;border-radius:var(--r-s);
 font-size:13px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--mid);opacity:0;transition: opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);
 width:max-content;max-width:300px;z-index:9;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);box-shadow:var(--shadow)}
.probe.on{opacity:1}
.probe b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
.probe u{text-decoration:none;color:var(--gold);font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;font-weight:500}
.probe hr{border:0;border-top:1px solid var(--edge);margin:8px 0}
/* the arithmetic under a reading. It is how the number was computed and it is
   not what the number means, so it sits under the meaning and reads quieter. */
.probe .tt-q{font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;opacity:.6;letter-spacing:.01em}
/* the rail's tooltip. Same object as the canvas probe, fixed rather than
   absolute because it hangs off a rail that scrolls inside the page. */
/* A NAMED THING IN THE RAIL IS A BUTTON, and it has to look like one on
   hover without becoming a row of boxes at rest. */
.kbjump{width:100%;background:none;border:0;padding:4px 6px;margin:0 -6px;
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);cursor:pointer;text-align:left;font:inherit;color:inherit;
 transition: background var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.kbjump:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.kbjump:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:1px}
/* THE DOCK, AT THE HEAD OF THE LEFT RAIL, as circles. It was one row along
   the stage's foot, grouped by kind, and it moved on ED in TASKS.md, his
   words: "now that we've got the circular one up here, all the buttons on the
   bottom don't make sense, you should go on the left hand side." Each reading
   is still a button that opens its reading on the right; only where it sits
   and the shape it is drawn in changed. */
.fdock{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;gap:16px;min-width:0;position:relative;
 padding:4px 0 16px 20px;margin:0 0 12px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
body:not(.tab-field) .fdock{display:none}
/* CQ AND DQ, A PAIR. "Line those up so they don't look like a design element
   that's not quite designed right, but has a symmetry, and I do want the CQ
   number bigger, so it's in your face." CQ is the large circle, and the row
   keeps one centre line through every circle on it. */
/* TWO ROWS, EZ in TASKS.md: "just make sure there's just two rows." SQ left
   the first row and accuracy took its place, so the first row is DQ, CQ and
   accuracy and the second the four. #key takes no box of its own here, so
   its two circles and accuracy are three items on one line, spread to the
   rail's edges.

   Spread and not centred in three equal thirds, which is what the row did
   with SQ in it, because accuracy keeps its word to the left of its circle,
   ruled, and word and circle together are 112 wide against a third of 264.
   Measured at 1600 with Marcus: DQ's pill ends 4.8 clear of CQ's ring, and
   CQ's pill, which rides its ring's lower right, reached 0.7 into the word's
   line box, so the word sits 3 higher than its circle's centre line and the
   two clear by 2.3. */
/* AND ACCURACY LEFT THE ROW FOR THE STAGE'S LOWER LEFT, round LR in TASKS.md,
   so the row is DQ and CQ. Spread to the rail's edges, two circles read as
   two ends of nothing, so they sit centred on the four's own gap. */
/* THE READINGS ARE BARS, round OG. His words: "instead of the circles, have
   those horizontal bars stacked on top of each other with the text, with their
   name, and the color of the bar based off of the percent. This should tighten
   up that root energetics area." Six rows, a name on the left and the figure
   on the right with a bar under both, each row still a button to its reading
   and each at the 44px floor, because the whole row is the target. CQ and DQ
   are the first group, the four that move through a person the second, a
   hairline between them. The fill and its colour are written inline by
   rbRow (ui/component.js); this sheet only lays them out. */
.fdock{gap:2px}
.fdock .fdrow{display:block;min-width:0}
.fdock .fdrow .key,.fdock .keylo{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;gap:2px;flex-wrap:nowrap}
.fdock .keylo{margin-top:6px;padding-top:6px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.fdock .kb.rbar{position:relative;display:block;width:100%;min-width:0;height:var(--tap);
 min-height:var(--tap);padding:0;border:0;border-radius:var(--r-xs);overflow:hidden;
 background:rgba(128,128,128,.14);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--edge);text-align:left;
 white-space:nowrap;cursor:pointer}
/* the fill is the row itself, and a soft light travels along its edge so a
   bar reads as live and not as a printed rectangle; reduced motion holds it */
.rb-t{position:absolute;inset:0;display:block;border-radius:inherit;overflow:hidden}
.rb-t i{display:block;height:100%;background:linear-gradient(90deg,color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 78%,#000),var(--c));
 position:relative;overflow:hidden}
.rb-t i::after{content:"";position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;right:0;width:26px;
 background:linear-gradient(90deg,transparent,rgba(255,255,255,.34));opacity:.7}
/* ROUND OJ. A reading is live, so the light in it travels: a soft band runs
   along the fill from the empty end to the edge and starts again, at the
   rate the Field's own threads run on (--rate, written by rbRate from the
   Shadow layer's DQ), and the six rows take it up in reading order, a beat
   apart, the way the Field takes up its seven seats. Transform and opacity
   only. An unread row has no fill and so no light: idle looks idle. */
.rb-t i::before{content:"";position:absolute;inset:0;transform:translateX(-100%);
 background:linear-gradient(100deg,transparent 30%,rgba(255,255,255,.2) 50%,transparent 70%);
 animation:rbsweep calc(var(--t-breath) * 1.5 / var(--rate,1)) var(--ease-breath) infinite;
 animation-delay:calc(var(--k,0) * var(--t-stagger) * 7)}
@keyframes rbsweep{0%{transform:translateX(-100%)}62%,100%{transform:translateX(100%)}}
#key .rbar:nth-child(1){--k:0}
#key .rbar:nth-child(2){--k:1}
#keylo .rbar:nth-child(1){--k:2}
#keylo .rbar:nth-child(2){--k:3}
#keylo .rbar:nth-child(3){--k:4}
#keylo .rbar:nth-child(4){--k:5}
.rbar.off .rb-t i::before,.rbar.off .rb-t i::after{display:none}
/* THE SHADOW LEANS. Its whole fill breathes on the shadow wave, the period
   the wheel's pulses breathe on (1 / PUL_WAVE_HZ) and the swing the wheel
   gives them (PUL_WAVE times DQ, widened so the eye can find it): a record
   with no shadow holds still, a heavy one surges and eases. */
.rbar[data-q=dq] .rb-t i{animation:rbwave var(--wave,9.09s) var(--ease-breath) infinite}
@keyframes rbwave{0%,100%{opacity:calc(1 - var(--amp,0))}50%{opacity:1}}
/* WHAT CHANGED. A hairline where the fill stood, fading over 2.4 seconds. */
.rbar.rb-gh .rb-t::after{content:"";position:absolute;top:7px;bottom:7px;left:var(--gw,0%);width:2px;
 margin-left:-1px;border-radius:1px;background:var(--ink);opacity:0;
 animation:rbghost 2.4s var(--ease-out) var(--gd,0ms) 1 both}
@keyframes rbghost{0%{opacity:.9}65%{opacity:.5}100%{opacity:0}}
/* THE ROW'S OTHER PARTS, round OJ, and the one motion language they share
   (ui/component.js, rbFx). The mark is drawn in the word's own ink, inside
   .rb-n, so the clipped second copy carries it across the fill's edge with
   the word. */
.rb-ic{flex:0 0 auto;width:16px;height:16px;margin-right:8px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
/* the leading edge of the fill, which only ever shows while a reading moves */
.rb-h{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;bottom:0;width:4px;margin-left:-4px;opacity:0;pointer-events:none;
 background:linear-gradient(90deg,transparent,rgba(255,255,255,.85));will-change:transform,opacity}
/* the share of the fill the shadow is holding, along the fill's foot where no
   word sits */
.rb-pull{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;height:5px;
 background:repeating-linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(0,0,0,.55) 0 2px,rgba(0,0,0,.12) 2px 4px)}
/* DECOHERENCE AS SEVEN HASHES, round OM. One mark a seat, root to crown, in
   the seat's own colour. Each stands from the foot of the row as high as the
   charge on that seat's addresses against what they could hold, and a seat
   with nothing is still a hash, two pixels of its colour, so the seven are
   always there to be read. They are moved twice. The height is a transform
   the row's tween writes (scaleY, from the root up on the bars' own stagger),
   and inside it each mark breathes on the shadow wave with a phase of its own,
   by the amplitude the wheel's pulses take (--amp, written by rbRate), so a
   heavy record shivers and a clear one stands still. */
.rb-hash{position:absolute;right:58px;top:7px;bottom:7px;display:flex;align-items:flex-end;gap:3px;pointer-events:none}
.rb-hash s{position:relative;display:block;width:5px;height:100%;border-radius:2px;text-decoration:none;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--k) 16%,transparent)}
.rb-hash s::before{content:"";position:absolute;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;height:2px;border-radius:1px;background:var(--k)}
.rb-hash b{position:absolute;inset:0;display:block;transform-origin:50% 100%}
.rb-hash u{position:absolute;inset:0;display:block;border-radius:2px;background:var(--k);transform-origin:50% 100%;
 animation:rbhash var(--wave,9.09s) var(--ease-breath) infinite;animation-delay:calc(var(--q) * -.6s)}
@keyframes rbhash{0%,100%{transform:scaleY(1)}50%{transform:scaleY(calc(1 - var(--amp,0) * .5))}}
body.quiet .rb-hash u,body.rm .rb-hash u{animation:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.rb-hash u{animation:none}}
/* FLOW AS A SINE WAVE, round OM. The row is taller, its name on the first
   line and the wave under it, seven stretches root to crown, each in its
   seat's colour over a faint band of the same, with the full range marked by
   a dashed centre line. A bead rides it at the Field's rate and loses light
   at each seat by the share that seat holds back. */
.fdock .kb.rbar.rb-waverow{height:68px;min-height:68px}
.rb-waverow .rb-row{bottom:auto;height:30px}
.rb-wave{position:absolute;left:12px;right:12px;top:30px;bottom:4px;display:block;pointer-events:none}
.rb-wave svg{display:block;width:100%;height:100%;overflow:visible}
.rb-wave .wb rect{opacity:.11}
.rb-wave .wm{fill:none;stroke:var(--edge-2);stroke-width:1;stroke-dasharray:2 4;vector-effect:non-scaling-stroke}
.rb-wave .wv{fill:none;stroke-width:2.2;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;vector-effect:non-scaling-stroke}
.rb-bead{fill:var(--ink)}
body.quiet .rb-bead,body.rm .rb-bead{display:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.rb-bead{display:none}}
/* the percent fill is not drawn on these two rows, and the words are not
   clipped, because nothing is under them but the track */
.rbar.rb-hashrow .rb-t i,.rbar.rb-waverow .rb-t i{opacity:0;animation:none}
.rbar.rb-hashrow .rb-over,.rbar.rb-waverow .rb-over,.rbar.rb-hashrow .rb-h,.rbar.rb-waverow .rb-h{display:none}
/* the chip on the shadow row rides the corner above its figure, because the hashes
   and the name take the middle of the row */
.rbar.rb-hashrow .rb-d{right:8px;top:1px;margin-top:0;height:14px;line-height:14px;font-size:11px;padding:0 5px}
.rbar.rb-waverow .rb-d{right:60px;top:15px;margin-top:-9px}
.rbar.off .rb-hash,.rbar.off .rb-h,.rbar.off .rb-pull{display:none}
/* the chip, with a ground of its own, because it lies across the fill's edge
   and has to read on either side of it. In over 90ms and a 4px rise to rest,
   held, out by 2.2s; set by a negative delay so a rewrite continues it. */
.rb-d{position:absolute;right:60px;top:50%;margin-top:-9px;height:18px;padding:0 6px;border-radius:9px;
 background:var(--panel-2);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--edge-2);font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;
 font-weight:600;line-height:18px;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink);opacity:0;pointer-events:none}
.rb-d.bad{color:var(--alarm)}
.rb-d.on{animation:rbchip 2.4s var(--ease-out) var(--cd,0ms) 1 both}
@keyframes rbchip{0%{opacity:0;transform:translateY(4px)}4%{opacity:1;transform:none}82%{opacity:1;transform:none}100%{opacity:0;transform:none}}
/* still: it prints and clears at 2.4s with no slide, the way a status does. A
   timer clears it (rbChip), because a reduced motion profile has every CSS
   animation off at the root and a chip cleared by one would never print. */
.rb-d.on.st{opacity:1;animation:none;transform:none}
.rb-row{position:absolute;inset:0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;
 gap:10px;padding:0 12px;pointer-events:none}
.rb-n{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:13.5px;font-weight:500;line-height:1.2;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:.01em}
.rb-v{font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-size:16px;font-weight:600;
 line-height:1;color:var(--ink);text-align:right}
/* the same words again, dark, and shown only over the fill: the clip is the
   filled width, so a letter turns at the edge of the bar */
.rb-over .rb-n,.rb-over .rb-v{color:var(--on,#0B0D10)}
.rb-over{clip-path:inset(0 calc(100% - var(--w,0%)) 0 0)}
/* PAIRED. A row lights the glass bar's circle for the same reading, and the
   circle lights the row, one ring in the row's own colour. */
.fdock .kb.rbar:hover,.fdock .kb.rbar.rb-hot{box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--c)}
.fdock .kb.rbar:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--c);outline-offset:1px}
.rbar.off .rb-v,.rbar.off .rb-n{color:var(--dim)}
.rbar.off .rb-over{display:none}
body.quiet .rb-t i::before,body.rm .rb-t i::before,body.quiet .rb-t i::after,body.rm .rb-t i::after,
body.quiet .rbar[data-q=dq] .rb-t i,body.rm .rbar[data-q=dq] .rb-t i{animation:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
 .rb-t i::before,.rb-t i::after,.rbar[data-q=dq] .rb-t i{animation:none}}
.fdock .kb:hover{background:transparent}
.fdock .kb:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:3px}
.key{display:flex;flex-wrap:nowrap;gap:4px;align-items:center}
.kb{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;min-height:var(--tap);padding:2px 4px;
 background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 color:var(--mid);font:inherit;font-size:12px;cursor:pointer;text-align:left;white-space:nowrap}
.kb:hover{background:var(--sunk);color:var(--ink)}
/* THE LETTERS IN THE RING. Ruled: CQ, DQ and SQ are essentially an icon, so
   they sit where an icon sits. Tabular figures and tight tracking, because
   two characters in a small ring have to read as a unit rather than as two
   letters that happen to be near each other. */
.cr .gl-t{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
 font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.03em;
 color:var(--c);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;line-height:1}
/* the strip is rings now, so the button is the ring and nothing else, and the
   padding is stated once. Two .kb rules both setting padding is a rule that
   depends on which one loses, which the design gate now refuses. */
.kb b{color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;margin-right:4px}
body.punch .kb{border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
/* the wheel is the main graphic. in Punch it carries a fifth more colour. */
body.punch canvas#cv{filter:saturate(1.2)}
.gate-r .ad-m{margin-left:auto;font-family:var(--num);color:var(--dim);font-size:11.5px}
/* ============================================================
   THE BADGE. The icon carries the percent, the pill carries the number.

   One object in two resolutions, so the reading is a shape before it is a
   figure and a rail of twenty of these reads as fuller and emptier rings
   rather than as a column of decimals.
   ============================================================ */
/* THE PILL LIVES INSIDE THE BADGE'S OWN BOX.

   Hung off the corner with a negative offset it was outside the element, so
   the row's grid cell clipped it and a 5.4 rendered as a 5 with the point
   cut off. The badge reserves the space as padding and the pill sits in it,
   which means nothing overflows and nothing can clip it, whatever it is put
   inside. */
.crb{position:relative;display:inline-block;flex:0 0 auto;box-sizing:content-box}
.crb-a{display:block;transform:rotate(-90deg)}
.crb-g{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;display:grid;place-items:center;color:var(--c);
 pointer-events:none}
.crb-g svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
/* the pill, at the lower right, overlapping. Two things side by side are two
   things; this is one reading. */
.crb-v{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;
 font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;line-height:1;
 background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink);
 border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 44%,transparent);
 border-radius:999px;padding:2px 6px;white-space:nowrap;
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* THE PILL SITS CLEAR OF THE RING, in the padding the badge reserves for it.
   Laid on the corner of the box it covered the glyph it was qualifying and a
   row of five read as five smudges. */
.crb.xs{width:26px;height:26px;padding:0 20px 7px 0}
.crb.xs .crb-g{width:26px;height:26px}
.crb.xs .crb-g svg{width:11px;height:11px;stroke-width:2}
.crb.xs .crb-v{font-size:11px;padding:1px 5px}
.crb.sm{width:34px;height:34px;padding:0 24px 8px 0}
.crb.sm .crb-g{width:34px;height:34px}
.crb.sm .crb-g svg{width:15px;height:15px;stroke-width:1.8}
.crb.sm .crb-v{font-size:11.5px;padding:1.5px 6px}
.crb.md{width:44px;height:44px;padding:0 28px 9px 0}
/* the ring and the mark, with no pill and therefore no room kept for one. The
   codex row carries its figure at the far right, aligned with the column. */
.crb.bare{padding:0}
.crb.md .crb-g{width:44px;height:44px}
.crb.md .crb-g svg{width:20px;height:20px;stroke-width:1.6}
.crb.md .crb-v{font-size:12.5px;padding:2px 7px}
body.punch .crb-v{border-color:transparent;background:var(--c);color:var(--on-accent)}
/* a badge needs room at its lower right, which a tight row does not give it */
.ad-r{column-gap:14px}
.ad-r.static{cursor:default}
/* the state beside the band name on the scale, so the ladder reads as ten
   conditions and not ten adjectives */
.ad-k em{font-style:normal;color:var(--dim);font-size:11.5px;margin-left:8px}
.ad-r.on{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c,var(--gold)) 22%,transparent)}
/* balance, along the bottom of the stage. the centre is the neutral point
   and the marker moves out from it. */
/* the howto sits bottom left and the accuracy block bottom right, and the
   strip crossed both. it takes the lane between them and sits above them. */
/* THE BALANCE STRIP MOVED INTO THE RAIL.

   It was absolutely positioned at the bottom left of the Field canvas and
   hidden on every other tab, so masculine against feminine existed on one
   screen out of seven. In the rail it is a block like every other reading,
   in flow, on every tab, and it needs none of the pinning. */
.bal{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;width:100%;text-align:left;
 background:none;border:0;padding:6px 0;cursor:pointer;min-height:var(--tap)}
/* the definition of the two ends of an axis, said once in the rail rather than
   only in a tooltip, because a tooltip is not reachable by a thumb. */
.stk-def{margin:10px 0 8px;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--dim)}
/* a glyph set beside the name of the thing it stands for, in a drill header */
.ad-ic{width:20px;height:20px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;vertical-align:-3px;margin-right:8px;
 opacity:.9}

/* a tier row that carries the percentage object rather than a bare figure */
.tierow.pill{display:grid;grid-template-columns:54px minmax(0,1fr) auto;gap:8px;
 align-items:center;min-height:var(--tap)}
.tierow.pill .tn{color:var(--ink);font-size:14px;min-width:0;overflow:hidden;
 text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap}

/* the protocol control on an address reading. It is the one action on that
   panel, so it is the one filled button, and the line under it says what the
   run will actually be rather than leaving a person to press and find out. */
/* .ad-prot, not .ad-act. The drills already had an .ad-act row and the design
   gate caught the second declaration on the first run, which is the whole
   reason that check exists: the .card collision cost a rail thirty two pixels
   and a turn to find. */
.ad-prot{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;margin-top:16px;padding-top:14px;
 border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.ad-prot .btn{align-self:flex-start}
.ad-prot-n{font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--dim)}

.stk-def b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500}
/* the accuracy block owns the bottom right, so the howto stops before it
   rather than running under it at the narrower desktop widths. */
.howto{position:absolute;bottom:14px;left:16px;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);
 pointer-events:none;line-height:1.75;white-space:pre-line;
 max-width:min(46ch,calc(100% - 320px))}
/* The corners. A circle inside a rectangle never reaches them, so a readout
   parked in one cannot land on a label however the box is resized. The right
   offset clears the compass column, which is the only other thing on that
   side. The old layout was doing this by accident in three corners and by
   mistake at the bottom centre, where the circle does reach and the wheel
   draws the word Heart. */
.tl{position:absolute;top:14px;right:152px;text-align:right;pointer-events:none;max-width:210px}
.tl b{display:block;font-size:17px;font-weight:600}
/* lower right, ruled, and it was already there. What moved is the label,
   which now sits to the left of the figure and carries the scale. */
/* THE FAR END OF THE DOCK, ruled 26 September, and in the dock's own cell. It
   was absolute in the stage's lower right with a large ring, 66 high against
   44 for every other readout, which is the size difference he named. It keeps
   its word, which is ruled, and takes the same box and ring as the rest. */
/* BACK ON THE STAGE, IN THE LOWER LEFT, round LR in TASKS.md: "lower
   left-hand corner, opposite of like the zoom keys on the horizontal line."
   The dock in the left rail is where it went at ED, and a first visit lands
   on that column shut, so he could not find it. It is the band word's
   capsule, the same glass on the same ground, #railtop above, and it is
   placed by zoom and not by the stage: the capsule is 50 high where a zoom
   circle is 44, so 9 from the foot puts its centre line on the centre line
   of the lowest zoom circle, 12 from the foot. On a phone zoom is a row in
   flow under the picture and the same 9 holds, because zoom keeps the same
   12 of foot there, below. */
.acc{position:absolute;left:12px;bottom:9px;z-index:6;display:flex;align-items:center;
 padding:2px;border-radius:999px;background:var(--fb-glass);border:1px solid var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 8px 22px var(--fb-shade)}
.acc-b{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;background:transparent;border:0;
 padding:0 0 0 14px;border-radius:999px;color:inherit;font:inherit;cursor:pointer;min-height:var(--tap)}
/* AND ACCURACY TAKES NO BOX THE OTHER CIRCLES DO NOT. GB in TASKS.md, his
   words: "The accuracy pill is bigger than everything else. Make all of
   those uniform." Its ring and value pill were already the orb size, DQ's to
   the pixel, measured on every lighting at five widths. What was bigger was
   the box round it. DQ and CQ are .kb and the dock cleared .kb's hover fill
   and gave its focus a round outline; accuracy is .acc-b, the button it was
   when it sat boxed in the stage's corner, and it kept that button's hover:
   a rounded slab 112 by 44 behind the word and the circle together, twice
   the width of anything on the row, run into the rail's edge. Flat went one
   further and filled the word's own box at rest, from its list of boxed
   controls. So the slab goes, the word loses its fill, and keyboard focus
   rings the circle as it rings DQ's, 3 out. It came back to the stage with
   the readout, round LR, and the capsule is its one edge there. */
.acc-b:hover{background:transparent}
body.flat .acc .acc-l{background:transparent}
.acc-b:focus-visible{outline:none}
.acc-b:focus-visible .cr{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:3px}
/* AND ITS VALUE PILL IS MADE OF THE GROUND, as the bar's are. The orb's pill
   mixes into var(--panel), which is the lighting's panel and not the stage's:
   under Snow the panel is paper and the stage stays the ruled #101010, so the
   pill came out pale lilac carrying the glass's pale ink, measured on the
   first cut of round LR. The bar's own pill, .fb-v, mixes into --fb-pillbg,
   which follows the ground, and so does this. Punch keeps its solid fill. */
body:not(.punch) #acc .cr .v{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 30%,var(--fb-pillbg))}
body:not(.tab-field) .acc{display:none}
.acc-n{font-family:var(--num);font-size:34px;font-weight:400;line-height:1;color:var(--gold)}
.acc-n small{font-size:16px;opacity:.6;margin-left:2px}
.acc-n em{font-style:normal;font-size:15px;opacity:.55;margin-left:7px}
.acc-l{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:flex-end;gap:2px;
 font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim);text-align:right}
.acc-l b{font-size:13px;font-weight:500;color:var(--mid);letter-spacing:.01em}
.acc-l em{font-style:normal;font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);
 font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.acc-w{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:5px;max-width:300px;margin-left:auto;line-height:1.55}
.acc-lean{opacity:.72;margin-top:3px}
/* the compass belongs to the field tab alone. in the original both rules
   declared the same properties, so it floated over every other tab. */
/* the compass shares the right edge with the accuracy block, which is 124
   tall. it stops above it rather than running behind it. */
/* the pole meter rides with the wheel, so it moved down a row with it when the
   legend took row 2. A child left on the old row would have sat in the
   legend's line at full height and squeezed it to nothing. */
.pol2{grid-column:3;grid-row:3;z-index:4;cursor:pointer;height:100%;
 display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:flex-end;background:none;border:0;
 padding:0 16px 0 8px;margin:0}
.pol2:hover svg{opacity:.85}
body:not(.tab-field) .pol2{display:none}
.pol2 svg{display:block;height:100%;width:104px}
.pol2-t{font-family:var(--num);font-size:10px;fill:var(--dim)}
/* the two ends of the cone are doors, so they say so on hover */
.ad-act{display:flex;gap:7px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:13px}
/* one question of the age test, with its two answers on the same line */
.ag-q{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;
 gap:8px 12px;padding:9px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);font-size:14px;color:var(--ink)}
.ag-q:last-of-type{border-bottom:0}
.ag-b{display:flex;gap:6px;flex:0 0 auto}
/* the opening. only on screen while there is nothing to read. */
.start{margin-bottom:16px}
.start[hidden]{display:none}
.st-lead{font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--mid);margin:0 0 11px}
.stbtn{display:block;width:100%;text-align:left;background:var(--sunk);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:11px 13px;margin-bottom:7px;cursor:pointer;
 font:inherit;color:var(--ink);min-height:var(--tap)}
.stbtn:hover{border-color:var(--gold);background:var(--panel-2)}
.stbtn b{display:block;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:3px}
.stbtn span{display:block;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--dim)}
.p2end{cursor:pointer}
.pol2:hover .p2end{opacity:1}
.pol2-c{font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;font-weight:500}

/* ---------- left rail : collapsible sections ---------- */
/* THE CORNER ON THE STAGE, NOT THE HEAD OF THE RAIL. Round IT moved it to the
   Field's upper right, under the Wheel, Frames and Dial switch. It lies on the
   picture, so it is made of that switch's glass and takes the same capsule,
   and 64 is the switch's own top of 8 plus its 50 of height plus the 6 the
   bar keeps between circles. The rail's rule and bottom edge went with the
   rail: over a canvas they drew a line across the wheel. */
/* THE PROFILE MENU, round OI. Glass like the Field's floats, rows at the 44px
   floor, the section's own seat colour on its icon, nothing else. */
.pmenu{position:fixed;z-index:60;min-width:224px;padding:6px;border-radius:14px;color:var(--ink);
 background:var(--fb-glass-on,var(--panel));border:1px solid var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 14px 36px rgba(0,0,0,.32)}
.pmenu[hidden]{display:none}
.pm-who{padding:8px 10px 6px;font-size:14px;font-weight:600}
.pm-who span{display:block;margin-top:2px;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;color:var(--dim);overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
.pm-it{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;width:100%;min-height:var(--tap);padding:0 10px;border:0;
 border-radius:10px;background:transparent;color:var(--ink);font:inherit;font-size:13.5px;text-align:left;cursor:pointer}
.pm-it:hover,.pm-it:focus-visible{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c,var(--accent)) 14%,transparent);outline:none}
.pm-it .ac-gl{display:inline-flex;width:22px;height:22px;color:var(--c,var(--accent))}
.pm-it .ac-gl svg{width:22px;height:22px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.pm-sep{height:1px;margin:4px 6px;background:var(--edge)}
.pm-all{font-weight:600}
/* the Sound effects row is a switch: the track fills with the accent when on */
.pm-sfx .ac-gl{color:var(--accent)}
.pm-sw{margin-left:auto;position:relative;width:34px;height:20px;border-radius:10px;
 background:rgba(128,128,128,.28);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--edge-2);transition:background var(--t-element,.2s) ease}
.pm-sw i{position:absolute;top:3px;left:3px;width:14px;height:14px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--ink);
 transition:transform var(--t-element,.2s) ease}
.pm-sfx[aria-checked="true"] .pm-sw{background:var(--accent)}
.pm-sfx[aria-checked="true"] .pm-sw i{transform:translateX(14px);background:var(--on-accent,#0B0D10)}
body.rm .pm-sw,body.rm .pm-sw i{transition:none}
/* ROUND OG: THE BAND PILL MOVES TO THE LOWER LEFT, RIGHT NEXT TO ACCURACY. His
   words: "Where it says compounding, I want the lower left hand side right
   next to accuracy." It was the upper right under the three way switch. It
   keeps accuracy's own bottom of 9 so their centre lines are one line, and
   sits 8 clear of accuracy's right edge: accuracy is left 12 and 132 across,
   so left 152. The wheel measures its free radius against it, OVERLAY. */
.railtop{position:absolute;bottom:9px;left:152px;z-index:6;display:flex;align-items:center;
 padding:2px;border-radius:999px;background:var(--fb-glass);border:1px solid var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 8px 22px var(--fb-shade)}
body:not(.tab-field) #railtop{display:none}
/* it is a button now, and a button that carries a definition owes the tap
   floor like every other control. */
.railtop .rt-t{font-size:14px;color:var(--ink);font-weight:500;
 background:transparent;border:0;padding:0;font-family:var(--sans);cursor:pointer;
 text-align:left;min-height:var(--tap);display:flex;align-items:center;
 border-bottom:1px dotted var(--edge-2)}
.railtop .rt-t:hover,.railtop .rt-t:focus-visible{color:var(--gold);
 border-bottom-color:var(--gold)}
/* the ring and the band word in one box, the dock's .kb box exactly: one
   edge, one radius, 44 high, the same inset padding. The word inside keeps
   its dotted underline, which is what says it opens. The edge is an inset
   shadow and not a border because the word is a button that owes the 44
   pixel tap floor, and a real border round it made the box 46, two taller
   than the cells it is meant to match. */
.rt-cell{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;
 padding:0 10px 0 4px;box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs)}
body.punch .rt-cell{box-shadow:none;background:var(--panel-2)}
/* inside the capsule the capsule is the edge, so the cell's own inset edge
   would be a second outline one pixel inside the first */
.railtop .rt-cell{box-shadow:none;background:transparent;border-radius:999px;padding-right:14px}
.s-band{font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--mid);margin:14px 0 0;max-width:62ch}
.s-band b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
/* the tier reads as a word you can open, not as a verdict printed at you */
/* THE ROOT ROW. Four buttons on one line, always. It was a wrapping flex row
   of auto width nowrap items, which is a row that fits until it does not, and
   the owner watched Witness drop to a line of its own. Four equal columns
   cannot wrap: the count is the grid and the width is whatever is left. */
.rootrow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:5px;margin-bottom:14px}
/* an arrow and nothing else, so the two of them are the same square and the
   pair stops changing width every time the stack moves. */
/* AN ICON ONLY BUTTON STILL OWES THE 44px FLOOR. Dropping the label took the
   width from 85 to 37, which the tap gate caught on all eight surfaces at
   once. Both arrows are square now and both clear it. */
.undobtn,.redobtn{min-width:var(--tap);justify-content:center;
 padding-left:9px;padding-right:9px}
.undobtn{color:var(--gold);border-color:var(--gold)}
.undobtn svg,.redobtn svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.tierbtn{background:none;border:0;padding:2px 0;font:inherit;color:inherit;
 cursor:pointer;text-align:left;min-height:var(--tap,44px);
 border-bottom:1px dotted var(--edge-2)}
.tierbtn:hover,.tierbtn:focus-visible{color:var(--gold);border-bottom-color:var(--gold)}
.railtop .rt-h{font-family:var(--num);font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;margin-left:auto}
.lsec{border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
.lsec:last-child{border-bottom:0}
.lsec-hd{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;width:100%;
 min-height:var(--tap);background:transparent;border:0;padding:14px 2px;cursor:pointer;color:var(--mid);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;text-align:left;letter-spacing:.02em}
.lsec-hd:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.lsec-hd i{width:8px;height:8px;border-right:1.6px solid currentColor;
 border-bottom:1.6px solid currentColor;transform:rotate(45deg);transition: transform var(--t-element) var(--ease-out);margin-right:4px}
.lsec.open .lsec-hd{color:var(--gold)}
.lsec.open .lsec-hd i{transform:rotate(-135deg)}
.lsec-bd{display:none;padding-bottom:16px}
.lsec.open .lsec-bd{display:block}
/* EVERY SECTION HEADER CARRIES ITS MARK. FV in TASKS.md, his words: "we're
   breaking a lot of our design rules on the left hand side ... everything
   should have meaning, everything should have an icon." The headers were the
   one row of the rails printing a bare word: every reading under them carried
   a glyph and the name over them did not. Each mark says what the section
   holds, never a decoration: three nested rings for the root, blueprint and
   archetype levels, the dial's own track for orientation and balance, the one
   ring the stack already uses for a child emotion, a grid for the matrix,
   Flow's own mark, a pulse for what is running, columns for the laws. Root
   Energetics is three rings overlapping, because it is where the systems
   overlap. Stroked in the header's own colour, so it opens gold with it. */
.lsec-hd>span{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;min-width:0}
.lsec-ic{width:16px;height:16px;flex:0 0 auto;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;
 stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
/* ROOT ENERGETICS IS SET APART, on both rails. FV: "I want a slightly
   different treatment for root energetics." Everything else on the rail is a
   reading of the field; this is the birth, the one layer nothing a person does
   can move. So it sits on a panel of its own: the accent at six percent under
   it and a hairline of the accent round it, the same two moves the dock's
   cells and the selected chip already make, and its header in ink rather than
   the rail's grey, with its mark in the accent. Nothing new is invented for
   it: a tint and an edge, the restraint the rest of the rail keeps. */
.lsec.re{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 6%,transparent);
 box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 24%,transparent);
 border-radius:var(--r-s);border-bottom:0;padding:0 12px;margin:0 -6px 12px}
.lsec.re .lsec-hd{color:var(--ink)}
.lsec.re.open .lsec-hd{color:var(--gold)}
.lsec.re .lsec-ic{color:var(--gold)}
body.punch .lsec.re{box-shadow:none;background:var(--panel-2)}
/* ---- ROOT ENERGETICS, READ ACROSS. ui/rootsum.js. ---- */
.rs-lead,.rs-none{font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--mid);margin:0 0 10px}
.rs-go{margin:0 0 6px}
.rs-note{font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--dim);margin:0 0 4px}
.rs-eye{margin:14px 0 4px}
.rs-ic{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.rs-a{padding:12px 0 10px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.rs-eye + .rs-a{border-top:0;padding-top:6px}
.rs-hd{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:11px}
/* the theme's mark in the ring every named thing on the rail sits in */
.rs-g{width:34px;height:34px;border-radius:50%;flex:0 0 auto;display:grid;place-items:center;
 color:var(--gold);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1.6px color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 70%,transparent)}
.rs-g .rs-ic{width:17px;height:17px}
.rs-light .rs-g{color:var(--mid);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1.2px var(--edge-2)}
.rs-t{display:flex;flex-direction:column;min-width:0;line-height:1.35}
.rs-t b{font-size:15px;color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
.rs-t em{font-style:normal;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--mid)}
.rs-strong .rs-t em{color:var(--gold)}
/* THE BAND OF FOUR. One cell per system, lit where that system lands on the
   theme, so how many agree is a shape and never a count against a total,
   which is his objection on file. A lit cell fills from its left edge in
   system order on the wheel's beat, and it fills again every time the section
   opens, because an animation runs from when its element is first drawn. */
.rs-band{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:4px;margin:10px 0 8px}
.rs-seg{position:relative;overflow:hidden;height:22px;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--edge);display:grid;place-items:center;color:var(--dim)}
.rs-seg .rs-ic{position:relative;width:13px;height:13px;opacity:.55}
.rs-seg.on{color:var(--ink);box-shadow:none}
.rs-seg.on .rs-ic{opacity:1}
.rs-seg.on::before{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;transform-origin:left center;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 40%,transparent);
 animation:rsFill var(--t-enter) var(--ease-enter) both;animation-delay:calc(var(--i) * var(--t-stagger))}
.rs-light .rs-seg.on::before{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 26%,transparent)}
@keyframes rsFill{from{transform:scaleX(0)}}
.rs-say{font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink);margin:0 0 8px}
.rs-chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:5px}
.rs-chip{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;font-size:12px;color:var(--mid);
 background:var(--sunk);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px 3px 6px;line-height:1.3}
.rs-chip .rs-ic{width:12px;height:12px;flex:0 0 auto;color:var(--gold)}
.rs-range{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px}
.rs-r{display:grid;grid-template-columns:16px minmax(0,1fr);column-gap:9px;padding:6px 0;
 border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.rs-r:first-child{border-top:0}
.rs-r .rs-ic{width:15px;height:15px;color:var(--gold);margin-top:2px;grid-row:1 / span 2}
.rs-rn{font-size:13px;color:var(--ink);font-weight:500}
.rs-rs{font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--mid);grid-column:2}
/* ---- THE LEFT COLUMN'S FOLD. EZ in TASKS.md: "I want to be able to close up
   that column." ui/ui.js, colFold. The control sits in the top right corner
   of the rail over Root energetics' header, which gives it the width a
   section's chevron would otherwise run into, so it costs the rail no row. */
#lpanel{position:relative}
.lfold{position:absolute;top:16px;right:8px;z-index:2;width:var(--tap);height:var(--tap);
 display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:0;border:0;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 background:transparent;color:var(--mid);cursor:pointer;
 transition:background-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.lfold:hover{background:var(--sunk);color:var(--ink)}
.lfold svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.lfold .lf-ch{transform-box:fill-box;transform-origin:center;transition:transform var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.lsec[data-sec=energetics] .lsec-hd{padding-right:52px}
/* shut, the column is the control and nothing else. It keeps its panel, so
   the way back open is a thing on a surface rather than a button floating in
   the gutter, and the chevron turns to point the way the column will go. */
body.lshut #lpanel>*:not(.lfold){display:none}
body.lshut #lpanel{padding:6px;align-items:center}
/* and the rail's tail goes with the rest. It is a pseudo element, 88vh of
   room to scroll a header to the top, so the rule above cannot reach it, and
   left standing it squeezed the one control in a shut column to eighteen
   pixels, measured, and gave an empty column a scroll. */
body.lshut #lpanel::after{display:none}
body.lshut .lfold{position:static;flex:0 0 auto}
body.lshut .lfold .lf-ch{transform:rotate(180deg)}
/* the stage takes the width. Settings has no columns at all, so it keeps its
   own single track and this never reaches it; and below 1180 the columns are
   stacked, where shut is simply the panel folded to its control. */
@media (min-width:1181px){
 body.lshut:not(.tab-settings) .mid{grid-template-columns:58px 1fr 336px}}
/* ---- AND THE RIGHT COLUMN'S. LO in TASKS.md: "Add a widget to the right menu
   to collapse it." ui/ui.js, railFold. The left fold mirrored, and its own
   class rather than lfold's, because every shut rule above is keyed on lfold
   and a shared class would have folded this control with the other column.
   It sits where lfold sits, the head of the rail at its right corner.

   PINNED, WHERE LFOLD SCROLLS AWAY. This rail moves itself: Selection opens
   and is brought to the top on every press of every surface, and measured on
   the Compass at 1600 the rail stood 1,843 pixels down with the control 1,705
   pixels above the screen. A fold that has to be scrolled back to is not
   there. So it is sticky at the rail's top with no height of its own, and
   every header on this rail keeps its right end free for it, so no chevron
   and no word is ever under it. Scrolled, it takes the panel's ground, so
   what passes beneath it does not show through the mark. ui/ui.js railFold
   says when the rail is scrolled. Pinned sixteen pixels up, into the panel's
   own padding, because that is where a pressed header is brought to: at top
   nought it stood half a row under the header beside it, measured. */
.rfold{position:sticky;top:-16px;align-self:flex-end;flex:0 0 auto;margin:0 -10px -44px 0;z-index:3;
 width:var(--tap);height:var(--tap);
 display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:0;border:0;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 background:transparent;color:var(--mid);cursor:pointer;
 transition:background-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
#rpanel.scrolled .rfold{background:var(--panel);box-shadow:0 0 0 1px var(--edge)}
.rfold:hover,#rpanel.scrolled .rfold:hover{background:var(--sunk);color:var(--ink)}
.rfold svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.rfold .lf-ch{transform-box:fill-box;transform-origin:center;transition:transform var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.lsec[data-rail=right] .lsec-hd{padding-right:52px}
/* important, because the Run a release row at the rail's foot carries its
   display inline and stood alone in the shut column without it, measured */
body.rshut #rpanel>*:not(.rfold){display:none!important}
body.rshut #rpanel{padding:6px;align-items:center}
body.rshut #rpanel::after{display:none}
body.rshut .rfold{position:static;margin:0;align-self:center}
/* stacked, the rail does not scroll: the page does, and sticky there would
   hold the control at the top of the phone's screen over whatever is under it */
@media (max-width:1180px){.rfold{position:relative;top:0}}
body.rshut .rfold .lf-ch{transform:rotate(180deg)}
/* the stage takes the width. Three surfaces have no right column in the row
   at all, and a shut one must not put a 58 pixel track back into it: Settings
   and the practitioner's page are one track, and the Story without its bank
   is ruled further down with a heavier selector that already wins. The both
   shut rule outweighs body.tab-settings .mid, so without the last line here
   Settings with both columns shut lays its one surface into the 58 pixel
   track: measured with the line taken out at 1600, 56 pixels wide, and 1578
   with it in. */
@media (min-width:1181px){
 body.rshut .mid{grid-template-columns:302px 1fr 58px}
 body.lshut.rshut .mid{grid-template-columns:58px 1fr 58px}
 body.rshut.tab-settings .mid,body.rshut.tab-prac .mid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
/* SPIRIT AND PSYCHE, the rail's two blocks, ruled 26 September. A block is
   one level above a section, so it is set in ink and a size up from the
   section headers under it, and it is not a control: nothing here opens or
   closes, which is why it carries no chevron and takes no pointer. */
.rblk{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;margin:2px 2px 0;padding:6px 0 2px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;font-weight:600;
 color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:.01em;line-height:1.4}
.lsec + .rblk{margin-top:14px;padding-top:16px}

/* ---------- orientation and balance: one dial, two designs ----------
   FJ in TASKS.md. axDial in ui/ui.js draws both from one spec: a symbol and
   a share at each end, a break at the centre where even sits, and a fill out
   of the break toward the heavier end, as long as the difference. The switch
   at the head of the section picks the design for both at once. */
.ax-pick{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;margin:0 0 12px}
.ax-pl{font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);margin-right:4px}
.ax-pb svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.ax-pb{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;
 min-height:var(--tap);min-width:var(--tap);padding:0 14px;border-radius:999px;
 border:1px solid var(--edge-2);background:none;color:var(--dim);font:inherit;font-size:12.5px;
 cursor:pointer;transition:background var(--t-micro,120ms) cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1),color var(--t-micro,120ms) cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)}
.ax-pb:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.ax-pb.on{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.polbar{margin-bottom:4px}
.ax{position:relative;width:100%}
/* the figure carries its own ground, the panel's pill, because the fill
   reaches under it exactly when the reading is strongest: measured when this
   was the orientation bar alone, ink at 11 pixels read 3.12 to 1 over the
   benign fill bare and 14.39 on the pill. The design gate holds it. */
.ax .lb{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;
 font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}
.ax .lb b{background:var(--panel);border-radius:999px;padding:2px 7px;line-height:1;font-weight:500}
/* ring, never fill, like every icon here. The heavier end takes its colour */
.ax-g{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;flex:0 0 auto;color:var(--dim);
 transition:color var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.ax-g svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.ax .lb.on .ax-g{color:var(--c)}
/* the pole names, on both dials now. Empty on an unread dial and the row
   keeps its height, so the layout does not move when a reading arrives */
.ax-nm{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;height:14px;margin-top:5px;
 font-size:11px;line-height:14px;color:var(--dim)}
.ax-nm .on{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
.ax-nm .ax-nl,.ax-nm .ax-nr{min-width:0}
.ax-bar{display:block}
/* A. BAR. The trough, as the orientation bar shipped */
.ax-tr{height:var(--tap);border-radius:var(--r-xs);position:relative;overflow:hidden;background:rgba(128,128,128,.14);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--edge)}
.ax-tr .fill{position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:0;opacity:.68;
 transition:width var(--t-enter) var(--ease-enter),background var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.ax-tr .fill{overflow:hidden}
/* the dials take the bars' light, on the bars' clock (--rate, written on the
   dock by rbRate), so the six readings and the two dials are one instrument */
.ax-tr .fill::before{content:"";position:absolute;inset:0;transform:translateX(-100%);
 background:linear-gradient(100deg,transparent 30%,rgba(255,255,255,.2) 50%,transparent 70%);
 animation:rbsweep calc(var(--t-breath) * 1.5 / var(--rate,1)) var(--ease-breath) infinite;animation-delay:calc(var(--t-stagger) * 8)}
body.quiet .ax-tr .fill::before,body.rm .ax-tr .fill::before{animation:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.ax-tr .fill::before{animation:none}}
.ax-tr .fill.rt{left:50%}
.ax-tr .fill.lt{right:50%}
/* the break sits over the fill, so even reads as a gap and not as a seam */
.ax-tr .mid{position:absolute;left:calc(50% - 1px);top:0;bottom:0;width:2px;background:var(--bg);z-index:2}
.ax-tr .lb{position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;padding:0 10px;z-index:3}
.ax-tr .lb.l{left:0}
.ax-tr .lb.r{right:0}
/* sex at birth, a reference point and not a reading, on balance only */
.ax-tr .ax-sx{position:absolute;top:50%;width:2px;height:13px;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);
 background:var(--dim);border-radius:1px;z-index:2}
/* B. ARC. A gauge: the half ring over the top, the break at twelve, the fill
   round the ring toward the heavier end, and a needle that lands on the lean
   on an overshoot, because a needle arriving is a landing */
.ax-arc{display:flex;align-items:flex-end;justify-content:space-between;gap:6px;height:60px}
.ax-arc .lb{flex-direction:column;gap:5px;padding-bottom:2px}
.ax-svg{flex:0 1 120px;width:120px;height:60px;overflow:visible}
.ax-trk{fill:none;stroke:var(--sunk);stroke-width:7;stroke-linecap:round}
.ax-svg .fill{fill:none;stroke-width:7;stroke-dasharray:0 100;opacity:.8;
 transition:stroke-dasharray var(--t-enter) var(--ease-enter),stroke var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.ax-svg .mid{stroke:var(--bg);stroke-width:2.5}
.ax-svg .ax-sx{stroke:var(--dim);stroke-width:2;stroke-linecap:round}
.ax-nd{transform-origin:60px 54px;transition:transform var(--t-context) var(--ease-land)}
.ax-nd line{stroke:var(--c,var(--ink));stroke-width:2;stroke-linecap:round}
.ax-nd circle{fill:var(--panel);stroke:var(--c,var(--ink));stroke-width:1.6}
.polnote{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin:6px 0 0;line-height:1.55}

/* ---------- icon grids ---------- */
/* THE ICON GRIDS, AND WHY THEY RAN OFF THE EDGE. An .ib is aspect-ratio 1 with
   min-height var(--tap), and an aspect ratio turns a height floor into a width
   floor, so every cell was at least 44 wide whatever the column said. Six of
   them plus five six pixel gaps is 294 inside a 264 content box, so the last
   two icons of each archetype row sat 29px past the right hand edge of the
   rail. Measured, then fixed, then measured again.

   The column count was the mistake, not the cell. A hard six cannot know how
   wide the rail is, and the rail width is a layout decision made three rules
   away from here. auto-fill with a floor of one tap target fits as many whole
   columns as there is room for and then stops, so the count follows the width
   at every density and every rail size and the overflow cannot come back. */
/* the rows sit four apart and the columns six. A row gap is height the rail
   pays for on every row, and it is spacing between targets rather than part
   of one, so it is the one to give back; see fitGrid for the rest. */
.g6{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(var(--tap),1fr));gap:4px 6px}
/* nineteen domains on five columns, so each root cluster is one contiguous
   row of one colour. Under high hue quantity, scattered colour delays
   attentional orienting; contiguous blocks of one colour search fastest. */
.gdom{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(var(--tap),1fr));gap:4px 6px}
/* THE CELL IS A RECTANGLE ONCE THE GRID HAS BEEN FITTED, AND ONLY THEN.
   .ib is a square, so in the rail's icon grids a column count is also a
   height: dropping the twelve archetypes from five columns to four to close
   the gap he named widened every cell from 48 to 61.5 and made it 61.5 tall
   with it, which put eighty one pixels of new scroll into the rail. fitGrid
   pins the row, to the tap floor since 26 September, and the aspect ratio has
   to give way to that or the button overflows its own row and sits on top of
   the next one, which is what the first cut measured: rows of 48 carrying
   buttons of 61.5.

   Gated on data-fit, which fitGrid writes, so a grid that has not been
   measured yet keeps the square it has always drawn rather than collapsing
   to its minimum for a frame. */
.g6[data-fit]>.ib,.gdom[data-fit]>.ib{aspect-ratio:auto;height:100%}
/* THE ARCHETYPE ICONS SHRINK AND THE GRID LOSES A ROW. EZ in TASKS.md: "the
   Jung icons are taking up too much space, so shrink them the icon size down
   so we can collapse the row size down one." Twelve in each grid sat four by
   three, 61.5 wide cells in a 264 rail.

   The tap floor is 44 and a measured floor, so the drawn tile shrinks and the
   target does not. The gaps go to nought, which is what lets fitGrid find six
   44 wide columns in 264, and the space between tiles is drawn inside each
   target instead: the ground is clipped to the content box, three pixels in
   from a border that stays at the target's edge. Twelve read six by two, each
   tile 35 across with its mark at 16, and selection is still a ring, now one
   that stands off the tile rather than sitting on its edge. The border width
   is restated because Punch and Lumen take it to nought on every .ib, and at
   nought the ground would run to the edge and the tiles would touch. */
#ar1,#ar2{gap:0}
#ar1>.ib,#ar2>.ib{padding:3px;background-clip:content-box;border-width:1.5px}
.ib{aspect-ratio:1;min-height:var(--tap);background:var(--sunk);border:1.5px solid transparent;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 cursor:pointer;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:0;
 transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);position:relative}
/* the icon takes the seat colour and keeps generous inner spacing. narrow
   inner spacing is what actually interferes with icon recognition. */
/* AND IT RESTS AT FULL STRENGTH. It rested at opacity .78 and went to 1 under
   a pointer, which mixed every stroke a fifth of the way into its own dark
   tile until somebody hovered it, and that was most of what the owner called
   dull. The proto/rooticons board measured it on Dark: the nineteen domain
   icons drew at an effective OKLCH chroma of 0.103 against 0.125 in the
   tokens they were given. Ruled off on 26 September, option B. Hover keeps
   its tile and border change, which is the affordance; the icon stops being
   the thing that changes. */
.ib svg{width:46%;height:46%;stroke:var(--c,var(--mid));stroke-width:1.7;fill:none;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.ib:hover{background:var(--panel-2);border-color:var(--edge-2)}
/* SELECTION IS A RING. recognition speed matters most before selection, so a
   fill spends legibility on the one option already found. */
.ib[aria-pressed=true]{border-color:var(--gold);background:var(--panel-2);
 box-shadow:0 0 0 2px rgba(223,204,126,.28)}
.ib[aria-pressed=true] svg{opacity:1;stroke-width:2}
.ib[data-r="2"]{border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 52%,transparent)}
.ib[data-r="2"] svg{opacity:1}
.ib .rt{position:absolute;top:calc(50% - 21px);right:calc(50% - 21px);z-index:2;width:5px;height:5px;border-radius:50%}
/* THE ROOT BUTTON, and the three things it has to say. A root is off, or it is
   lit because the blueprint domain you have selected lives in it, or it is
   added because you clicked it. The old control only had off and added, and it
   said added with a border tint and a 44 percent glow, so the owner had a
   profile loaded, four roots that all looked identical, and no way to see
   which one his own selection sat in. That is the bug: the row was a filter
   pretending to be a readout.

   Added is a flat fill, on the owner's ruling. The ring-not-fill rule governs
   the icon grids, where a fill would destroy the glyph; this is a text chip
   and he asked for flat colour, so it is flat colour. The ink on top is a
   fixed dark rather than var(--bg), because all four root colours are light
   and --bg goes light in snow, which would have printed light on light. */
.rootb{min-height:var(--tap);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
 padding:8px 2px 6px;border-radius:var(--r-xs);border:1.5px solid var(--edge);
 background:transparent;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:400;
 line-height:1.15;text-align:center;cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);
 color:var(--rc,var(--mid))}
.rootb:hover{border-color:var(--rc,var(--edge-2))}
/* lit: your selection is in this root. a wash, not a fill, because you did not
   choose it and a control that looks chosen invites you to unchoose it. */
.rootb[data-lit="1"]{border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--rc) 46%,transparent);
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--rc) 13%,transparent);color:var(--rc)}
/* added: you clicked it. flat. */
.rootb[aria-pressed=true]{background:var(--rc);border-color:var(--rc);color:#14151C;
 font-weight:500}
/* AND ON PAPER THE FOUR ARE NOT LIGHT ANY MORE. Snow and Glass white draw the
   roots from their own deepened palette, so the fixed dark ink above would
   sit dark on dark. The ground's own paper was tried first and read 4.35 on
   Snow and 4.18 on Glass white against the 4.5 text floor, because paper is
   not white; white is, and it clears it. Fixed for the same reason the dark
   ink is fixed. */
body.snow .rootb[aria-pressed=true],body.glasswhite .rootb[aria-pressed=true]{color:#FFFFFF}
/* THE MARK SITS ABOVE THE NAME, which is why .rootb is a column. The chip
   was one line of text; stacked, it measures 51.6 tall against 44, which is
   seven and a half pixels across one row, paid back seven times over by the
   icon grids below it in the same round. The mark takes currentColor, so off, washed and added each carry it
   without a rule of their own, and it stays an open stroke on every state,
   filled chip or not. */
.rootb svg{width:19px;height:19px;flex:0 0 auto;stroke:currentColor;fill:none;
 stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.rootb[aria-pressed=true] svg{stroke-width:1.9}
.rootb span{display:block;line-height:1.1}
.rootlegend{font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);
 margin:-9px 0 14px;line-height:1.5}
.rootb[aria-pressed=true]{font-weight:600}
.cap{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--gold);margin-top:9px;text-align:center;min-height:2.6em;line-height:1.5}
.cap b{font-weight:600}

/* ---------- number fields ---------- */
.nf{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;margin-bottom:7px;min-height:var(--tap)}
.nf label{flex:1 1 auto;font-size:14px;color:var(--mid);overflow:hidden;
 text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap;font-weight:400}
.nf i{width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:50%;flex:0 0 auto}
.nf i.nf-g{width:16px;height:16px;border-radius:0;display:grid;place-items:center}
.nf i.nf-g svg{width:16px;height:16px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.nf input{width:54px;min-height:var(--tap);flex:0 0 auto;background:rgba(128,128,128,.14);border:0;box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--num);font-size:13px;font-weight:500;
 padding:5px 4px;text-align:center;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* The drag track reads as a 6px bar and was a 6px target. The bar stays 6px;
   the box grows to the tap minimum and paints only its content box, so the
   finger gets 44px and the eye still gets the thin line. */
.nf .tr{flex:0 0 58px;height:6px;padding:calc((var(--tap) - 6px) / 2) 0;
 background:rgba(128,128,128,.22);background-clip:content-box;border-radius:3px;
 cursor:ew-resize;position:relative;box-sizing:content-box}
.nf .tr:hover{outline:1px solid var(--gold);outline-offset:2px;border-radius:3px}
.nf .tr b{display:block;height:100%;border-radius:3px;transition: width var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out)}
.nf.oppf label{opacity:.74;font-size:13px;padding-left:4px}
.nf.oppf{margin-bottom:12px;margin-top:-2px}
.bulk{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:center;margin-bottom:11px}
.bulk label{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--mid);flex:0 0 58px}
.bulk input[type=range]{flex:1 1 auto;-webkit-appearance:none;appearance:none;height:var(--tap);
 background:transparent;cursor:grab}
.bulk input[type=range]::-webkit-slider-runnable-track{height:6px;background:rgba(128,128,128,.22);border-radius:3px}
.bulk input[type=range]::-moz-range-track{height:6px;background:rgba(128,128,128,.22);border-radius:3px}
.bulk input[type=range]::-webkit-slider-thumb{-webkit-appearance:none;width:22px;height:22px;
 border-radius:50%;background:var(--panel);margin-top:-8px;border:2px solid var(--gold);box-sizing:border-box}
.bulk input[type=range]::-moz-range-thumb{width:22px;height:22px;border-radius:50%;
 background:var(--panel);border:2px solid var(--gold);box-sizing:border-box}

/* ---------- the matrix ---------- */
.mx{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(19,1fr);gap:1.5px;margin-top:4px}
.mx button{aspect-ratio:1;border:0;border-radius:2px;padding:0;cursor:pointer;
 background:var(--sunk);transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.mx button:hover{outline:1px solid var(--gold);outline-offset:0}
.mx button[data-sel="1"]{box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--gold)}
.mxk{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.65;margin-top:10px}
.mxk b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}

/* ---------- spiritual states ---------- */
.sp-hd{margin:14px 0 6px;padding-bottom:4px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--gold)}
/* THE VALUE HOLDS ITS LINE AND THE NOTE TAKES WHAT IS LEFT. The value was the
   flexible column and the note sized to its own text, so a note of any length
   squeezed the value: with the Eastern rows saying what they do, Water Dog
   broke over two lines under its own glyph. A value is a name and a name does
   not wrap; a note is a phrase and can. */
.sp-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:62px auto minmax(0,1fr);gap:10px;align-items:center;
 min-height:var(--tap);
 font-size:13.5px;color:var(--mid);padding:6px 5px;width:100%;background:transparent;border:0;
 text-align:left;cursor:pointer;border-radius:var(--r-xs);font-family:var(--sans)}
.sp-row:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.sp-row.static{cursor:default}.sp-row.static:hover{background:transparent}
.sp-k{font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim)}
.sp-v{color:var(--ink);white-space:nowrap}
.sp-x{color:var(--mid);font-size:12.5px;text-align:right}
.sp-g{font-style:normal;font-size:16px;margin-right:7px;opacity:.95;color:var(--gold)}
.sp-hd{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px}
.sp-hi,.sp-gi{width:14px;height:14px;flex:0 0 auto;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;
 stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.sp-gi{width:16px;height:16px;margin-right:7px;vertical-align:-3px;color:var(--gold)}
.sp-none{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.7;padding:10px 0}

/* ---------- right rail ---------- */
.rcard{border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:14px 16px;margin-bottom:9px;position:relative;
 overflow:hidden;cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);background:var(--panel-2)}
.rcard:hover{transform:translateX(3px);outline:1px solid var(--edge-2)}
.rcard .bar{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;bottom:0;opacity:.17}
.rcard .tier{font-size:11px;font-weight:600;opacity:.85;position:relative}
.rcard .nm{font-size:19px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;position:relative;margin:4px 0 4px;letter-spacing:0}
.rcard .sub{font-size:13px;opacity:.72;position:relative;margin:0;color:var(--mid);font-weight:300}
.rcard .w{position:absolute;right:14px;top:12px}
.rnone{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--dim);padding:10px 0}
.person{border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:15px 17px;background:var(--panel-2);
 border-left:3px solid var(--gold)}
.person h3{margin:0;font-size:20px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0}
.prole{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:3px}
.psay{font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--mid);margin:11px 0 0;font-style:italic;font-weight:300}
.tierow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:72px 1fr auto;gap:10px;align-items:baseline;
 font-size:13.5px;color:var(--mid);padding:4px 0}
.tk{font-size:11px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim)}
.tn{color:var(--ink)}
.tp{color:var(--gold);font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* ---------- the record ---------- */
.rec-strip{display:flex;align-items:flex-end;gap:3px;height:74px;margin:14px 0 6px;
 padding:0 2px;overflow-x:auto;scrollbar-width:none}
.rec-strip::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}
.rec-p{flex:0 0 14px;height:100%;background:none;border:0;padding:0;cursor:pointer;position:relative;
 display:flex;align-items:flex-end;border-radius:3px}
.rec-p:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.rec-pb{display:block;width:100%;background:var(--c);border-radius:3px;opacity:.42;min-height:6px}
.rec-p.on .rec-pb{opacity:1}
.rec-p{margin-top:14px}
.rec-pm{position:absolute;top:-14px;left:0;right:0;font-family:var(--num);font-size:9px;
 color:var(--gold);text-align:center;font-weight:500}
.rec-when{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;font-size:11.5px;
 color:var(--dim);font-family:var(--num);margin-bottom:14px}
.rec-gap{color:var(--mid)}
.rec-lead{padding:14px 16px;border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--sunk);margin-bottom:16px}
/* the next threshold, on this person's own scale */
.rec-next{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:baseline;gap:6px 11px;margin-top:12px;
 padding-top:11px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.rec-next b{font-size:15px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)}
.rec-nl{font-family:var(--num);font-size:13px;color:var(--gold)}
.rec-ns{flex:1 0 100%;font-size:12px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.5}
.rec-big{font-family:var(--num);font-size:34px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.1;color:var(--gold);
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.rec-hd,.rec-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.5fr) 64px 64px 72px;gap:8px;
 align-items:baseline;padding:6px 6px;font-size:13px}
.rec-hd{font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.06em;color:var(--dim);border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
.rec-row{border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
.rec-row:last-of-type{border-bottom:0}
.rec-k{color:var(--mid);overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap}
.rec-a,.rec-b,.rec-d{font-family:var(--num);text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.rec-a{color:var(--dim)} .rec-b{color:var(--ink)} .rec-d{color:var(--gold)}
.rec-d.lvl{color:var(--dim)}

/* ---------- the knowledge base ---------- */
/* ============================================================
   THE CODEX IS A DECK OF ROWS, AND THE ROW IS FOUR THINGS.

   Ruled: "what we need to see is just these atomised: the icon, the percent
   it is impacting you, and the word, and the chakra that is associated with
   it." Four things, and nothing else on the line.

   This replaced a card. The card was 236 by 150 and carried a 19 pixel glyph,
   which is nine tenths of one percent of its own area, plus three clamped
   lines of subtext that were 78 percent of every character on the page and
   repeated verbatim down a column: nine saboteur cards said "Positive
   Intelligence, 3 addresses" and twenty one law cards spent their third line
   restating their second line as a sentence.

   Measured at 1600 and at 390, counted in a screenshot: twelve entries above
   the fold became thirty nine, and one became six. The scroll of the largest
   deck fell from 18,043 pixels on a phone to 6,605.
   ============================================================ */
.kb-top{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;padding:4px 0 10px}
/* eyebrow, deck name and what the percent means, on one baseline. Three
   stacked blocks cost 90 pixels to say what one line says. */
.kb-hdr{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.kb-h{margin:0;font-size:22px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:var(--ink)}
.kb-scale{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.5}
.kb-p{margin:0;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--mid);max-width:76ch}
.kb-bar{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.kb-q{flex:1 1 240px;background:var(--sunk);border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--ink);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:0 13px;min-height:var(--tap);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px}
.kb-q:focus{border-color:var(--gold)}
.kb-found{font-size:12px;color:var(--dim);font-family:var(--num)}
/* in order, or by weight. Two states, one control, and the default is the
   deck's own order because a codex that reorders itself per person has no
   stable answer to "where is Fear". */
.kb-sw{display:inline-flex;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 overflow:hidden;flex:0 0 auto}
.kb-swb{background:transparent;border:0;color:var(--dim);font-family:var(--sans);
 font-size:12.5px;padding:0 13px;min-height:var(--tap);cursor:pointer}
.kb-swb.on{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--accent);font-weight:500}
/* THE DECK STRIP SCROLLS SIDEWAYS RATHER THAN WRAPPING. Eleven chips wrapped
   to two rows at 1600 and three on a phone, which is 96 and 144 pixels of
   every screen spent on navigation a thumb can reach by swiping. */
.kb-strip{display:flex;gap:5px;overflow-x:auto;overflow-y:hidden;
 padding-bottom:2px;scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:var(--edge-2) transparent}
.kb-strip::-webkit-scrollbar{height:5px}
.kb-strip::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb{background:var(--edge-2);border-radius:3px}
.kb-t{background:var(--sunk);border:0;color:var(--mid);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 padding:0 13px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;cursor:pointer;
 min-height:var(--tap);display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;flex:0 0 auto;
 white-space:nowrap}
.kb-t b{font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;color:var(--dim)}
.kb-t.on{background:transparent;color:var(--accent);font-weight:500}
.kb-t.on b{color:var(--accent)}
/* the glossary answers above the rows and answers once. It is not a deck. */
.kb-gloss{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;margin:2px 0 14px;
 padding:14px 16px;border-left:2px solid var(--accent);background:var(--panel)}
.kb-g{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:3px}
.kb-gt{font-size:14px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}
.kb-gd{margin:0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--mid);max-width:74ch}
/* three columns at 1600, two at 900, one on a phone. The gap is horizontal
   only: the hairline under each row is the vertical rhythm. */
.kb-rg{display:grid;gap:0 24px;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);flex:1 1 auto}
.kb-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;width:100%;min-height:56px;
 padding:0 2px;background:transparent;border:0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);
 text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);color:var(--mid);cursor:pointer;
 transition:background var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.kb-row:hover{background:var(--panel)}
.kb-row:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:-2px}
.kb-rt{min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1px}
.kb-rn{font-size:15px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.2;
 white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
.kb-rs{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.2;
 white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
/* THE FIGURE TAKES THE INK AND NOT THE SEAT. Root is 4.17 to 1 against the
   panel, which is what this row stands on under the pointer, and a number
   needs 4.5. The ring already carries the seat colour, so a coloured figure
   is one channel used twice, and on the moral integrity deck a high figure in
   a warm colour would read as an alarm on a law that is being kept. */
.kb-rv{margin-left:auto;font-family:var(--num);font-size:14px;font-weight:500;
 color:var(--ink);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;flex:0 0 auto}
/* a zero is a reading and prints. It is not a finding and does not shout. */
.kb-rv.z{color:var(--dim);font-weight:400}
/* not scored is not zero, and never a figure off a default. */
.kb-rv.off{color:var(--dim);font-weight:400}
/* the axis break on the universal laws, so the flow he asked for is visible
   rather than implied. Drawn only in the deck's own order. */
.kb-sec{grid-column:1/-1;font-size:11px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.06em;
 color:var(--dim);padding:20px 0 7px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge-2)}
.kb-sec:first-child{padding-top:2px}
/* ---------- settings, in the centre ---------- */
.set-wrap{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px;width:100%;
 max-width:1040px;margin:0 auto;padding:4px 0 24px}
.set-hd{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px}
/* the centre has width a sheet never did, so the sections sit beside each
   other rather than in one long scroll. */
.set-grid{display:grid;gap:14px;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(278px,1fr));
 align-items:start}
.set-sec{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:9px;padding:17px 19px;
 border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--panel)}
body.punch .set-sec{background:var(--panel-2);border-color:transparent}
/* ============================================================
   THE SUMMARY. Plate, reading with a home, output row, information panel.
   ============================================================ */
.s-plate{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:18px;align-items:center;
 padding:4px 0 18px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);margin-bottom:20px}
.s-pname{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:clamp(34px,5vw,58px);font-weight:600;
 line-height:1.02;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--ink)}
.s-pwho{font-size:14px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:6px}
.s-pl-r{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:14px}
.s-pband{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px}
.s-pband b{font-size:19px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)}
.s-pband em{font-style:normal;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim)}
.s-ptoward{grid-column:1/-1;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--mid);
 max-width:68ch;margin-top:4px}
.s-ptoward .pm-eye{display:block;margin-bottom:5px}
/* the reading gets a home. It was text on the page ground. */
.s-readbox{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:22px 24px;margin-bottom:18px}
.s-readbox>*:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
.s-cols{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.35fr) minmax(300px,.85fr);
 gap:22px;align-items:start}
.s-main{min-width:0}
.s-side{min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px}
/* THE SIDE COLUMN WAS FIVE SECTIONS IN ONE SCROLL. Glance, structures,
   lenses, spiritual, numerology, stacked, and it ran far taller than the
   reading beside it: measured on James at 1600 with both rails shut, the
   side column ran 2649px against the reading's 971. "Organised, symmetrical,
   clean" and "read this easily" both point at the same fix once there is
   width to spend on it: split the five into two columns that stand at
   closer to the same height, rather than one column that keeps going. Split
   the same way, the side column measures 1954px against the same 971,
   nearer the reading's own height and considerably less scrolling to reach
   the numerology at the foot of it. Below the point where there is real
   width to give it, it stays one column and reads top to bottom exactly as
   it always did; nothing about the narrow or phone reading changes. */
.s-side-a,.s-side-b{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;min-width:0}
@media (min-width:1500px){
 .s-cols{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.15fr) minmax(460px,1fr);gap:28px}
 .s-side{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:0 24px;align-items:start}
}
/* the output row. Three cards, the block this page never had. */
.s-outrow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(196px,1fr));
 gap:12px;margin-bottom:18px}
.s-out{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;padding:15px 16px;
 border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--panel)}
.s-out-n{font-size:16px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.25}
.s-out-s{font-size:13px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.5}
.s-oact{margin-top:6px;align-self:flex-start}
body.punch .s-readbox,body.punch .s-out{background:var(--panel-2);border-color:transparent}
@media (max-width:1100px){.s-cols{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
/* THE COMPASS CONTROLS ARE THE GLASS BAR'S CIRCLES NOW, round OJ, and the text
   pills that stood here, .cn-b and its mark .cn-ic, are gone with the words
   they carried. The switches keep the name .cn-b on the circle for the gates
   that find them by it, and the look is .fb-b's, in the glass bar's sheet,
   with the compass's own rules beside the compass further down. */
/* ============================================================
   THE SUMMARY BY MEANING. Questions in the order a person asks them, then
   where it comes from. One grid: every row is two equal halves or three equal
   thirds, and the two cards in a row stand at the same height.
   ============================================================ */
.sg-z{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;margin:0 0 20px;min-width:0}
.sg-zh{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;margin:0 0 12px;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;
 letter-spacing:0;color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--sans);line-height:1.3}
.sg-z>.sg-zh{margin:0}
.sg-ic{width:22px;height:22px;flex:0 0 auto;fill:none;stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.6;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.sg-two{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:20px;align-items:stretch;margin:0 0 20px}
.sg-card{min-width:0;padding:20px 22px;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);
 background:var(--panel);display:flex;flex-direction:column}
.sg-card>.pm-eye:first-child{margin-bottom:10px}
.sg-gap{margin-top:18px}
.sg-gap>.pm-eye{margin-bottom:6px}
.sg-flat{background:none;border:0;padding:0;margin:0;border-radius:0}
.sg-card .s-told{margin-bottom:16px}
.sg-card .s-axes{border:0;padding:0;margin:14px 0 0;background:none}
.sg-card .s-axes .s-p{font-size:15px;line-height:1.65}
/* the plate */
.sg-who .s-plate{margin-bottom:0;padding:0 0 4px;border-bottom:0}
.sg-who .s-pname{font-size:clamp(34px,4.2vw,50px)}
.sg-who .sg-plain{grid-template-columns:1fr}
/* the first drivers: a cascade, one numbered ring after another on a line */
.sg-c-k{font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim)}
.sg-c-g{flex:0 0 auto;width:44px;height:44px;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;
 justify-content:center;border:1.5px solid var(--rc,var(--accent));color:var(--rc,var(--accent))}
.sg-c-g svg{width:22px;height:22px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.sg-c-g .s-num-g{font-size:20px;color:var(--rc,var(--accent))}
.sg-sub{margin:18px 0 6px}
.sg-st{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px}
.sg-st+.sg-st{margin-top:6px}
.sg-st-h{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;min-height:30px;font-size:15px;color:var(--ink)}
.sg-st-n{width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid var(--accent);color:var(--accent);
 display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-family:var(--num);font-size:14px}
.sg-st-b{margin-left:13px;padding-left:23px;border-left:1.5px solid var(--edge)}
.sg-st-b>.sg-note{margin:2px 0 4px}
.sg-down{align-self:flex-start;margin:6px 0 0 3px;display:inline-flex}
.sg-down .sg-ic{width:22px;height:22px;stroke:var(--dim)}
.sg-drive>.sg-fold{margin-top:auto}
.sg-drive>.sg-down+.sg-fold{margin-top:14px}
.sg-fold-b .sg-m:first-child{padding-top:6px}
.sg-init{font-family:var(--num);font-size:19px;color:var(--accent);line-height:1}
.sg-note{margin:0 0 8px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--dim)}
.sg-meets{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0}
.sg-m{display:grid;grid-template-columns:44px minmax(0,1fr) auto;gap:14px;align-items:center;
 padding:10px 0;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.sg-m:first-child{border-top:0;padding-top:6px}
.sg-m-t{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1px;min-width:0}
.sg-m-t b{font-size:16px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.25}
.sg-m-t p{margin:1px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--mid)}
.sg-marks{margin:0 0 20px;padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);
 background:var(--panel)}
/* THE BOX IS ROUND THE STRIP AND NEVER ROUND A GLYPH. Ruled: the little boxes
   are out and the icons are in. One edge holds the seven, and each mark stands
   on its own. */
.sg-marks .s-chips{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(104px,1fr));gap:4px;margin-top:0}
.sg-marks .s-chip{min-height:72px;justify-content:center;border:0}
/* the nine, three by three */
.sg-nine{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:8px}
.sg-n{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;text-align:center;gap:6px;
 padding:10px 6px 9px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s)}
.sg-n.top{border-color:var(--accent)}
.sg-n-t{display:flex;flex-direction:column;min-width:0;align-items:center}
.sg-n-t b{font-size:14px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)}
.sg-n-t em{font-style:normal;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.3}
.sg-card .s-glance{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(88px,1fr));gap:4px;
 padding:0;border:0;margin:0}
.sg-card .s-gl{flex-direction:column;border-radius:var(--r-s);min-height:44px;width:100%;
 padding:6px 2px;text-align:center;gap:4px;align-items:center}
.sg-card .s-gl-k{white-space:normal;font-size:12px;line-height:1.25}
/* the output row, three equal cards */
.sg-todo .s-outrow{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));margin:0;gap:20px}
.sg-todo .s-out{padding:18px 20px}
/* the day slot. It is empty until the daily summary fills it, and an empty
   slot takes no room. */
.sg-day{margin:0 0 20px}
.sg-day:empty{display:none}
.sum-wrap.sg-slots .sg-day:empty{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
 min-height:96px;border:1.5px dashed var(--accent);border-radius:var(--r-s);color:var(--accent);
 font-size:13px;font-weight:600}
.sum-wrap.sg-slots .sg-day:empty::before{content:"slot: " attr(data-slot) " summary, under the story, above what to do"}
/* folds, a nested group the person opens. The row carries a chevron. */
.sg-folds{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:12px;align-items:start}
.sg-fold{border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);min-width:0;margin-top:14px}
.sg-folds .sg-fold{margin-top:0;background:var(--panel)}
.sg-card>.sg-fold:nth-last-of-type(1){margin-bottom:0}
.sg-card>.sg-fold:first-of-type{margin-top:auto}
.sg-fold>summary{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;
 min-height:48px;padding:0 16px;cursor:pointer;list-style:none;font-size:15px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink)}
.sg-fold>summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}
.sg-chev{width:20px;height:20px;fill:none;stroke:var(--dim);stroke-width:1.8;stroke-linecap:round;
 stroke-linejoin:round;transition:transform .15s}
.sg-fold[open] .sg-chev{transform:rotate(180deg)}
.sg-fold-b{padding:4px 16px 16px}
.sg-fold-b .s-numer,.sg-fold-b .s-spirit,.sg-fold-b .s-ig{margin:0;padding:0;border:0}
.sum-start{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:12px;max-width:none;margin-top:0}
.sg-start .sum-start{grid-template-columns:1fr}
.sg-start .sum-hero{margin-bottom:18px}
.sg-folds.sg-one{grid-template-columns:1fr}
.sum-start>.pm-eye,.sum-start>.st-lead{grid-column:1/-1;margin:0}
@media (max-width:1100px){
 .sg-two,.sg-folds{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
@media (max-width:700px){
 /* ON A PHONE THE WAY OUT COMES RIGHT AFTER WHAT WAS SAID. Measured at 390:
    the first thing a person could do sat 4045px down, three times further than
    before the rebuild. The document keeps its one order, and this lifts the
    day slot and the output row to follow the story, the drivers and the birth
    marks, with the detail cards after them. */
 .sum-wrap{display:flex;flex-direction:column}
 .sum-wrap>.sg-lead{order:1}
 .sum-wrap>.sg-marks{order:2}
 .sum-wrap>.sg-day{order:3}
 .sum-wrap>.sg-todo{order:4}
 .sum-wrap>.sg-two:not(.sg-lead){order:5}
 .sum-wrap>.sg-folds{order:6}
 .sg-who .s-plate{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:10px}
 .sg-who .s-pl-r{justify-content:flex-start}
 .sg-todo .s-outrow{grid-template-columns:1fr}
 .sg-card{padding:16px 16px}
 .sg-m{grid-template-columns:44px minmax(0,1fr)}
 .sg-m .sg-band{grid-column:2}
 .sg-marks .s-chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center}
 .sg-marks .s-chip{flex:0 0 calc(33.333% - 6px)}
 .sg-nine{gap:6px}
 .sg-n-t em{font-size:12px}
 .sg-card .s-glance{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))}}
/* the compass controls. Four switches on the card, each one a reading the
   figure can carry rather than a decoration it can wear. */
/* the row version of .cone-ctl is gone with the row. The controls sit on the
   figure now, declared once, further down with the rest of the compass. */
.cn-b{background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--mid);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:7px 13px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;
 cursor:pointer;min-height:var(--tap);display:inline-flex;align-items:center;
 transition:background .12s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1), color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out), border-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.cn-b:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.cn-b.on{border-color:var(--accent);color:var(--accent);font-weight:500}
/* the switch's mark before its word, LO, CN_IC in ui/cone.js. Its own class,
   because the seat buttons in the same class carry a ring in their seat's
   colour that a rule on every svg in .cn-b would have turned to ink */
.cn-ic{width:15px;height:15px;flex:0 0 auto;margin:0 7px 0 -2px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;
 stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.cn-sp{flex:1 1 auto;min-width:6px}
body.punch .cn-b{border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
/* the Story's segmented choices, the sort and the pace. The release settings
   they were first written for went into the Story's third column, round IJ,
   and the rows, the list and the foot that held them went with it.
   min-width as well as min-height. A one character label gave a 29px wide
   target, which passes a height floor and fails a tap floor, and the gate is
   right that 44 is both dimensions. */
.st-rb{background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--mid);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:6px 11px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;
 cursor:pointer;min-height:var(--tap);min-width:var(--tap);
 display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center}
.st-rb:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.st-rb.on{border-color:var(--accent);color:var(--accent);font-weight:500}
body.punch .st-rb{border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
/* the bank and the vault are outlined pills everywhere else, and Punch takes
   the outline off, which left them as two words floating in the header */
body.punch .st-ico{border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
.kb-deck{margin-top:22px;padding:18px 20px;border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--panel-2);
 display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:9px;align-items:flex-start}
body.punch .kb-q{border-color:transparent}
body.punch .kb-sw{border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
body.punch .kb-gloss{background:var(--panel-2)}
/* two columns before the name starts truncating, one on a phone. The measured
   break is where a 15px name at 22 characters stops fitting beside a 44 ring
   and a right aligned figure. */
@media (max-width:1100px){.kb-rg{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}}
@media (max-width:720px){.kb-rg{grid-template-columns:1fr}}

/* ---------- games ---------- */
/* the games header keeps the stacked block and the wrapping chip row the
   codex used to have. Two rules, owned by the surface that wants them. */
.gm-hd{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px}
.gm-tabs{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:5px}
.gm-intro,.gm-done,.gm-soon,.gm-open,.gm-say{padding:16px 18px;border-radius:var(--r-s);
 background:var(--panel-2);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:9px;align-items:flex-start;margin:14px 0}
.gm-soon{background:var(--sunk)}
.gm-p{margin:0;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--mid);max-width:74ch}
.gm-bar{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:14px;align-items:flex-end;justify-content:space-between;
 padding:12px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);margin-bottom:14px}
.gm-chan{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px}
.gm-chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px}
.gm-chip{background:var(--sunk);border:0;color:var(--mid);border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:6px 11px;
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;cursor:pointer;min-height:34px}
.gm-chip.on{background:var(--gold);color:var(--on-accent)}
.gm-meter{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:flex-end;gap:3px}
.gm-clock{font-family:var(--num);font-size:26px;font-weight:400;color:var(--ink);
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;line-height:1}
.gm-of{font-size:12px;color:var(--dim);font-family:var(--num)}
.gm-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(112px,1fr));gap:8px;margin:6px 0 14px}
.gm-grid.mt{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(84px,1fr))}
.gm-card{aspect-ratio:3/4;border:0;border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--sunk);cursor:pointer;
 display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:5px;padding:8px;
 font-family:var(--sans);color:var(--ink);transition: background var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.gm-card:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.gm-card.face{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 24%,var(--panel-2))}
.gm-card.done{opacity:.34;cursor:default}
.gm-card.mt{aspect-ratio:1/1}
.gm-card.mt svg{fill:none;stroke:var(--c);stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.gm-back{width:22px;height:22px;border-radius:50%;border:1.6px solid var(--edge-2);opacity:.6}
body.punch .gm-back{border-color:var(--panel);background:var(--panel)}
.gm-cn{font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;text-align:center;line-height:1.25}
.gm-cb{font-size:10.5px;color:var(--dim)}
.gm-tick{font-size:10.5px;color:var(--dim);letter-spacing:.05em}
.gm-line{margin:0;font-size:17px;line-height:1.45;color:var(--ink);max-width:52ch}
/* the truth reads as the answer to the line above it, so it is set apart and
   coloured with the installed pole rather than the held one. */
.gm-line.tru{margin-top:9px;padding-top:9px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge);
 color:var(--good);font-size:16px}
/* the track statement is what the pattern says in the person's own voice. it is
   quoted, because it is not an instruction. */
.gm-track{margin:0 0 9px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--gold);
 font-style:italic;max-width:52ch}
.gm-track:before{content:'\201C'}.gm-track:after{content:'\201D'}
.gm-src{margin-top:10px;font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);letter-spacing:.03em}
.gm-note,.gm-shut{padding:13px 16px;border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--sunk);
 margin:0 0 12px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px}
.gm-gates{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;margin:4px 0 2px}
.gm-glist{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px}
.gm-gate{font-size:11px;color:var(--mid);background:var(--sunk);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 padding:4px 9px;letter-spacing:.02em}
body.punch .gm-gate{background:var(--panel-2)}
.gm-chip em{display:block;font-style:normal;font-size:10px;opacity:.7;letter-spacing:.02em}
/* a card line in the right rail. the release and the truth paired, because a
   release with nothing to install is half the protocol. */
.vt{position:relative}

/* DENSITY. One variable scales the whole interface, so a person who wants
   more on screen gets it without the browser zoom that breaks fixed layouts.
   Set on the root so every rem and every var(--ui) derives from it. */
:root{--ui:1}
body{font-size:calc(14.5px * var(--ui))}
body.dens-tight{--ui:.86}
body.dens-wide{--ui:1.1}

/* A sheet, for the profile and the help. One surface, opened by either
   button, dismissed by the backdrop or by escape. */
.sheet{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:70;background:rgba(6,8,11,.62);
 display:flex;align-items:flex-start;justify-content:flex-end;padding:56px 14px 14px}
.sheet[hidden]{display:none}
.sheet-card{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r);
 width:min(390px,100%);max-height:100%;overflow-y:auto;padding:18px 18px 20px;
 box-shadow:0 18px 60px rgba(0,0,0,.5)}
.sh-h{font-size:17px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink);margin:0 0 3px}
.sh-sec{margin-top:16px}
.sh-row{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;gap:10px;
 padding:7px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);font-size:13px;color:var(--mid)}
.sh-row:last-child{border-bottom:0}
.sh-row b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500}
.sh-p{margin:0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--mid)}
.sh-p.dim{color:var(--dim);font-size:12.5px}
.sh-act{display:flex;gap:7px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:11px}
/* THE IMPORT BOX. A textarea rather than a file picker alone, because a person
   on a phone who copied the record cannot always reach a file, and one who
   saved the file cannot always paste it. Both routes, one handler. */
.sh-imp{margin-top:14px;padding-top:14px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.sh-ta{width:100%;min-height:calc(var(--tap) + 20px);padding:10px 12px;
 background:var(--sunk);border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--num);font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;
 resize:vertical}
.sh-ta:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:1px;
 border-color:var(--accent)}
.sh-impmsg:empty{display:none}
.sh-impmsg.ok{color:var(--heart)}
.sh-impmsg.bad{color:var(--alarm)}
/* ---------- the compass with volume ---------- */
.cone-card{width:min(860px,95vw);max-height:92vh;padding:18px 20px 20px;
 border-radius:var(--r);background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 box-shadow:0 26px 64px rgba(0,0,0,.5);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px}
.cone-hd{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px}
/* TWO ZONES. The figure holds its own column with its switches on it, and
   everything that is a reading sits in the information column on the right,
   which is where the information layer lives everywhere else in this product.
   The figure was taking the full width and then a stack of prose under it. */
/* THE SECOND STRIP. Lower left, carrying what moves through the person, while
   the top strip carries what the instrument reads. Ruled. */
/* ONE LINE. Four chips of one kind are one row, and I capped the strip at
   half the stage so the fourth wrapped underneath on its own, which is not a
   design decision, it is a width I typed without checking what it did. */
/* AND IT MUST NOT COVER THE WHEEL. "Nothing on the stage covers the wheel" is
   a standing rule and this broke it the moment it went in: the strip took
   pointer events across its whole box, so an atom in the lower left could not
   be hovered at all. The functional gate caught it by walking every anchor in
   a story and finding no reachable atom anywhere.

   The container passes the pointer through and only the chips take it. */
/* AND IT BELONGS TO THE FIELD, WHICH NOTHING SAID UNTIL NOW. It is absolutely
   positioned inside .stage at z-index 3 with no tab guard at all, so the
   Field's key strip sat on top of whatever every other surface had in that
   corner, the codex's lower rows included. This file already carries the
   lesson from the other direction: the render watch took the first visible
   child of .stage as the surface host and measured this strip nine times,
   because it is visible on every tab. It was a layout defect then and nobody
   read it as one. One line, on the surface that owns the strip. */
body:not(.tab-field) .keylo{display:none}
/* in the dock now, in flow, so it no longer passes the pointer through to a
   canvas it used to sit on */
.keylo{display:flex;gap:4px;flex-wrap:nowrap;align-items:center}
/* ONE COLUMN. The information that filled the second one has gone to the rail,
   which is what "the information pane" meant, so the figure takes the whole
   card. It was 528 pixels of a 920 pixel card and it is the hero art. */
.cone-body{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:16px;align-items:start;
 min-height:0}
.cone-fig{position:relative;min-width:0}
.cone-cv{display:block;width:100%;height:min(54vh,470px);touch-action:none;cursor:grab;
 border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--sunk)}
.cone-cv:active{cursor:grabbing}
/* ZOOM ON THE COMPASS, round KQ: "the icon's already there. I want to be able
   to zoom in." The Field's three circles, ui/fieldbar.js fbOrb, on the
   figure's own lower right, a column as on the Field. The canvas and the
   circles share a holder so the corner is the canvas's and not the card's,
   whatever the key under the figure is doing. Zoomed in, a drag moves the
   figure, so the hand says so. */
.cn-cvw{position:relative;min-width:0}
/* right 20 and not the Field's 12: the reframe circle's pill rides 20
   pixels past its circle, and at 12 it hung off the canvas's edge */
.cn-zoom{position:absolute;right:20px;bottom:12px;z-index:3;display:flex}
.cn-zoom .fb-grp{flex-direction:row;gap:6px}
.cone-cv.zoomed{cursor:grab}
/* THE NAMES AND THE SWITCHES NO LONGER SIT OVER THE FIGURE'S WELL, round OJ.
   The names are panels beside the pane and the switches are glass circles
   with a ground of their own, so the plate this rule laid under each one while
   the figure was zoomed in has nothing left to do. */
/* on a phone the figure is the full width and its lower right is the crown
   of inversions, so the circles follow the figure in a row, right aligned,
   on the well's own ground, as the Field's do under its picture */
@media (max-width:720px){
 .cn-zoom{position:static;justify-content:flex-end;padding:6px 24px 2px 10px;background:var(--sunk)}
 body.lumen .cn-zoom{background:#101010}
 .cn-zoom .fb-grp{flex-direction:row}}
/* THE OVERLAYS SIT ON THE FIGURE, UPPER LEFT, AND HOW IT IS DRAWN UPPER RIGHT.
   Ruled, and now the Field's own two corners, round OJ: the glass bar's
   position, top 10 and left 12, and the switch capsule's mirror of it. The
   row is the pane's and not the card's, so the corner is the canvas's. */
.cn-top{position:absolute;top:10px;left:12px;right:12px;z-index:3;display:flex;
 align-items:flex-start;justify-content:space-between;gap:8px 12px;pointer-events:none}
.cn-top>*{pointer-events:auto}
.cnbar{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;min-width:0;margin:0}
.cnbar .fb-tog{order:-1}
.cnbar.shut>*:not(.fb-tog){display:none}
.cnbar.shut .fb-tog .lf-ch{transform:rotate(180deg)}
/* HOW IT IS DRAWN is one of two, so it is the Field's capsule: the circles sit
   on a lozenge of the glass because they are one control choosing, where the
   bar's circles are separate switches. The same three rules .fview has. */
.cnview{display:flex;align-items:center;flex:0 0 auto;padding:2px;border-radius:999px;
 background:var(--fb-glass);border:1px solid var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 8px 22px var(--fb-shade)}
.cnview .fb-grp{gap:4px}
/* A WAY OF DRAWING HAS NO READING, so it carries no pill and its ring is
   whole while it is on and absent while it is off, the rule .fview gives a
   rendition. A dash of nought with a round cap is a dot, and a dot at twelve
   o'clock reads as a reading of one. */
.cn-st .fb-arc .val{stroke:var(--accent);stroke-dasharray:100 100}
.cn-st:not(.on) .fb-arc .val{opacity:0}
.cn-st .fb-arc .val,.cn-b .fb-arc .val,.cn-sb2 .fb-arc .val{transition:opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.cone-info{display:grid;grid-template-rows:auto minmax(0,1fr) auto;gap:14px;min-width:0}
.cone-mid{min-height:0;overflow-y:auto;scrollbar-width:thin}
/* UNDER THE FIGURE MEANS UNDER IT, NOT ON IT. The hint was absolute at bottom
   10 of .cone-fig, which is the canvas's bottom edge, and the canvas draws
   "Decoherent" at by+30 (ui/cone.js), the same band. Measured on Abraham,
   every lighting: at 1600 "Press any name" sat across "Decoherent", and at
   390 the hint wraps to two lines and both were overprinted with the label.
   Found beside the header collision above and the same kind of defect: a
   block pinned over a drawing that was never told it was there. In flow it
   takes its own line under the canvas at every width.

   And it stays on the well. The first cut put it in flow on the stage, which
   is the ruled #101010, and under Snow the hint's own --dim measured 2.94 to 1
   there against the light well it had been sitting on. So the well grows a
   line instead: the hint carries the canvas's ground and the canvas gives up
   its lower corners to it, which is the look it had, without the overprint. */
.cone-cv{border-radius:var(--r-s) var(--r-s) 0 0}
.cone-hint{margin:0;padding:4px 12px 10px;font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.5;
 background:var(--sunk);border-radius:0 0 var(--r-s) var(--r-s);
 color:var(--dim);pointer-events:none}
/* THE KEY TO REGULATION AND LAYERS, which used to be painted across the
   figure at nine and a half pixels (ui/cone.js, coneKey). On the well, like
   the hint, and in the name rail's own type so the two read as one surface:
   the label is .cn-nq's quiet 11 and 500, the name is .cn-nu's 12.5 in its
   colour. The value drops to the mid ink, because the colour already says
   which arrow and the number is the one thing that should not shout. */
.cone-key{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px 28px;margin:0;padding:8px 12px 4px;
 background:var(--sunk)}
.ck-g{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;min-width:0}
.ck-h{font-size:11px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.02em;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.3}
.ck-rows{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:3px 14px}
.ck-r{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:5px;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600;
 line-height:1.35;color:var(--ax)}
.ck-v{font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-weight:500;
 color:var(--mid)}
.cone-txt{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:9px}
.cone-p{margin:0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--mid);max-width:68ch}
/* ---------- coherence over time ---------- */
.cn-gr{border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:12px 13px 11px;
 background:var(--panel-2);cursor:pointer;
 transition:border-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.cn-gr:hover{border-color:var(--edge-2)}
body.punch .cn-gr{border-color:transparent}
.cn-gh{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;margin-bottom:10px}
.cn-spans{display:flex;gap:3px;flex-wrap:wrap}
/* MIN WIDTH AS WELL AS MIN HEIGHT. "Day" is a three character label and came
   back 42 wide, which the tap gate caught. A short word does not make a small
   target. */
.cn-sb{min-height:var(--tap);min-width:var(--tap);padding:6px 9px;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 border:1px solid var(--edge);background:var(--sunk);color:var(--dim);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12px;cursor:pointer;
 transition:color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),border-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.cn-sb:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.cn-sb.on{color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--accent)}
.cn-gsvg{display:block;width:100%;height:84px;overflow:visible}
.cn-gf{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;margin-top:7px;
 font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-size:13px;color:var(--dim)}
.cn-gf b{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12px;font-weight:500;color:var(--mid)}
.cn-gp{margin:0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--dim)}
.cn-gmore{display:block;width:100%;min-height:var(--tap);margin-top:10px;
 background:transparent;border:0;border-top:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--accent);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;cursor:pointer;text-align:left;padding:9px 0 0}
body.punch .cn-gmore{border-top-color:var(--panel)}
@media (max-width:1180px){
 .cone-body{grid-template-columns:1fr}
 .cone-cv{height:min(46vh,380px)}}
/* Three named rows rather than three letters. A setting chosen once should say
   what it does, and there is room for it here where there was not in the bar. */
.dens-list{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px}
.dens-opt{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:flex-start;gap:2px;
 background:var(--sunk);border:1px solid transparent;color:var(--mid);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:9px 12px;cursor:pointer;text-align:left;width:100%;
 min-height:44px}
.dens-opt b{font-size:13px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}
.dens-opt em{font-style:normal;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--dim)}
.dens-opt.on{border-color:var(--gold)}
.dens-opt.on b{color:var(--gold)}
@media (max-width:720px){ .sheet{align-items:flex-end;padding:0}
 .sheet-card{width:100%;max-height:82vh;border-radius:var(--r) var(--r) 0 0} }

/* SCROLLBARS. Edge and Windows Chrome draw the classic seventeen pixel
   scrollbar, which is what the owner is looking at in the middle column. It is
   grey furniture against a muted dark palette and it takes real width out of a
   panel that is already tight. Thin, seat coloured, and it stays visible,
   because rule 10 says never hide a control with no affordance. */
*{scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:var(--edge-2) transparent}
::-webkit-scrollbar{width:9px;height:9px}
::-webkit-scrollbar-track{background:transparent}
::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb{background:var(--edge-2);border-radius:99px;
 border:2px solid transparent;background-clip:content-box}
::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover{background:var(--dim);background-clip:content-box}
::-webkit-scrollbar-corner{background:transparent}

/* what the instrument knows, on the tab that is about what it knows. The
   .ab-band rule went with the string it dressed: "plus or minus 11" set in
   gold beside the figure, which is a lab readout given the accent colour. A
   class left behind after its only writer is deleted is a rule nobody can
   account for, so it goes in the same change rather than in a later sweep. */
.ab-acc{display:flex;align-items:baseline;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin-top:12px;
 padding-top:11px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.ab-acc b{font-family:var(--num);font-size:22px;font-weight:400;color:var(--ink)}
.ab-note{flex:1 1 100%;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.5}
.kb-pair{margin:0 0 10px;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
.kb-pair:last-child{border-bottom:0}
.kb-rel{margin:0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--ink)}
.kb-tru{margin:5px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--good)}
/* one side clear is not the release. it reads as partial, not as done. */
.gm-card.half{opacity:.62}
.gm-card.half .gm-tick{color:var(--gold)}
.gm-on{font-size:22px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}
.gm-oo{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--gold)}
.gm-act{display:flex;gap:7px;flex-wrap:wrap}

/* ---------- the letting go deck ---------- */
.card{width:min(380px,calc(100% - 32px));background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r);padding:22px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;box-shadow:var(--shadow)}
body.punch .card{border-color:transparent}
.card-hd{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;
 border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);padding-bottom:10px}
.card-rk{font-family:var(--num);font-size:26px;font-weight:500;color:var(--c)}
.card-suit{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.06em;color:var(--c)}
.card-nm{font-size:24px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.2}
.card-ad{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:4px}
.card-say{border-left:2px solid var(--c);padding-left:13px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px}
.card-say p{margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink)}
.card-ft{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;font-size:12px;color:var(--dim);
 border-top:1px solid var(--edge);padding-top:10px}
.card-sq{font-family:var(--num);color:var(--ink)}
.card-act{display:flex;gap:6px;flex-wrap:wrap}
/* the stack tabs under the reading */
.stack{margin-top:14px}
.stk-tabs{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px;margin-bottom:8px}
/* THE TAP FLOOR IS A FLOOR. These two strips set 32 and 36 against a measured
   minimum of 44, and they render on every surface in the product, so one
   declaration was responsible for five of the seven failing tabs. Gate 8 now
   watches it, which is why it stopped being a preference. */
.stk-t{background:var(--sunk);border:0;color:var(--mid);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 padding:8px 11px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12px;cursor:pointer;
 min-height:var(--tap);display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px}
.stk-t svg{width:15px;height:15px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;flex:0 0 auto;opacity:.75}
.stk-t.on svg{opacity:1;stroke:var(--accent)}
.stk-t b{font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;color:var(--dim);margin-left:2px}
.stk-t.on{background:transparent;color:var(--accent);font-weight:500}
.stk-t.on b{color:var(--accent)}
.stk-hd{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);
 text-transform:none;padding:2px 6px 4px;letter-spacing:.02em}
.stk-r{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:10px;width:100%;
 min-height:var(--tap);padding:3px 6px;border:0;background:transparent;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;color:var(--ink);text-align:left;cursor:pointer}
.stk-r.static{cursor:default}
.stk-r:not(.static):hover,.stk-r.on{background:var(--panel-2)}
.stk-l,.stk-p{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;min-width:0}
.stk-l{flex:1 1 auto;overflow:hidden;white-space:nowrap;text-overflow:ellipsis}
.stk-p{flex:0 0 auto;color:var(--mid);font-size:12px}
.stk-r em{font-style:normal;color:var(--dim);font-size:11px;margin-left:4px}
.rows{font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.85;color:var(--dim);margin-top:14px;padding-top:12px;
 border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.rows b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500;font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.rows .k{color:var(--gold);font-size:11px;font-weight:600;font-family:var(--sans)}
.it{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;color:var(--mid);cursor:pointer;
 padding:5px 8px;border-radius:var(--r-xs);font-size:13.5px;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.it:hover{color:var(--ink);background:var(--panel-2)}
.it.pin{background:rgba(223,204,126,.2);color:var(--ink)}
.it b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500;font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* a Running row: the badge, then the name, which takes the rest and ellipses */
.it.run-it{justify-content:flex-start;align-items:center;gap:8px}
.it.run-it .run-n{min-width:0;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;white-space:nowrap}
.bmrow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:86px 1fr 46px;gap:10px;align-items:center;
 font-size:11px;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:7px}
.bmk{color:var(--dim)}
.bmbar{height:8px;background:var(--sunk);border-radius:4px;overflow:hidden}
.bmbar i{display:block;height:100%;border-radius:4px;transition: width var(--t-context) var(--ease-out)}
.bmv{text-align:right;font-size:13.5px;font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.bmnote{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.55;margin-top:5px;font-weight:300}

/* ---------- select ---------- */
select#psel{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 min-height:var(--tap);border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:8px 11px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;
 font-weight:400;cursor:pointer;max-width:262px}
select#psel option{background:var(--panel);color:var(--ink);padding:5px}
select#psel optgroup{background:var(--sunk);color:var(--dim);font-style:normal;
 font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600}

/* ============================================================
   ENERGY MAP
   ============================================================ */
.emap{position:absolute;inset:0;display:none;flex-direction:column;overflow:hidden;
 border-radius:var(--r);background:transparent}
.emap.on{display:flex}
.pm-top{position:absolute;top:12px;left:12px;z-index:3;display:flex;gap:4px;flex-wrap:wrap;
 max-width:52%;padding:6px;border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--panel);
 border:1px solid var(--edge);box-shadow:var(--shadow)}
.pm-lb{background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--mid);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:8px 14px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;
 font-weight:400;cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);
 min-height:var(--tap);display:inline-flex;align-items:center}
.pm-lb:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.pm-lb.on{background:var(--gold);border-color:var(--gold);color:var(--on-accent);font-weight:500}
/* the count each layer holds, so a layer with nothing in it says so before it
   is opened rather than after. An empty layer stays reachable and reads back. */
.pm-lb b{font-family:var(--num);font-weight:400;font-size:11.5px;margin-left:7px;
 opacity:.66;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.pm-lb.empty{opacity:.42}
.pm-lb.empty:hover{opacity:.72}
.pm-reg{position:absolute;top:66px;left:12px;z-index:3;max-width:56%;padding:9px 11px;
 border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);box-shadow:var(--shadow)}
.pm-regb{display:flex;gap:4px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:7px}
.pm-rb{background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--mid);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 padding:5px 10px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.pm-rb:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.pm-rb.on{background:var(--gold);border-color:var(--gold);color:var(--on-accent);font-weight:500}
.pm-well{flex:1;position:relative;min-height:0;overflow:hidden}
/* ---- THE BODY'S TWO BARS, ACROSS THE TOP OF THE WELL. LM in TASKS.md, his
   words: "I want to overlay menu on the left side, on the left top across the
   top, like the Field ... the third tier nav that says Mark ... on the upper
   right opposite the overlay, both have buttons that close them. The right
   one starts closed." ui/map.js bmBuild writes them, bmOvBuild and bmMkBuild
   fill them. One row holding both, so they share the width and each wraps
   inside its share before the two can meet; its gaps take no press, so the
   figure under them still answers. The fold circle of each is its outer
   end: the burger first on the left, the sliders last on the right. ---- */
/* the overlays take whatever the Mark row and its circle leave, from a basis
   of nothing, so the circle on the right is never pushed off the line and
   the overlays wrap inside their share instead */
.bm-top{position:absolute;top:10px;left:12px;right:12px;z-index:3;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;
 align-items:flex-start;gap:8px 12px;pointer-events:none}
.bm-ov{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:12px;min-width:0;flex:1 1 0;order:1}
.bm-mkb{order:2}
.bm-mk{display:flex;flex-direction:row-reverse;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;flex:0 0 auto;order:3}
.bm-mk>*,.bm-mkb{pointer-events:auto}
.bm-mk.shut+.bm-mkb{display:none}
.bm-mk .fb-arc .val{opacity:0}
/* a phone has no room for the row beside the circles, and squeezed beside
   them it stood as a column of three buttons with Point in patch broken over
   two lines, measured at 390. It takes the next line, right aligned under
   the circle that opened it, and the circle stays where it was pressed. */
@media (max-width:720px){.bm-mkb{order:4;flex:0 0 auto;margin-left:auto}}
/* the Mark row lies on the picture, so it sits on the bar's own glass, the
   capsule the Field's Wheel, Frames and Dial switch sits on */
.bm-mkb{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:flex-end;align-items:center;gap:5px;min-width:0;
 padding:3px 4px;border-radius:24px;background:var(--fb-glass);border:1px solid var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 8px 22px var(--fb-shade)}
.bm-mkb .pm-lb{border-radius:999px}
.bm-whole{background:var(--fb-glass);border-radius:999px}
.bm-whole[hidden]{display:none}
/* ---- THE PARTS, ON TWO SHELVES DOWN THE SIDES OF THE WELL. LQ, his words:
   "I want the zone controls on the left and right sides of the center
   display area ... on a shelf that pops out ... a minimize button ... both
   start minimized." ui/map.js bmShBuild, bmRegBar and bmRegFit. Front on the
   left edge beside the front figure, Back on the right beside the back one,
   each one circle until pressed, and pressed the panel drops under it on
   the bar's glass, the way the Mark row opens beside its circle. The layer
   covers the well and takes no press, so the figure between the shelves
   still answers. The shelves start under the band the top bars take, and
   never higher than the burger's own circle, 58 pixels down with the band
   shut. The target stays 44, the tap floor; the frame drawn inside it is 32
   and one pixel. ---- */
.bm-regs{position:absolute;inset:0;z-index:3;pointer-events:none}
.bm-sh{position:absolute;top:calc(max(var(--bmtop,0px),58px) + 4px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;
 align-items:flex-start;gap:6px}
.bm-sh.bm-front{left:12px}
.bm-sh.bm-back{right:12px;align-items:flex-end}
.bm-sh>*{pointer-events:auto}
.bm-sh .fb-arc .val{opacity:0}
.bm-sh.shut .bm-shp{display:none}
.bm-shp{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(var(--bmc,2),44px);padding:4px;border-radius:14px;
 background:var(--fb-glass);border:1px solid var(--edge-2);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 8px 22px var(--fb-shade)}
.bm-pt{position:relative;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:44px;height:44px;
 padding:0;margin:0;border:0;background:transparent;color:var(--mid);cursor:pointer;touch-action:manipulation}
.bm-pt>i{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:32px;height:32px;box-sizing:border-box;
 border:1px solid var(--edge-2);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 transition:border-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),background-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.bm-pt svg{width:24px;height:24px}
.bm-pt:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.bm-pt:hover>i{border-color:var(--mid)}
.bm-pt.on>i{background:var(--gold);border-color:var(--gold)}
.bm-pt b{position:absolute;top:0;right:1px;margin:0;font-family:var(--num);font-weight:400;font-size:11px;
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink)}
/* Punch outlines nothing: the frame is a solid tile there, as every pm-lb is */
body.punch .bm-pt>i{border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
body.punch .bm-pt.on>i{background:var(--gold)}
.pm-aura{position:absolute;inset:-18%;pointer-events:none;z-index:0;filter:blur(40px);
 transition: opacity var(--t-context) var(--ease-out), background var(--t-context) var(--ease-out)}
.pm-svg{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;display:block;z-index:1}
.pm-fig{display:none}
.pm-art{opacity:.62}
body.snow .pm-art{filter:invert(1) brightness(.32);opacity:.82}
/* the vector figure. stands in when the raster is absent, and it needs a
   resolved colour for its currentColor stroke. */
.pm-vec{color:var(--ink)}
.pm-seat,.pm-n,.pm-it,.pm-gr,.pm-mask{cursor:pointer}
.pm-gr:hover{opacity:.14!important}
.pm-lbl{font-family:var(--num);font-size:2.1px;fill:var(--ink);opacity:.92;
 paint-order:stroke;stroke:var(--bg);stroke-width:.55px;stroke-linejoin:round}
.pm-gl{font-family:var(--num);font-size:1.5px;opacity:.9}
/* the seat names beside the figure. Not .pm-sn: that is the seat name in the
   rail's own rows, and the first cut shared it, so the rail's seven names
   dropped to 1.6px and gate 4 caught them. Sized from the well in map.js. */
.pm-seatn{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1.6px;font-weight:500;pointer-events:none}
/* the count takes its seat's colour, not --dim: the well stays dark under Snow
   and --dim goes dark with the theme, which left the numbers unreadable there */
.pm-seatc{font-weight:400;opacity:.72}
.pm-gv{opacity:.55}
.pm-gs{font-family:var(--num);font-size:1.15px;fill:var(--ink);opacity:.6}

/* ---------- energy shelf. lives in the right rail. ---------- */
.pm-hd{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;
 padding-bottom:13px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
.pm-big{font-family:var(--num);font-size:42px;font-weight:400;line-height:1;
 letter-spacing:0;color:var(--gold);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.pm-big small{font-size:18px;opacity:.55;margin-left:2px}
.pm-side{text-align:right}
.pm-dom{font-size:17px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;margin-top:4px}
.pm-sub{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:5px;max-width:172px;line-height:1.5}
.pm-seats{margin-top:12px}
.pm-sr{display:grid;grid-template-columns:10px 1fr 62px auto auto;gap:10px;align-items:center;
 width:100%;background:transparent;border:0;border-left:3px solid transparent;
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:9px 11px;cursor:pointer;color:var(--mid);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;text-align:left;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);margin-bottom:3px}
.pm-sr:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.pm-sr.on{background:rgba(223,204,126,.16);border-left-color:var(--gold);color:var(--ink)}
.pm-sr.clear{opacity:.52}
.pm-sr i{width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%}
.pm-gauge{grid-column:3;height:6px;background:var(--sunk);border-radius:3px;overflow:hidden;align-self:center}
.pm-gauge b{display:block;height:100%;border-radius:3px}
.pm-sn{font-weight:500}
.pm-sk{grid-column:2;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:-2px}
.pm-sv,.pm-sp{font-family:var(--num);font-size:12.5px;font-weight:500;color:var(--dim);
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.pm-sp{color:var(--gold);min-width:40px;text-align:right}
.pm-det{margin-top:14px;padding-top:13px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.pm-dn{font-size:19px;font-weight:600;margin:4px 0 5px;letter-spacing:0}
.pm-dm{font-size:13px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.65}
.pm-dm b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500}
.pm-pat{font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--mid);border-left:3px solid var(--gold);
 padding-left:11px;margin:6px 0 0;font-weight:300}
.pm-rows{margin-top:10px}
.pm-r{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:baseline;font-size:13px;padding:4px 6px;
 color:var(--mid);cursor:pointer;border-radius:var(--r-xs)}
.pm-r:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.pm-r em{font-style:normal;color:var(--heart);flex:1;opacity:.88;font-size:12.5px}
.pm-r b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500;font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.pm-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto;gap:3px 12px;margin-top:11px;
 font-size:13.5px;color:var(--dim)}
.pm-grid b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500;text-align:right;font-family:var(--num);
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.pm-chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin-top:6px}
.pm-chip{font-size:12.5px;background:var(--sunk);border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:4px 9px;
 color:var(--mid);font-weight:400}
.pm-chip.over{background:var(--alarm-soft);color:var(--alarm)}
.pm-more{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);padding:5px 6px}
/* the layer list. in the original these classes were emitted and never styled. */
.pm-list{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:3px;margin-top:7px}
.pm-li{display:grid;grid-template-columns:10px 1fr auto;gap:6px 10px;align-items:center;
 width:100%;background:transparent;border:1px solid transparent;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 padding:9px 11px;cursor:pointer;color:var(--mid);font-family:var(--sans);text-align:left;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.pm-li:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.pm-li.on{background:rgba(223,204,126,.16);border-color:rgba(223,204,126,.42);color:var(--ink)}
.pm-li i{width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;align-self:center}
.pm-ln{font-size:14.5px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink)}
.pm-lw{font-family:var(--num);font-size:14px;font-weight:500;color:var(--gold);
 text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.pm-ls{grid-column:2;font-size:12px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:-2px}
.pm-lk{grid-column:3;font-family:var(--num);font-size:11.5px;color:var(--dim);text-align:right}
.pm-leg{display:grid;grid-template-columns:auto 1fr;gap:6px 12px;margin-top:7px;
 font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5}
.pm-lgk{color:var(--gold);font-weight:500}
.pm-lgv{color:var(--dim)}

/* ============================================================
   STORY
   ============================================================ */
.iq{position:absolute;inset:0;display:none;flex-direction:column;overflow-y:auto;padding:18px}
/* ============================================================
   THE STORY IS THREE COLUMNS, LAYOUT H, AND THE ONE IN USE GETS THE ROOM.

   Round IJ wired in round II's prototype, proto/story-redesign2. Write,
   imprints and release stand side by side with nothing below the fold at
   1600, his round HY ruling: "I want story, imprints and release to all be
   above the fold". The flex values are the prototype's as measured. Which
   column has the room is data-focus on the row, set by ui/storyui.js, so the
   whole of the shape change is written here and none of it in script.

   The panels inside the stage are tinted with the lighting's own ink rather
   than given a panel colour, because the stage already is one on every
   lighting and a panel colour on a panel is invisible on four of the seven.
   ============================================================ */
.st-flow{display:flex;gap:12px;width:100%;height:100%;min-height:0;align-items:stretch;text-align:left}
.st-colw,.st-colr,.st-colx{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;min-width:0;min-height:0;
 transition:flex var(--t-context) var(--ease-out)}
.st-colw{flex:1.5 1 0}
.st-colr{flex:1 1 0}
.st-colx{flex:0 0 300px}
.st-flow[data-focus="read"] .st-colw{flex:1 1 0}
.st-flow[data-focus="read"] .st-colr{flex:1.55 1 0}
.st-flow[data-focus="release"] .st-colw{flex:1 1 0}
.st-flow[data-focus="release"] .st-colx{flex:0 0 430px}
.st-pan{display:flex;flex-direction:column;min-width:0;min-height:0;position:relative;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 3%,transparent);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:14px 16px}
.st-jr{flex:1 1 0}
.st-ch{flex:0 0 50%}
.st-ls{flex:1 1 0}
.st-eb{font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim)}
.st-tag{font-size:12px;color:var(--dim);margin-right:auto;white-space:nowrap}
.st-none{color:var(--dim);font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.55;margin:10px 0 0;max-width:52ch}
.st-ring{display:inline-block;width:12px;height:12px;border-radius:50%;flex:0 0 auto;
 border:1.6px solid var(--c,var(--accent))}
/* the column itself does not scroll: the list and the release queue do, each
   on its own, so a long entry never pushes Run off the screen. */
/* Record alone since round LO took the eyebrow off its left, so the row ends
   on it where space-between used to push it */
.st-hd{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:flex-end;gap:6px 10px;
 flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:9px}
/* ---- the instrument's panel: the name, the bank and the vault, the sort ---- */
.st-chd{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;min-height:24px}
.st-ico{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;min-height:var(--tap);min-width:var(--tap);
 padding:0 11px;border-radius:999px;border:1px solid var(--edge-2);background:transparent;
 cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;color:var(--mid);justify-content:center;
 transition:border-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.st-ico svg{flex:0 0 auto;width:18px;height:18px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;
 stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-linecap:round}
.st-ico:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.st-ico[aria-pressed="true"]{border-color:var(--accent);color:var(--ink)}
.st-ico b{font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* THE BANK AND THE VAULT ARE ICONS ONLY, round LO: "I just want their icons
   only, not the text." The word is clipped rather than removed, the same
   rule a folded tab's name gets (.tabnames-i:not(:checked) above), so it
   stays the button's name to a screen reader. It was display:none on a phone
   only, which took the name away from the reader there too and left the
   title to carry it; that rule is gone, this one covers every width. */
.st-lb{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);
 clip-path:inset(50%);white-space:nowrap}
/* the list door exists for the phone, where the list folds away */
.st-open{display:none}
.st-sort{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px;align-items:center;margin-top:8px}
.st-sort .st-eb{margin-right:4px}
/* the canvas sits on the lighting's sunk ground. .st-cvw carries the ink and
   the hidden key carries dim and the accent, which is how the canvas reads
   the lighting: a custom property never reaches a 2D context. */
.st-cvw{position:relative;flex:1 1 auto;min-height:120px;margin:8px -6px 0;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 background:var(--sunk);overflow:hidden;color:var(--ink)}
.st-cvw canvas{position:absolute;inset:0;display:block;width:100%;height:100%}
.st-cvk{display:none;color:var(--dim);border-top:1px solid var(--accent)}
.st-ctr{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:6px;min-height:18px}
/* ---- the list: this entry by lane, or the vault ---- */
.st-lshd{font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim);min-height:22px}
.st-lsb{flex:1 1 auto;overflow:auto;margin:4px -6px 0;padding:0 6px;
 scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:var(--edge-2) transparent}
.st-grp{margin:10px -6px 0;border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:2px 6px 6px;
 transition:background var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.st-grp.hot{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 9%,transparent)}
.st-ghd{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:8px;font-size:12.5px;
 border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);padding:4px 0}
.st-ghd b{color:var(--c);font-weight:600;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px}
.st-ghd svg{width:15px;height:15px;fill:none;stroke:var(--c);stroke-width:1.8;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-linecap:round}
.st-ghd em{font-style:normal;color:var(--dim);text-align:right}
.st-ghd em.warn{color:var(--mid)}
.st-pills{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin-top:7px}
.st-pill{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 45%,transparent);
 border-radius:999px;padding:4px 11px 4px 8px;font-size:13.5px;min-height:30px;color:var(--ink)}
.st-pill.inf{border-style:dashed}
.st-pill small{color:var(--mid);font-size:12px}
.st-pill.new{animation:stPillIn var(--t-surface) var(--ease-land)}
@keyframes stPillIn{from{transform:scale(.6);opacity:0}}
/* ---- the release, lit from within by the heaviest address it will run ----
   Round HX, design B, which he did not strike. The glow is the selected seat
   and its brightness is how heavy that address is, both set by storyui.js. */
.st-rl{flex:1 1 auto;display:flex;flex-direction:column;min-height:0;border-radius:var(--r-s);
 padding:14px 16px;--glow:var(--accent);
 background:radial-gradient(70% 90px at 50% 0,color-mix(in srgb,var(--glow) 30%,transparent),transparent),
  radial-gradient(120% 70% at 50% 0%,color-mix(in srgb,var(--glow) calc(var(--lux,.3) * 50%),transparent),transparent 70%),
  color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 3%,transparent);
 box-shadow:0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb,var(--glow) 40%,transparent),0 18px 60px -18px color-mix(in srgb,var(--glow) 50%,transparent)}
.st-rlhd{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:9px}
