/* RECORD IS RED, UNAMBIGUOUSLY, WHILE IT IS RECORDING. Round LV, his words:
   "The record button I want as a red record button. When I press it,
   there's an animation showing me that it's being recorded, and I want the
   time showing how long my recording is."

   The dot already carried the universal record colour at rest, so a person
   sees before they press that this is the control that goes red. It did
   not carry that through to the recording state itself, where the button
   read green against a red dot, the one moment it most needed to be plain:
   .st-mic.on already had a second, later rule turning the whole control
   red, and the dot's own green never gave way to it. Now the two agree: the
   button is solid alarm red the instant it is on, the dot turns white so it
   still reads against its own ground, a ring breathes out from it once a
   beat, and the elapsed time sits inside the same pill. Transform and
   opacity only, so quiet and reduced motion hold the dot and the ring lit
   and still rather than run either faster. */
.st-mic{min-height:var(--tap);display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;
 background:var(--panel-2);border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--mid);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:7px 15px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;cursor:pointer;
 transition:background var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),border-color var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),
  color var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.st-mic svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;
 stroke-linejoin:round;flex:0 0 auto}
.st-dot{width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:50%;flex:0 0 auto;position:relative;
 background:var(--alarm);transition:background var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.st-mic.on{background:var(--alarm);border-color:var(--alarm);color:#fff;font-weight:500}
.st-mic.on .st-dot{background:#fff;animation:micPulse 1.15s ease-in-out infinite}
/* the ring is the dot's own ::after, not a third element, so it travels
   with it and needs nothing wired */
.st-mic.on .st-dot::after{content:"";position:absolute;inset:-5px;border-radius:50%;
 border:1.5px solid #fff;opacity:0;pointer-events:none;
 animation:micRing 1.15s ease-out infinite}
@keyframes micPulse{0%,100%{opacity:1;transform:scale(1)}50%{opacity:.45;transform:scale(.78)}}
@keyframes micRing{0%{opacity:.55;transform:scale(.35)}100%{opacity:0;transform:scale(2)}}
/* mm:ss, ROUND LV. The same numeric family the chart's own figures use, so
   it reads as a measurement and not as more label. */
.st-mictime{font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-size:13px;opacity:.92}
body.quiet .st-mic.on .st-dot,body.rm .st-mic.on .st-dot{animation:none;opacity:1}
body.quiet .st-mic.on .st-dot::after,body.rm .st-mic.on .st-dot::after{animation:none;opacity:0}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
 .st-mic.on .st-dot{animation:none;opacity:1}
 .st-mic.on .st-dot::after{animation:none;opacity:0}}
/* ============================================================
   THE EDITOR IS TWO LAYERS IN REGISTER.

   A textarea cannot carry colour inside its own value, so the highlight is a
   layer behind it holding the same text, and the textarea's own text is
   transparent with only its caret and its selection showing.

   Every metric that affects where a glyph lands has to be identical on both
   or they drift: font, size, weight, line height, padding, border width,
   letter spacing and the wrapping mode. They are set once on the shared
   parent and inherited, which is the only way to keep them from parting.
   ============================================================ */
.st-ed{position:relative;flex:1 1 auto;min-height:260px;display:flex;
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:16px;line-height:1.75;font-weight:300;
 letter-spacing:normal}
.st-ta,.st-hl{
 flex:1 1 auto;min-height:260px;
 border:1px solid transparent;border-radius:var(--r-s);padding:18px 20px;
 font-family:inherit;font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;font-weight:inherit;
 letter-spacing:inherit;
 white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;overflow-wrap:break-word;
 margin:0}
.st-hl{position:absolute;inset:0;overflow:hidden;pointer-events:none;
 background:var(--sunk);border-color:var(--edge);color:transparent;z-index:0}
.st-ta{position:relative;z-index:1;resize:none;background:transparent;
 color:transparent;caret-color:var(--ink);outline:none;overflow:auto}
.st-ta::selection{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 34%,transparent)}
.st-ta:focus+.st-hl,.st-ed:focus-within .st-hl{border-color:var(--accent)}
/* the text a person reads is the layer behind, so that is the one with ink */
.st-hl{color:var(--ink)}
/* AND THE FETTERS ARE LIT IN THEIR OWN SEAT COLOUR.

   Bold and coloured, ruled. The tint behind them is what makes a run of
   three in one sentence read as a cluster rather than as three loud words.

   THE BOLD IS GONE, AND THAT REVERSES THE RULING ABOVE UNTIL HE CONFIRMS IT.
   The mark sat at 600 on this layer while the textarea that owns the caret
   sat at the editor's 300, which broke the rule in the block above: weight is
   a metric that moves glyphs. Bold Inter is wider than light Inter, so every
   character after a mark drew to the right of where the textarea placed it,
   and the caret landed inside the words a person was reading. Measured on the
   round HT build at 1600 with "I was angry and ashamed and tight and angry and
   ashamed then it ended here", five marks, the end of the line sat at x 719.66
   with the marks at 600 and x 706.83 without: 12.83px of drift on one line,
   growing with every mark. Emphasis is carried by colour, tint, the ring and
   an underline in the seat colour instead, none of which takes up any width.
   Weight is the one treatment this layer cannot use while the layers are in
   register, so do not put it back here; if the owner rules bold must stay,
   the textarea has to carry the same marks' widths, which it cannot. */
.st-f{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 17%,transparent);color:var(--c);
 border-radius:3px;
 box-shadow:0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 26%,transparent),
  inset 0 -2px 0 var(--c)}
.st-ta:focus{border-color:rgba(223,204,126,.48)}
.st-ta::placeholder{color:var(--dim)}
.st-bar{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;margin-top:11px}
.st-ct{flex:1 1 auto;font-family:var(--num);font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim)}
/* ---------- the Story's rail, GO in TASKS.md ----------
   The imprints and the release live in the right rail on the Story and
   nowhere else. The rail's other sections step aside there, except Selection,
   which is where Detail on an imprint opens its drill. */
body:not(.tab-story) .lsec.st-rail{display:none}
body.tab-story .lsec[data-rail="right"]:not(.st-rail):not([data-sec="sel"]){display:none}
/* #railtop left the rail for the Field's stage in round IT and is hidden off
   the Field by its own rule, so only #bRel is stepped aside here */
body.tab-story #bRel{display:none}
/* five grouping buttons are wider than a rail, and a row that overflows with
   no affordance reads as a missing control, so it wraps. */
.st-rail .ip-ctl{flex-wrap:wrap}
/* THE RAIL STEPS ASIDE ON THE STORY, AND THE BANK BRINGS IT BACK. Round HY,
   his words: "feel free to break that right navigation." The three columns
   take its width, so the stage track is the rest of the row. The bank sets
   st-bank and the rail returns with the Imprints page in it and Selection
   under it, where that page's Detail opens. Stacked, the rail is a block
   under the surface, so it is simply not there until the bank asks for it. */
body.tab-story:not(.st-bank) .mid>.col:not(#lcol){display:none}
@media (min-width:1181px){
 body.tab-story:not(.st-bank) .mid{grid-template-columns:302px 1fr}
 body.lshut.tab-story:not(.st-bank) .mid{grid-template-columns:58px 1fr}}
/* ---------- Source AI, over the journal ----------
   Its own panel and its own look, HT in TASKS.md: no box, a bar in the
   accent down its left edge, so it reads as the system speaking and not as
   another place to type. The opening question is the largest line on the
   page while nothing is written, and steps back once the person is writing,
   because from then on they lead and the question is theirs to answer or not. */
/* A DISPLAY, ON ITS OWN BLACK, round LO: "Right now the source scripted
   area is on gray ... I want it to look like a display area. So I want it to
   have its own black background." It sits inside the journal's panel now, over
   the box, so its ground is the box's own, --sunk, the ground the chart and
   every other display in the product reads from. That is black in the dark
   lightings and follows the light ones, where a hard black slab would be the
   one thing on the page that did not. The accent bar stays: it is what says
   the system is speaking. */
/* .st-glow wraps .st-pe, round LV, see below; the sizing and the spacing
   that used to sit on .st-pe itself now sit on the wrapper, since it is the
   wrapper that is the flex item of the journal's column now. */
.st-glow{flex:0 0 auto;margin-bottom:10px;position:relative}
.st-pe{border-left:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:var(--r-s);
 background:var(--sunk);padding:12px 12px 14px 16px;min-width:0;position:relative}
/* ---------- Source AI, alive. Round LT ----------
   His words: "I want Source AI to feel like it's a thinking system ... makes
   that text look like it's alive." Three moves, all on this panel and all on
   transform and opacity, so the compositor carries them and no layout runs:

     the bar     a light passes down the accent bar every six seconds and
                 rests between passes. While keys are coming it runs quick
                 and bright, .hear, because srcHear really is reading the
                 whole entry again on every key.
     the mark    a dot before the name breathes on the same slow beat, and
                 holds lit while it hears.
     the words   a question new to the screen lands a word at a time, on the
                 product's own stagger, each word arriving in the accent and
                 settling to ink, and a block caret blinks twice after it.

   Both lights sit on #stsrc itself, which outlives the repaint the column
   takes on every key, so a key never restarts the run it is feeding. Quiet
   and reduced motion hold all three still, lit, the way .av-pulse does. */
.st-pe::before,.st-pe::after{content:"";position:absolute;left:-3px;top:0;width:3px;height:38%;
 border-radius:3px;pointer-events:none;will-change:transform,opacity;
 background:linear-gradient(to bottom,transparent,var(--ink) 50%,transparent);
 box-shadow:0 0 10px 1px var(--accent)}
.st-pe::before{opacity:0;animation:srcPass 6s var(--ease-enter) infinite}
.st-pe::after{opacity:0;transition:opacity var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.st-pe.hear::before{animation:none;opacity:0}
.st-pe.hear::after{opacity:.9;animation:srcScan 1.1s linear infinite}
@keyframes srcPass{0%{transform:translateY(0);opacity:0}8%{opacity:.7}
 40%{transform:translateY(163%);opacity:0}100%{transform:translateY(163%);opacity:0}}
@keyframes srcScan{from{transform:translateY(0)}to{transform:translateY(163%)}}
.src-hd{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;min-height:20px}
.src-live{width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;flex:0 0 auto;background:var(--accent);
 animation:srcBreath 6s ease-in-out infinite}
@keyframes srcBreath{0%,100%{opacity:.35;transform:scale(.8)}50%{opacity:1;transform:scale(1)}}
.st-pe.hear .src-live{animation:none;opacity:1;transform:scale(1.15);
 box-shadow:0 0 6px var(--accent)}
.src-land .src-w{display:inline-block;animation:srcWd var(--t-element) var(--ease-out) both;
 animation-delay:calc(var(--i) * var(--t-stagger) * .5)}
@keyframes srcWd{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(.28em);color:var(--accent)}
 60%{opacity:1;color:var(--accent)}}
.src-land::after{content:"";display:inline-block;width:.42em;height:.92em;margin-left:.18em;
 vertical-align:-.1em;background:var(--accent);opacity:0;
 animation:srcCur 1.2s steps(1,end) both;
 animation-delay:calc(var(--n) * var(--t-stagger) * .5 + var(--t-element))}
@keyframes srcCur{0%{opacity:1}25%{opacity:0}50%{opacity:1}75%,100%{opacity:0}}
body.quiet .st-pe::before,body.rm .st-pe::before,body.quiet .st-pe::after,body.rm .st-pe::after{animation:none;opacity:0}
body.quiet .src-live,body.rm .src-live{animation:none;opacity:1;transform:none}
body.quiet .src-land .src-w,body.rm .src-land .src-w,
body.quiet .src-land::after,body.rm .src-land::after{animation:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.st-pe::before,.st-pe::after{display:none}
 .src-live{animation:none;opacity:1}}
/* ---------- Source AI, deeper still. Round LV ----------
   His words: "I want that location to feel like it's alive and innovative,
   like it's a part of the screen that has a different nature to it, and I
   want us to pull elements from the field style, not those techniques per
   se, but aspects of it, to give this a more animated, alive feel." Not the
   Field's canvas, its threads or its wheel, which have no business on a
   page a person is writing in: its aesthetic, in three borrowings, named.

     the breath   the Field's own nodes breathe on a 4.2 second wave, see
                  wheel.js. The same wave now runs under this panel: a soft
                  halo at its edge opens out and draws back on that exact
                  beat, the way a node's own glow does.
     the halo     a node's glow is earned in three stops, never a flat
                  tint: a white cast core, the colour itself, a darker ring
                  at the edge. This halo is the same three stops, in
                  miniature, so the panel reads as lit from inside rather
                  than painted on.
     the register the halo runs brighter and a half beat quicker while
                  Source AI is hearing, :has() reading the class the bar
                  and the dot already carry, so the whole panel answers
                  together rather than three things moving near each other
                  on their own clocks. :has() is already load bearing
                  elsewhere in this file, e.g. html:has(body.snow) above.

   A WRAPPER CARRIES IT BECAUSE .st-pe HAS NO PSEUDO ELEMENT LEFT TO SPEND.
   It already gave both of its own, ::before and ::after, to the passing
   light and the scan above. .st-glow is a plain box around it that bleeds
   a few pixels past .st-pe's own edge, where nothing else is drawn, and
   sits outside what srcPaint rewrites: only innerHTML(#stsrc) changes on
   every key, never its parent, which is the same reason the bar and the
   dot already survive one. Transform and opacity only, so quiet and
   reduced motion hold it lit and still, never a faster version of the same
   wave, and nothing here costs more than a radial-gradient the compositor
   already had to paint once. */
.st-glow::before{content:"";position:absolute;inset:-13px;z-index:-1;pointer-events:none;
 border-radius:calc(var(--r-s) + 13px);
 background:
  radial-gradient(55% 50% at 4% 20%,color-mix(in srgb,#fff 32%,var(--accent)) 0%,transparent 46%),
  radial-gradient(85% 78% at 4% 24%,var(--accent) 0%,transparent 62%),
  radial-gradient(100% 92% at 4% 28%,color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 55%,#000) 0%,transparent 78%);
 opacity:.14;animation:srcHalo 4.2s ease-in-out infinite}
@keyframes srcHalo{0%,100%{opacity:.09;transform:scale(.94)}50%{opacity:.22;transform:scale(1)}}
.st-glow:has(#stsrc.hear)::before{animation-duration:2.8s}
body.quiet .st-glow::before,body.rm .st-glow::before{animation:none;opacity:.16;transform:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.st-glow::before{animation:none;opacity:.16;transform:none}}
/* the question, and the two presses that change it, the Avatar's .avs-ask
   shape. The presses are one group and the group takes the right edge,
   margin-left auto, so it stands at the panel's edge at every width and not
   straight after the question's own measure. */
.src-ask{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:10px}
.src-ask .src-open{flex:1 1 auto;min-width:0}
.src-acts{display:flex;gap:6px;flex:0 0 auto;margin-left:auto}
.src-new{flex:0 0 auto;width:var(--tap);height:var(--tap);border-radius:50%;padding:0;
 border:1px solid var(--edge-2);background:transparent;color:var(--accent);cursor:pointer;
 display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;
 transition:border-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.src-new svg{width:18px;height:18px;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.8;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.src-new:hover{border-color:var(--accent)}
.src-new:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:2px}
body.punch .src-new{border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
.src-open{font-size:22px;line-height:1.3;color:var(--ink);font-weight:400;margin:4px 0 2px;max-width:44ch;
 transition:font-size var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),color var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.src-open.quiet{font-size:15px;color:var(--mid)}
.src-q{font-size:20px;line-height:1.35;color:var(--ink);margin:4px 0 2px;max-width:48ch}
.src-note{font-size:13px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.55;margin:3px 0}
.src-say{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip-path:inset(50%);
 white-space:nowrap;margin:0}
.src-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px 12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:8px}
.src-gauge{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;font-size:13px;color:var(--dim)}
.src-seat{font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600;color:var(--c)}
/* the rung, drawn and never printed. Ten marks; the four it asks at carry
   the seat colour on their edge, so the asking end is visible before
   anything reaches it. */
.src-pips{display:inline-flex;gap:2px}
.src-pips i{width:5px;height:12px;border-radius:1px;border:1px solid var(--edge-2)}
.src-pips i.ask{border-color:var(--c)}
.src-pips i.on{background:var(--c);border-color:var(--c)}
/* stacked, the Story reads journal, Source AI, imprints, release, and the
   tools after. Without this the whole left rail sat between what a person
   wrote and what it found whenever the column was open. The breakpoint is the
   one that stacks the rails. */
@media (max-width:1180px){body.tab-story #lcol{order:1}}

/* ---------- imprints ---------- */
.ip-hd{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;
 margin-bottom:11px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.ip-ctl{display:flex;gap:3px;align-items:center}
.ip-g{background:var(--sunk);border:0;color:var(--mid);border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 padding:8px 13px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12.5px;cursor:pointer;transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out);
 min-height:var(--tap);display:inline-flex;align-items:center}
.ip-g:hover{color:var(--ink)}
.ip-g.on{background:var(--gold);color:var(--on-accent);font-weight:500}
.ip-bh{margin:15px 0 7px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;
 gap:10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);padding-bottom:5px;color:var(--c)}
.ip-bh em{font-style:normal;color:var(--dim);font-weight:400;font-size:12px}
.ip-cloud{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px}
/* where the child sits. one row per pattern: the axis, then the seat and the
   address it is held at, then what it carries. The rule down the left is the
   seat's colour at the same intensity the pill's border takes, so the block
   and the pill it points at are visibly the same treatment. */
.ip-kr{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:8px;padding:5px 0 5px 9px;
 border-left:2px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 88%,transparent)}
/* THE NAME KEEPS THE SEAT'S HUE AND STILL HAS TO BE READ. Straight var(--c)
   put Heart at #5FD5A6 on Glass white's paper, which measures 1.5 to 1 and is
   not text, it is a suggestion of text. Mixed toward the ink instead: the ink
   is dark on the paper lightings and light on the five others, so one rule
   darkens the name on paper and lifts it everywhere else, which is the same
   argument the pill's fill is built on. Measured at this share across every
   seat on all seven: the worst case is stated in the commit. */
.ip-kn{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;font-weight:500;
 color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 46%,var(--ink))}
.ip-ka{flex:1 1 auto;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--mid);font-weight:300}
.ip-kr b{font-weight:400;font-family:var(--num);font-size:12.5px;
 color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 46%,var(--ink));font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
/* the figure beside its one word label. The heading used to carry three
   counts inside one string with two commas in it, which the voice gate
   refused: a label is one word and a comma means a second part. So the
   label is a label and the number is a number, in the product's own
   numeral treatment. */
.ip-n{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;font-weight:500;color:var(--ink);
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;margin:0 12px 0 5px}
.ip-none{font-size:14px;color:var(--dim);line-height:1.7;padding:18px 0;font-weight:300}
.ip-foot{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:16px;padding-top:11px;
 border-top:1px solid var(--edge);line-height:1.6}
.ip-bar{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:center;margin-top:11px}
.ip-sel{flex:1 1 auto;font-family:var(--num);font-size:12.5px;color:var(--gold)}
/* the imprint pill. tinted fill, not full chroma. the number rides inside.
   full chroma belongs to the alarm state alone. */
/* every one of these is a control a person is meant to press, and all of them
   were set from their own padding rather than from the floor. */
.ip{min-height:var(--tap);display:inline-flex;align-items:center;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 17%,transparent);
 border:1.5px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 40%,transparent);
 border-radius:999px;color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:500;
 cursor:pointer;line-height:1.2;display:inline-flex;align-items:baseline;gap:6px;
 transition:all var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.ip b{font-weight:400;opacity:.66;font-family:var(--num);font-size:.84em;color:var(--c)}
.ip:hover{border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 70%,transparent);
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 26%,transparent)}
.ip.on{box-shadow:0 0 0 2px var(--gold)}
.ip.hot{background:var(--alarm-soft);border-color:rgba(255,46,31,.5)}
.ip.hot b{color:var(--alarm);opacity:1}
/* THE CHILD PATTERN. ONE CHANNEL, SEVEN ANSWERS.

   Ruled: a more intense colour of the chakra colour, not a new colour. So the
   only thing that moves is the seat colour's share of the fill, 17 to 46, and
   the border's, 40 to 88. Nothing else: no second hue, no weight, no ring, no
   glow. A second channel would be a second thing for a person to learn and
   the palette would stop being the one that the body map speaks.

   WHY ONE RULE COVERS ALL SEVEN LIGHTINGS. Every seat colour in all three
   palettes is darker than the two paper grounds and lighter than the five
   dark ones, so raising its share moves the pill AWAY from its ground in
   whichever direction that ground lies: darker on Snow and Glass white,
   brighter on Dark, Punch, Glass, Flat and Lumen. That is the owner's ruling
   about the paper lightings, held by arithmetic rather than by a second rule
   that could drift out of step with the first.

   It sits after .hot on purpose. An address at nine is both alarming and a
   child pattern, and the two do not fight over one channel: this owns the
   fill, .hot keeps the numeral, so both read at once. */
.ip.kid{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 58%,transparent);
 border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 88%,transparent)}
/* Punch mixes against the panel rather than against transparency, because
   nothing in Punch is see through. Same two numbers, same direction. */
body.punch .ip.kid{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 68%,var(--panel-2))}
/* pending. the sniffer has found it, nothing is committed. */
.ip.ghost{background:transparent;border-style:dashed;
 border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 45%,transparent);color:var(--mid)}
.ip.ghost b{color:var(--c);opacity:.8}
/* installed. the address is cleared and carrying its coherent opposite.
   that is not load, so it does not read as a zero. */
.ip.inst{background:transparent;border-style:solid;
 border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 52%,transparent);color:var(--mid)}
.ip.inst b{color:var(--c);opacity:1}

