/* THE WEAVE-ALONE MOTION, SECTION 5, DESIGN-character-effects.md 5.
   ui/character.js puts chv-weaving on .chv itself whenever CHV.weave is
   truthy, so every surface this round touches reads off the one fact
   rather than each one being told separately: the wash drops to .35, the
   hero's own rest (ui/character.js's chv-rest, every fill that is not the
   woven block and not a mark) falls to .08, the Field's own Fall number
   from section 3, and the hero's glow goes dark. A rail icon that does not
   carry the weave desaturates to a silhouette, "the rest go saturate(0)",
   leaving its rim and mark exactly where chv-weave-on already rings the
   ones that do. Rim and marks are never inside chv-rest to begin with
   (ui/character.js keeps them in their own paths), so "rim and marks hold"
   needs no rule here: it is already true by construction. */
.chv-weaving .chv-wash{opacity:.35;transition:opacity var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out)}
.chv-weaving .chv-hero .chv-rest{opacity:.08;transition:opacity var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out)}
/* ANIMATION:NONE HAS TO SIT BESIDE OPACITY:0 HERE, not opacity alone. The
   glow and the pool both breathe on chvBreath unconditionally (.chv-stage
   .chv-glow above, .chv-hero-wrap::before in its own rule), and a running
   CSS animation's own keyframe value for a property beats any ordinary
   rule's static value for that same property, specificity or no, short of
   !important. Measured first: opacity:0 alone left the glow breathing
   through .64 to 1 exactly as before, because chvBreath was still the
   thing deciding its opacity every frame. */
.chv-weaving .chv-hero .chv-glow{animation:none;opacity:0;transition:opacity var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out)}
.chv-weaving .chv-hero .chv-hero-wrap::before{animation:none;opacity:0;transition:opacity var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out)}
.chv-weaving .chv-rail .chv-m:not(.chv-weave-on) .chv-svg{
 filter:saturate(0);transition:filter var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out)}
/* THE WALK, PULSE FROM SECTION 3, BUILT FOR REAL HERE. One cell of the
   woven block brightened at a time in the order ui/character.js already
   laid the block down (the Hilbert walk chRead builds it on, --i), a step
   at --step (180ms slack to 90ms taut, wheel.js's own tension carried
   over rather than a second clock) then a rest before the pass repeats.
   filter and not opacity: the fill already carries the tier's own tone
   and its own opacity, and brightening it is a smaller claim than
   replacing it. chv-weaveband-still freezes the cell in place for an
   overexpressed group, "it holds visually still while the mask breathes,"
   DESIGN-character-effects.md 4, which a light reading's own weave always
   is: chOwn only ever shows an over group there. */
.chv-weavecell{animation:chvWalk calc(var(--n,1) * var(--step,180ms) + 600ms) linear infinite;
 animation-delay:calc(var(--i,0) * var(--step,180ms))}
@keyframes chvWalk{0%,100%{filter:brightness(1)}6%{filter:brightness(1.55)}14%{filter:brightness(1)}}
.chv-weaveband-still .chv-weavecell{animation:none}
/* RUNNING HOT, SECTION 4. The alpha of every dash is set inline in
   ui/character.js off frStress itself (zero at RUNHOT_AT, wheel.js, a
   visible step on crossing), so a reduced motion profile that turns this
   animation off still shows the correct picture rather than a blank one:
