    lies level with it in the lower left, round LR, placed by that 12 */
 .fzoom{position:static;order:1;align-self:flex-end;margin:8px 22px 12px 12px}
 .fzoom .fb-grp{flex-direction:row}
 /* Five blocks were absolutely positioned against a stage that is 900 wide
    on a desktop. At 390 they all landed on each other at the bottom of the
    wheel. On a phone the stage is a column: the canvas, then each block in
    flow under it, in the order a person reads them. */
 /* the canvas took its height from flex against a stage that no longer
    constrains it, so it collapsed to zero. it gets an explicit square. */
 /* The stage is a two column grid on a desktop so the compass has a lane of
    its own and nothing sits over the wheel. On a phone there are no lanes: it
    is one column in reading order, which is what the order rules below assume,
    and they are flex rules. So it goes back to being a flex column here. */
 .stage{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;
  overflow:visible;min-height:0;width:100%;box-sizing:border-box}
 .stage>*{max-width:100%;box-sizing:border-box}
 canvas#cv{width:100%}
 /* The stage stops being a box here: it drops its overflow and its min height
    so the wheel can flow down the screen. The tab panels were still absolutely
    positioned against it, so on a phone every one of them rendered into a 36px
    window with the rest clipped below: Knowledge had 5,729 pixels of content in
    it and Games 3,564. They flow too, and the stage grows to hold them. */
 .iq{position:static;inset:auto;overflow:visible;width:100%;padding:14px 0;order:0}
 /* The Energy map is absolutely positioned against the stage exactly like the
    tab panels were, and the same fix was applied to .iq and not to it. It
    rendered 10 pixels tall on every phone with 106 pixels of content in it. */
 .emap{position:static;inset:auto;overflow:visible;width:100%;min-height:0;order:0}
 canvas#cv{flex:0 0 auto;height:min(86vw,390px);order:1}
 /* a rendition takes exactly the wheel's square so the three are compared at
    one size */
 #frend{flex:0 0 auto;height:min(86vw,390px);order:1}
 /* THE COMPASS RENDERED ENTIRELY OFF SCREEN. Measured at 390x844: #conecv at
    y -612, 338 by 591, onScreen false, with its host #cone 10 pixels tall and
    still position:absolute. The tab named Compass showed a person nothing at
    all. Same cause as .iq and .emap, which were both released from absolute in
    this band while #cone was missed, so it is the same one line fix. The card
    inside it is given the square the canvas needs, in flow. */
 #cone.tabmode{position:static;inset:auto;overflow:visible;width:100%;
  padding:14px 0;order:0}
 /* and the ritual, for the same reason and with the same one line. A surface
    absolutely positioned inside a column that has collapsed measures ten
    pixels tall, which is what this measured before the rule existed. */
 body.tab-ritual #rit{position:static;inset:auto;overflow:visible;width:100%;
  padding:8px 0;order:0}
 body.tab-ritual #rit .rit-card{padding:4px 0 18px}
 body.tab-ritual #rit .rel-act{position:static;background:transparent}
 #cone.tabmode .cone-cv{height:min(88vw,400px)}
 /* one scroll on the phone, which is the page's. A column that scrolls inside
    a page that scrolls is two scrolls under one thumb. */
 #cone.tabmode .cone-card,#cone.tabmode .cone-body{height:auto;overflow:visible}
 #cone.tabmode .cone-info{max-height:none;overflow:visible;display:flex;
  flex-direction:column}
 #cone.tabmode .cone-mid{overflow:visible}
 /* THE BODY DREW NO BODY. .pm-svg measured 374 by 0. It is absolute inset:0
    against .pm-well, and .pm-well is flex:1 with min-height:0 inside .emap.
    Releasing .emap from absolute in this band left it auto height, so the flex
    child resolved to zero and the figure had no box to draw in. The well gets
    the height here, which is the one thing the phone band never replaced. */
 .emap{position:relative}
 /* and it grows by the band the Body's bars take across its top, --bmtop,
    written by bmSize in ui/map.js, so a row of circles that wraps to two
    lines at 390 costs the page height and not the figure's. Nought on the
    Masks door, which has no bars. */
 .pm-well{flex:0 0 auto;min-height:calc(min(122vw,470px) + var(--bmtop,0px))}
 /* AND THE BODY'S WELL TAKES BACK WHAT THE PARTS STRIP HAD, round LQ: "scale
    the bodies up because those buttons on the bottom are eating up too much
    valuable real estate." The strip under the well measured 253 pixels at
    390 and is gone to the two shelves inside it. The well grows by 115 of
    them, 470 to 585, which puts the one figure up by a quarter, a fit of
    4.37 to 5.50, and is as far as it goes with the whole well still on a
    390 by 844 screen with the overlays open, measured. The page is 138
    pixels shorter besides. The Masks door keeps its own height. */
 #emap .pm-well{min-height:calc(min(150vw,600px) + var(--bmtop,0px))}
 /* THE DEPTH LADDER RAN 996 PIXELS PAST THE SCREEN AND NOTHING COULD REACH IT,
    measured as body.scrollWidth 1386 against innerWidth 390 while
    documentElement stayed at 390, which hides it completely. The ladder is
    the glass bar's Depth circle now and the bar folds rather than scrolls,
    so what stays is the sub bar's own guard, for Body's row. */
 #subbar{min-width:0;overflow:hidden}
 /* .st-mnote is flex:1 1 100% inside .st-hd, which never wrapped, so the row
    held a full width item beside its siblings and forced the writing column to
    420 in a 376 lane. The basis says it was meant to have its own line. */
 .st-hd{flex-wrap:wrap}
 /* wrapping the row was not enough: the eyebrow and the mic button on its
    first line still had a combined minimum of 420, and a grid track cannot go
    below its content's minimum, so the writing column pushed the whole surface
    to 432. Everything in the chain is allowed to shrink, and the mic keeps its
    tap height while its label wraps. */
 .st-colw,.st-hd>*{min-width:0}
 .st-mic{flex-wrap:wrap;height:auto;padding-top:6px;padding-bottom:6px}
 /* and the lighting switch is a segmented row of six that cannot fit on one */
 #setthemes{flex-wrap:wrap}
 .tl,.howto{position:static;transform:none;text-align:left;max-width:none;
  width:auto;padding:0 14px;margin:0}
 .tl{order:2;margin-top:10px}
 .tl b{font-size:15px}
 /* ACCURACY STAYS OUT OF THIS COLUMN, round LR. It is not one of the five
    blocks that landed on each other: it lies level with zoom, which is the
    last thing in the column, so it keeps its absolute corner and takes only
    zoom's own inset from the side. */
 .acc{left:22px}
 .railtop{left:162px}
 .howto{order:5;margin:12px 0 4px;max-width:none}
 .pol2{order:6;position:static;height:auto;margin:12px auto 0;justify-content:center}
 .pol2 svg{height:190px}
}

/* ============================================================
   THE BOOT. Three seconds, and the timings are the reading.

   Every duration and delay here is deliberate and they are stated rather
   than tuned by eye: the seats are 90ms apart, the ring starts at 0.95s
   while the seats are still arriving, and the whole thing clears at 3.0s.
   The one easing used for arrival is a cubic with an overshoot, because
   follow through is what makes a mark land rather than stop.
   ============================================================ */
/* THE BOOT DOES NOT TAKE THE POINTER.

   It is a full screen sheet for three seconds and it is decoration. Somebody
   who reaches for a control in that time should reach the control, not a
   picture of a spine. It is also what made the browser gates race: they open
   the page, wait under a second, and click through a sheet that is still up. */
/* FIVE SECONDS, AND IT OPENS AND CLOSES ON BLACK. Ruled.

   Two beats of black at each end, which is the oldest device in title work
   and the reason a title sequence feels like an invitation rather than a
   splash: the room goes dark before anything arrives, and dark again before
   the thing you came for. The sequence itself gains two seconds, which is
   spent on the parts that were rushed rather than on holding the end frame
   longer.

   The ground is black, not --bg, because a bookend that fades to the app's
   own ground is not a bookend. */
.boot{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:9999;display:flex;flex-direction:column;
 align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:26px;background:#000;
 pointer-events:none;
 /* THE FADE IS QUICK AND IT IS EASED AT BOTH ENDS. Ruled: "a fade in very
    very quick, a nice little ease in, ease out, so it is just smooth."

    It was .58s on cubic-bezier(.4,0,1,1), which accelerates the whole way and
    never eases out: it arrives at full speed and stops dead. Half the length
    on a curve that is eased at both ends, which is the standard ease in out
    and what smooth actually means. The sheet is gone at 5.26s, a shade sooner
    than the 5.20 it used to end at, so the five beats are bought out of the
    sequence rather than added to a person's wait. */
 /* two seconds longer than it was. Ruled. The figure settles at 4.06 and
    stands still for the rest, which is the beat the brief asked for. */
 /* AND THAT RULING WAS NEVER SEEN, which is why this now says 5s. ui/panels.js
    removed the sheet at a typed 5450ms while this fade was set for 7.02s, so
    the fade never once played and the sheet was cut from black to the app in
    one frame (ET, EZ in TASKS.md). The arrival he has seen and called good
    ends its push at 4.86, so the fade starts at 5.00 and is gone at 5.24, and
    panels.js reads that end off this animation instead of carrying a copy of
    it. --hold is the two second ruling, still open, and moves both. */
 --hold:0s;
 animation:bootOut .24s cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1) calc(5s + var(--hold)) forwards}
/* the opening beat. Everything inside is held back behind it, so the first
   thing on screen is nothing, on purpose, for a third of a second. */
.boot::before{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;background:#000;z-index:2;
 animation:bootIn .46s cubic-bezier(.4,0,1,1) .34s forwards}
@keyframes bootIn{to{opacity:0}}
/* ============================================================
   THE ARRIVAL. Ported from proto/arrival, round ET, refined on EZ and FA.

   EZ, his words on ET's prototype: "very cool animation, more animation with
   the elements ... the timing needs to be exceptional, ease in and out to
   perfection that you can feel. The solid bars come in solid and when they
   can actually come in one at a time as well. The animation timing is good.
   Refine it."

   FA, his words on the five he was then shown side by side: "breath, the
   outside rings I like. Ori, not so much. Bloom, not so much. Ember, the
   secondary lines. Those are pretty cool ... like the second ring with all
   the little hashes. The one I like, needs the kind of like thicker bands.
   To come in with better timing and animation. I think overall, overall,
   this is pretty close. It just needs better like tighter direction."

   So this is a composite, and every part of it is named to the version it
   came from. The base is ET's, "the one you liked". The seven colour wave
   that leaves the centre and travels out past the ring is Breath's. The
   addresses thrown out from the centre as white hot streaks that overshoot
   and cool into their seats' colours are Ember's. Nothing of Orrery or
   Bloom. The length is ET's and not moved, because he has twice said the
   timing is good; what moved is inside it, and all of it is tighter.

   ONE BEAT, NINETY MILLISECONDS, EVERYWHERE A SEAT MOVES. The seats land on
   the spine ninety apart. They used to leave it sixty apart, and the bars
   grew in overlapping so heavily that seven bars read as one smear. Now the
   seats leave on the same ninety, and each seat's bar lands on the same
   ninety, so the eye counts seven of everything, root to crown, and it is
   one rhythm from the first seat to the last bar. Ninety sits inside the
   sixty to a hundred and ten where siblings read as one gesture and can
   still be counted.

   THE BANDS ARE THICKER AND THEY COME IN SOLID. 5.6 units against ET's 3.2,
   the most the room between the seats and the addresses allows. They used
   to draw on from their middle, a line being drawn, which reads as a stroke
   and not as a thing. Now each band is whole from its first frame and it
   lands: it falls in from outside the ring, accelerating, the way a thing
   with mass falls, meets the ring at the same instant its seat arrives from
   the inside, passes by a hair, and settles back. Two things moving toward
   each other and meeting on one frame is what makes the landing read.
   Accelerate into contact, then a rebound, is what separates something
   solid from something floating, and the two curves meet at contact at the
   same speed, measured on their tangents, so the landing has no seam.

   THE CENTRE RELEASES ONCE, AND THREE THINGS LEAVE IT ON THAT FRAME. The
   last band lands, the lens draws in, and as it opens Breath's wave and
   Ember's addresses leave the centre together: one event, the soul going
   from collapsed to expressed, which was EV's brief, and not three.

   EVERY OVERSHOOT IS WRITTEN AS A SPRING, NOT AS ONE BEZIER STACKED ON
   ANOTHER. ET put the landing curve on a keyframe that already overshot, so
   each seat overshot twice. Here the pose is keyframed, peak and trough, and
   the curve between two poses is eased at both ends, because a spring is at
   rest at the top of every swing. The first move off rest is an ease out,
   because it leaves at speed.

   NOTHING SQUASHES. Ruled BT1, 5 September, of this sheet: "get rid of the
   squash and stretch from the intro animation. Doesn't look good." Every
   scale here is uniform.

   THE ORDER, AT --hold 0. The seconds are in the rules below and nowhere
   else, which is the point of writing them there:
     0.00  black, the ruled opening beat
     0.50  the light behind blooms, and drifts until the ring is whole
     0.70  the spine rises root to crown
     0.78  the seven seats land on it, one a beat
     1.62  the column unfurls, a seat a beat, and the spine is spent behind
           them from the root up, so it is consumed rather than switched off
     1.78  the bands fall in, a seat a beat, each meeting its seat on the
           ring at 2.00 plus its beat
     2.42  the centre draws in, the anticipation, as the last band lands
     2.56  it releases: the glass lens opens, Breath's wave leaves it, and
           Ember's addresses are thrown out clockwise from twelve
     2.74  the lens's ring fills clockwise from twelve, the glass bar's ring
     3.00  the gold halo closes round the whole figure
     3.20  the wordmark lands, verbatim, ruled to the pixel
     3.50  every address is home. The figure stands for most of a second.
     4.40  a small draw back, then the push through the ring at three depths
     4.86  black again, the closing beat
     5.00  the sheet's own ruled fade, eased both ends. Gone at 5.24, and
           ui/panels.js reads that end off this animation, not off a number.

   --hold IS THE OPEN QUESTION, NOT A DEFAULT. The two second ruling (the old
   sheet's 7.02) was never once seen, because the old floor cut it at 5.45.
   What he has seen and called good is ET at 5.24. Setting --hold to 2s
   gives the 7.2 version and the removal follows it by itself.

   CURVES are the product's own, and two more that are named where used:
     ease out  .22,1,.36,1     arriving
     ease in   .4,0,1,1        leaving, and falling
     land      .34,1.56,.64,1  landing, overshoot
     in out    .4,0,.2,1       the sheet's fade, ruled
     swing     .45,0,.55,1     between two poses of a spring, at rest at both
     fill      .45,0,.15,1     a ring filling at hero size. The bar's ease out
                               at nine tenths of a second front loads so hard
                               that it reads as a snap and then a crawl; this
                               starts gently and settles long, which is the
                               same fill slowed to a size where it is watched.
   ============================================================ */
/* THE FIGURE IS 85 PER CENT OF WHAT IT WAS, round LP, his words: "for the
   intro animation, the main size, can you scale it down? Fifteen percent. So
   that when it zooms in, it comes in at about the same scale as the actual
   landing screen." It was min(86vw,58vh,600px), and every term is taken down
   by the same fifteenth so the three limits still hand over at the same
   window shapes. Only the figure moves: the wordmark under it is ruled to the
   pixel and keeps its size, and the wash is sized off --S, so it follows.
   Measured in Chromium at 1600 by 1000 with the animations held: the seat
   ring stood 360px across and the push carried it to 521, and now stands 306
   and is carried to 443; the gold halo round it now stands 434 and is carried
   to 629. The Field it lands on draws its three rings at about 745, 610 and
   450, read off the screenshot. At 390 by 844 the stage is 285 against 335,
   and the halo is carried to 364 on a Field canvas 335 high. */
/* THE DEVELOPER SKIP BUTTON. Its own class, not .boot-skip: that name
   belongs to tests/design.js gate 11, which still watches for it to stay
   at zero as the proof that the instruction line ruled out 21 September,
   "get rid of that," has not come back under a different label. This is a
   different control, a developer button and not a sentence to a stranger,
   and it earns its own name rather than borrowing that gate's.

   The ground here is pure black, not a token, same reasoning as the
   sheet's own comment above: a bookend that reads off --bg would drift
   with it. Hardcoded white at low opacity for the same reason .boot-wm's
   own subtitle is hardcoded rather than themed. */
.dev-skip{position:absolute;top:16px;right:16px;pointer-events:auto;z-index:2;
 background:transparent;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.22);
 color:rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:6px 12px;
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12px;min-height:var(--tap);cursor:pointer}
.dev-skip:hover{border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.4);color:rgba(255,255,255,.85)}
.boot.bx{--beat:.09s;--S:min(73.1vw,49.3vh,510px);overflow:hidden;gap:22px}
.bx-stage{position:relative;width:var(--S);height:var(--S);flex:0 0 auto}
/* THE PUSH, WITH ITS BACKSWING. ET went straight out on the ease in. A push
   with no draw back first starts from rest at full speed, which is the one
   thing nothing in nature does; a hundred and twenty milliseconds of drawing
   in by three percent is the anticipation that makes the push read as a
   push. The figure is the middle depth. */
.bx-fig{position:absolute;inset:0;
 animation:bxPush .46s linear calc(4.40s + var(--hold)) forwards}
@keyframes bxPush{
 0%{scale:1;opacity:1;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 26%{scale:.972;opacity:1;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.4,0,1,1)}
 100%{scale:1.45;opacity:0;visibility:hidden}}

/* THE LIGHT, the far depth. Seven pools of the seats' own colour on their
   own bearings, and one of the accent at the centre for the lens to frost.
   It blooms in from further away than it lands, and drifts a few degrees
   slower than anything else. It goes out slowest and least, because it is
   furthest away.

   THE DRIFT STOPS WHEN THE RING IS WHOLE, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CHOICE. It
   is the one expensive thing in the sheet: a layer two and a half figures
   wide, turned every frame. Measured at 1600 wide in this sandbox's software
   compositor, each part switched off in turn: the wash alone takes the boot
   from 60 frames a second to 35, and nothing else in the sheet moves it by
   more than 3. ET drifted it for four and a half seconds, and the last two
   of those moved it under two degrees, which nobody can see and every frame
   paid for. Ended at 2.9s, as the last bar lands, the back half of the sheet
   measured 48 to 55 against 31 to 33, and the first half is unchanged. On a
   machine with a graphics card turning a layer is close to free either way.
   The ground is still alive after it stops: the prism keeps turning under
   the glass, and Breath's wave and Ember's throw both leave after it. */
.bx-wash{position:absolute;left:50%;top:50%;width:calc(var(--S)*2.6);height:calc(var(--S)*2.6);
 translate:-50% -50%;pointer-events:none;opacity:0;
 animation:bxWash 2.1s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) .50s both,
  bxDrift 2.4s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) .50s both,
  bxWashOut .36s cubic-bezier(.4,0,1,1) calc(4.52s + var(--hold)) forwards}
@keyframes bxWash{from{opacity:0;scale:1.3}to{opacity:1;scale:1}}
@keyframes bxDrift{from{rotate:-24deg}to{rotate:0deg}}
/* AND GONE MEANS HIDDEN, NOT ONLY TRANSPARENT. Measured over the handoff,
   4.95s to 5.9s, when the sheet fades over the Field's entrance: a layer at
   opacity zero is still composited every frame, and this one is two and a
   half figures wide. With it still there the handoff ran 38 frames a second
   at 390 with a 100ms worst frame; with it gone, 60 and 33. So every layer
   that has finished leaving ends on visibility hidden. */
@keyframes bxWashOut{from{opacity:1;scale:1}to{opacity:0;scale:1.12;visibility:hidden}}

.bx-svg{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;overflow:visible;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round}

/* THE SPINE. It rises root to crown, the shipped figure's first move, and
   then it is spent from the root up as the seats leave it, over the span of
   their leaving, so the column is consumed by the unfurl rather than faded
   out from under the last three seats still standing on it. */
.bx-spine{stroke:var(--edge-2);stroke-width:1.2;stroke-dasharray:100 100;stroke-dashoffset:100;
 animation:bxSpine .5s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) .70s forwards,
  bxSpent .70s cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1) 1.66s forwards}
@keyframes bxSpine{to{stroke-dashoffset:0}}
@keyframes bxSpent{from{stroke-dashoffset:0;opacity:1}80%{opacity:1}to{stroke-dashoffset:-100;opacity:0}}

/* THE SEVEN. Still .b-seat and still PAL, so design gate 11 reads them as it
   read the old figure. Each lands on the spine on the beat, as a spring:
   out of rest at speed, a peak, a trough, rest. Then it leaves on an arc: the
   group turns and the seat reaches, two properties on two curves, so the
   path is a spiral and not a straight line. The turn draws back six degrees
   first and the reach draws in, which is the anticipation; the reach lands
   past its radius and settles, which is the follow through. */
.bx .b-seat{opacity:0;transform-origin:100px 100px;translate:0 calc(var(--r0) * -1px);
 animation:bxPop .44s both,bxReach .64s both;
 animation-delay:calc(.78s + var(--i) * var(--beat)),calc(1.62s + var(--i) * var(--beat))}
@keyframes bxPop{
 0%{opacity:0;scale:.2;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)}
 45%{opacity:1;scale:1.32;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 75%{opacity:1;scale:.94;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 100%{opacity:1;scale:1}}
@keyframes bxReach{
 0%{translate:0 calc(var(--r0) * -1px);animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 20%{translate:0 calc(var(--r0) * -.86px);animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.34,1.56,.64,1)}
 100%{translate:0 calc(var(--r1) * -1px)}}
.bx-sa{transform-origin:100px 100px;rotate:var(--a0);
 animation:bxTurn .64s both;animation-delay:calc(1.62s + var(--i) * var(--beat))}
@keyframes bxTurn{
 0%{rotate:var(--a0);animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 20%{rotate:calc(var(--a0) + 6deg);animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)}
 100%{rotate:var(--a1)}}
/* SOLID DRAWING. The seven are PAL, exactly, not an approximation of it. The
   boot runs before any script has painted so it cannot read the palette from
   the engine, and a boot in colours that nearly match the product is worse
   than one that does not try. These are canon.js values, copied, and
   tests/design.js gate 11 fails if the two ever part company. */
.b-s1{fill:#D6524C}   /* Root */
.b-s2{fill:#D8924E}   /* Sacral */
.b-s3{fill:#DABF6A}   /* Solar */
.b-s4{fill:#5FD5A6}   /* Heart */
.b-s5{fill:#5EBBDB}   /* Throat */
.b-s6{fill:#7D93E0}   /* 3rd Eye */
.b-s7{fill:#A77EDB}   /* Crown */

/* THE RING. The track comes up first, faint, the way the glass bar's track
   is there before its value: ink at sixteen percent, fieldbar's own. */
.bx-trk{stroke-width:5.6;stroke-opacity:0;animation:bxTrk .26s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) 1.70s forwards}
@keyframes bxTrk{to{stroke-opacity:.14}}
/* THE BANDS. Whole from the first frame, landing one a beat, root to crown.
   Scale is uniform about the centre, so a larger band is the same band
   further out: it falls inward onto the ring. It falls on the ease in for
   the first half, meets the ring at the half, which is 2.00s plus its beat,
   the frame its own seat first reaches its radius from inside; passes it by
   two percent on the ease out, and swings back. Opacity is its own
   animation over the first third, so the band is solid before it lands and
   not fading while it does. */
.bx-arc{stroke-width:5.6;stroke-linecap:round;opacity:0;transform-origin:100px 100px;scale:1.18;
 animation:bxBar .44s both,bxBarOp .14s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) both;
 animation-delay:calc(1.78s + var(--i) * var(--beat)),calc(1.78s + var(--i) * var(--beat))}
@keyframes bxBar{
 0%{scale:1.18;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.4,0,1,1)}
 50%{scale:1;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)}
 70%{scale:.978;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 100%{scale:1}}
@keyframes bxBarOp{from{opacity:0}to{opacity:.94}}

/* THE ADDRESSES, EMBER'S. Still .b-addr, still counted by gate 11 against
   the engine. FA: "Ember, the secondary lines ... the second ring with all
   the little hashes." In Ember every address is thrown out of the centre as
   a white hot streak with a turn on it, flies past its place, springs back,
   and cools to its seat's colour as it shortens into its mark. The same
   here, on ET's geometry, with two differences, both for tighter direction:
   they leave in order, clockwise from twelve, three milliseconds apart, so
   the throw is one sweep and not a scatter; and the flight is keyframed as a
   spring, pose to pose, rather than simulated, so it is the same every time
   and costs nothing to compute.

   Scale and turn are about the centre, so a tick at a tenth of its scale is
   the same tick near the middle: under the glass, where the throw starts.
   It is a full length streak in flight and settles to its seat's resting
   length only as it lands, which is Ember's stretch along the path of
   travel, done on the dash rather than on the shape, so nothing squashes. */
.bx .b-addr line{stroke:var(--c);stroke-width:1.1;opacity:0;stroke-dasharray:100 100;
 transform-origin:100px 100px;scale:.1;
 animation:bxThrow .62s both;animation-delay:calc(2.56s + var(--s) * .003s)}
@keyframes bxThrow{
 0%{opacity:0;scale:.1;rotate:-32deg;stroke:#FFF6E8;stroke-dasharray:100 100;
  animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)}
 10%{opacity:1}
 56%{opacity:1;scale:1.08;rotate:3deg;stroke:var(--c);stroke-dasharray:100 100;
  animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 78%{scale:.986;rotate:-.6deg;stroke-dasharray:var(--pk) 100;
  animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 100%{opacity:.64;scale:1;rotate:0deg;stroke:var(--c);stroke-dasharray:var(--rs) 100}}

/* BREATH'S OUTSIDE RING. FA: "breath, the outside rings I like." The seven
   colours on their own bearings, a soft band eighteen units wide with a
   bright line on it, leaving the centre on the release and travelling out
   through the ring and past the halo, where it dies. Its echo leaves a beat
   later, fainter and not as far, which is the follow through: the first
   ring does not stop the motion, it only leads it. Drawn once at its final
   size and scaled up from a point, so each frame is a transform and an
   opacity, which the compositor does and nothing is painted. */
.bx-wave{position:absolute;left:50%;top:50%;width:calc(var(--S) * var(--wb));height:calc(var(--S) * var(--wb));
 margin:calc(var(--S) * var(--wb) / -2) 0 0 calc(var(--S) * var(--wb) / -2);border-radius:50%;
 pointer-events:none;opacity:0;scale:.04;
 animation:bxWave 1.2s both,bxWaveGo 1.2s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) both;animation-delay:2.56s,2.56s}
.bx-echo{animation-delay:2.68s,2.68s;animation-name:bxEcho,bxEchoGo}
@keyframes bxWaveGo{from{scale:.04}to{scale:1}}
@keyframes bxEchoGo{from{scale:.04}to{scale:.9}}
@keyframes bxWave{
 0%{opacity:0;visibility:hidden;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)}
 8%{opacity:.62;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.33,0,.67,1)}
 100%{opacity:0;visibility:hidden}}
@keyframes bxEcho{
 0%{opacity:0;visibility:hidden;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)}
 8%{opacity:.3;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.33,0,.67,1)}
 100%{opacity:0;visibility:hidden}}

/* THE CENTRE, the near depth. A point of the accent under the lens, so
   through the glass it reads as light rather than as a dot. */
.bx .b-core{fill:var(--accent);opacity:0;transform-origin:100px 100px;
 animation:bxDot .3s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) 2.48s both}
@keyframes bxDot{from{opacity:0;scale:0}to{opacity:1;scale:1}}
.bx-pr{stroke-width:7;stroke-linecap:butt}
/* SOFTENED AT THE SOURCE, NOT ONLY BY THE GLASS. Under the lens's backdrop
   blur the seven segments still came through nearly sharp in Chromium, a
   pinwheel rather than light, because whether a backdrop filter sees an SVG
   sibling depends on the engine's backdrop root. A blur on the prism itself
   looks the same in every engine. It turns in under the glass and keeps
   turning, slowing, until the push: the one thing still moving at the
   centre while the figure stands. */
.bx-prism{filter:blur(2.4px);opacity:0;transform-origin:100px 100px;
 animation:bxPrism .62s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) 2.38s both,
  bxPrismTurn 2.1s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) 3.00s both}
@keyframes bxPrism{from{opacity:0;scale:.2;rotate:-40deg}to{opacity:.85;scale:1;rotate:0deg}}
@keyframes bxPrismTurn{from{rotate:0deg}to{rotate:51deg}}
/* THE LENS, the glass bar's own material at hero size: its ground, its one
   pixel inner highlight and its drop shadow, from .fb-orb in this sheet. It
   carries a backdrop blur the bar itself is not allowed, because the bar sits
   over the Field and gate 13 forbids it there; the sheet is gone before the
   Field is drawn under anything. It draws in before it opens. */
.bx-lens{position:absolute;left:50%;top:50%;width:calc(var(--S)*.19);height:calc(var(--S)*.19);
 margin:calc(var(--S)*-.095) 0 0 calc(var(--S)*-.095);border-radius:50%;
 background:rgba(22,24,32,.40);
 -webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(14px) saturate(1.4);backdrop-filter:blur(14px) saturate(1.4);
 border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.12),0 10px 28px rgba(0,0,0,.28);
 overflow:hidden;opacity:0;scale:.3;
 animation:bxLens .62s 2.42s both,
  bxLensOut .36s cubic-bezier(.4,0,1,1) calc(4.52s + var(--hold)) forwards}
@keyframes bxLens{
 0%{opacity:0;scale:.3;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.45,0,.55,1)}
 22%{opacity:1;scale:.2;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.34,1.56,.64,1)}
 100%{opacity:1;scale:1}}
/* the nearest layer goes furthest: the lens past the figure's 1.45 */
@keyframes bxLensOut{from{scale:1}to{scale:1.6}}
/* the specular: one soft highlight high on the rim, where a light above
   the screen would catch a disc of glass */
.bx-lens::after{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:50%;
 background:radial-gradient(120% 90% at 32% 14%,rgba(255,255,255,.18),rgba(255,255,255,0) 46%)}
/* the lens's ring: fieldbar's track, ink at sixteen percent, and its value in
   the accent with a round cap, from twelve and clockwise */
.bx-ctrk{stroke:rgba(239,237,232,.16);stroke-width:2.2;opacity:0;
 animation:bxFade .3s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) 2.62s forwards}
@keyframes bxFade{to{opacity:1}}
.bx-cval{stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:2.2;stroke-dasharray:0 100;opacity:0;
 transform-origin:100px 100px;rotate:-90deg;
 animation:bxFill .96s cubic-bezier(.45,0,.15,1) 2.74s both}
@keyframes bxFill{0%{opacity:0;stroke-dasharray:0 100}4%{opacity:1}100%{opacity:1;stroke-dasharray:100 100}}
/* THE HALO, gold as ruled, round the whole figure rather than over a head,
   on the same fill curve as the lens so the two closings are one family */
.bx-halo{stroke:var(--au);stroke-width:.8;stroke-dasharray:0 100;opacity:0;
 transform-origin:100px 100px;rotate:-90deg;
 animation:bxHalo .9s cubic-bezier(.45,0,.15,1) 3.00s both}
@keyframes bxHalo{0%{opacity:0;stroke-dasharray:0 100}4%{opacity:.7}100%{opacity:.7;stroke-dasharray:100 100}}

/* THE WORDMARK. The shipped landing, untouched, and a leaving added. */
.bx .boot-wm{animation:bootWm .92s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) 3.20s both,
 bxWmOut .24s cubic-bezier(.4,0,1,1) calc(4.52s + var(--hold)) forwards}
@keyframes bxWmOut{from{opacity:1}to{opacity:0;transform:translateY(-6px)}}
/* ============================================================
   THE COMPASS NAME RAILS. Four axes each side of the figure.

   They flank the drawing rather than sitting under it, because the figure is
   a column of its own and a list under it would push the graph off the fold
   again. Each row is one mirror pair: the quality, the coherent pole, and the
   inversion beneath it in the desaturated twin of the same hue.

   No outline on the row. The seat colour is on the type and on a two pixel
   edge that only appears when the row is the one facing the viewer, which is
   what "lights up as you wheel round" has to mean on a rail that is always
   present.
   ============================================================ */
/* THE PANEL. The stage's own glass, because it lies on the stage's ground
   and takes its tone from it (coneTone), with the rails' metrics: their
   radius, a hairline edge, a lift. Four rows, spread down the pane's height
   so each stands at its own stretch of the figure. */
.cn-pan{position:static;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:space-between;gap:6px;
 min-width:0;padding:12px 8px;border-radius:var(--r);
 background:var(--fb-glass);border:1px solid var(--edge-2);color:var(--mid);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.07),0 8px 22px var(--fb-shade)}
/* a row is a button: the pair of circles, then three lines of type. 44 is the
   tap floor and the two circles make it ninety. The plate that says which
   row faces the viewer is a pseudo element lit by opacity, so what moves as
   the figure turns is an opacity and nothing else. */
.cn-nr{position:relative;isolation:isolate;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;
 width:100%;min-width:0;padding:6px;border:0;border-radius:calc(var(--r) - 6px);
 background:none;color:inherit;font:inherit;text-align:left;cursor:pointer}
.cn-nr::before{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;z-index:-1;border-radius:inherit;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 8%,transparent);opacity:0;
 transition:opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.cn-nr.front::before,.cn-nr:hover::before,.cn-nr:focus-visible::before{opacity:1}
.cn-nr:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:-2px}
.cn-pr{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;flex:0 0 auto}
.cn-tx{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1px;min-width:0}
/* THE POLE'S CIRCLE: the bar's disc, ringed in the axis's own seat token, its
   ring the reading. Its mark is ink when its row faces the viewer and mid
   when it does not, the bar's on and off. */
.cn-or{--c:var(--ax);color:var(--mid)}
.cn-or.inv{--c:var(--axd);color:var(--dim)}
.cn-or .val{opacity:1;transition:none}
.cn-or.inv .trk{stroke:var(--axd);stroke-dasharray:3 3}
.cn-nr.front .cn-or,.cn-nr:hover .cn-or{color:var(--ink)}
.cn-nr.front .fb-orb,.cn-nr:hover .fb-orb{background:var(--fb-glass-on)}
body.punch .cn-nr.front .fb-orb{background:var(--c)}
body.punch .cn-nr.front .fb-gl svg{stroke:var(--on-flat)}
/* the pill rides the lower right of the pair, away from the type, so on the
   right panel, whose circles are on the far side, it rides the lower left */
/* THE TYPE IS THE SEAT'S TOKEN LEANED TOWARD THE INK, ON EVERY GROUND. Measured
   on the first cut against each panel's own glass: Root's name read 4.45 and
   its inversion at the brand's seventy two per cent 2.86, under the 4.5 the
   type needs, and on Glass white the names read 1.72 and 1.47, because the
   seat tokens are made for a dark ground. --ink turns with the glass, so one
   mix does both: 85 per cent token on the dark glass, 50 on the light, which
   measures 5.3 and 5.2 at the worst of the seven seats. The inversion's type
   is the same colour in a lighter weight, since a darker one could not be read;
   what says it is the other pole is its broken ring, its place and its weight. */
.cn-pan{--tm:85%}
.cn-pan.fb-lt{--tm:50%}
.cn-pan .cn-nu{color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ax) var(--tm),var(--ink))}
.cn-pan .cn-nd{color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ax) var(--tm),var(--ink));font-weight:500}
.cn-pan.fb-lt .cn-or{--c:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ax) 60%,var(--ink))}
.cn-pan.fb-lt .cn-or.inv{--c:var(--axd)}
.cn-nms-r .cn-nr{flex-direction:row-reverse;text-align:right;gap:12px}
.cn-nms-r .cn-tx{align-items:flex-end}
.cn-nms-r .cn-or .fb-v{left:auto;right:24px}
/* THE QUALITY, IN SENTENCE CASE AT THE TYPE FLOOR. It was 9px and uppercased,
   which broke two gates at once and both were right. Nine is under the floor
   and this is a string a person reads to use the thing, not a wordmark read
   once. And uppercase as a way of making a label quieter is the habit the
   all caps rule exists to stop: the way to make a line recede is colour and
   weight, which is what it does now. */
.cn-nq{font-size:11px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.02em;
 color:var(--dim);line-height:1.3}
.cn-nu{font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ax);line-height:1.25;
 display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px}
.cn-nd{font-size:11.5px;font-weight:500;color:var(--axd);line-height:1.25;
 display:flex;align-items:center;gap:5px}
/* the icon sits on the figure side of its name, so the pair reads as one
   object, which is how the canvas drew it before the names moved out */
.cn-gl{width:13px;height:13px;flex:0 0 13px;display:block}
/* the same icon on a drill row, where the type is a shade larger */
/* THE FOUR LENSES. One reading each, and each says what it was read off,
   which is what stops a lens being a horoscope. */
/* .cv-legend went with the legend, ruled 25 September (BA9). Why it was
   written and why it went are at its old place in shell/body.html. */
/* ============================================================
   ONBOARDING. Humble and warm, which is a typographic decision
   as much as a verbal one: wider measure, softer ground, and
   nothing on it shaped like a form.
   ============================================================ */
/* NO BACKDROP FILTER, and the design gate is why. A blur over the Field costs
   the wheel its frame rate: measured at 10.9 to 18 frames a second across the
   lightings with one on, against a floor the gate holds. A sheet that makes
   the instrument behind it stutter is a sheet that has already failed, and the
   blur was a nicety. An opaque ground reads as a sheet on its own, and it also
   stops the wheel showing through a card a person is meant to be reading. */
.ob{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:95;display:none;align-items:center;
 justify-content:center;background:rgba(6,6,8,.94);padding:24px;
 transition:opacity var(--t-element) var(--ease-in)}
/* THE HANDOFF FADE, ROUND MP: "there needs to be a transition between the
   last onboarding screen and the field." ui/onboard.js's obClose() adds
   this class, waits OB_LEAVE_MS, then removes the sheet; the fade plays in
   that window. The card itself eases down and out with it, so the sheet
   does not just go transparent in place, it visibly leaves. */
.ob.ob-leaving{opacity:0}
.ob.ob-leaving .ob-card{transform:translateY(6px) scale(.98)}
.ob-card{position:relative;overflow:hidden;width:min(560px,100%);max-height:88vh;
 background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:14px;
 padding:26px 28px 20px;transition:transform var(--t-element) var(--ease-in)}
/* THE CARD SCROLLS ON ITS OWN INNER LAYER, not the card element itself, so
   .ob-wash's own transform/animation is not fighting a scrollable ancestor
   the way it would if overflow:auto sat on .ob-card directly. */
.ob-card>.ob-scroll{max-height:calc(88vh - 46px);overflow:auto}
/* PULLED FROM THE FIELD PAGE, ROUND MP: "redesign this so it's visually
   appealing, see what you can pull from the field page." The boot sheet's
   own .bx-wash draws these same seven colours as a radial wash behind the
   standing figure; this is that same idea, softer and animated slowly,
   behind a card instead of a full screen figure. filter:blur, not
   backdrop-filter: gate 7 in tests/design.js measured a real frame rate
   cost from sampling the Field live through a blur and the fix was to stop
   doing that, not to blur less; this blurs its own painted gradient only
   and never samples anything behind the sheet, so that cost does not
   apply here. */
.ob-wash{position:absolute;inset:-15%;z-index:0;pointer-events:none;
 opacity:.55;filter:blur(30px);
 background:radial-gradient(circle at 12% 18%,rgba(214,82,76,.20) 0,transparent 38%),
  radial-gradient(circle at 88% 12%,rgba(216,146,78,.18) 0,transparent 40%),
  radial-gradient(circle at 92% 58%,rgba(218,191,106,.16) 0,transparent 40%),
  radial-gradient(circle at 72% 94%,rgba(95,213,166,.18) 0,transparent 40%),
  radial-gradient(circle at 22% 92%,rgba(94,187,219,.18) 0,transparent 40%),
  radial-gradient(circle at 6% 58%,rgba(125,147,224,.18) 0,transparent 40%),
  radial-gradient(circle at 48% 46%,rgba(167,126,219,.15) 0,transparent 46%);
 animation:obWashDrift 30s var(--ease-breath) infinite alternate}
@keyframes obWashDrift{from{transform:scale(1) rotate(0deg)}to{transform:scale(1.1) rotate(3deg)}}
/* THE STANDING FIGURE, FAINT, BEHIND EVERY CARD rather than shown once at
   full strength on the welcome screen alone: the same continuity the
   Field gives every tab, one figure the whole product is read against. */
.ob-fig-wm{position:absolute;inset:0;z-index:0;display:flex;align-items:center;
 justify-content:center;opacity:.07;pointer-events:none}
.ob-fig-wm .ob-fig{width:64%;height:auto}
.ob-card>:not(.ob-wash):not(.ob-fig-wm){position:relative;z-index:1}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.ob-wash{animation:none}}
.ob-h{font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;margin:6px 0 14px;font-weight:600;
 letter-spacing:-.012em;color:var(--ink)}
.ob-p{margin:0 0 13px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.62;color:var(--mid);
 max-width:58ch}
.ob-dim{color:var(--dim);font-size:13.5px}
.ob-acts{display:flex;gap:9px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:18px}
.ob-dots{display:flex;gap:6px;justify-content:center;margin-top:18px}
/* LOGIN. Same card as onboarding, .ob-card/.pm-eye/.ob-h/.ob-p/.ob-acts, a
   row per field is all that is new. Its own class rather than the account
   sheet's .avin, which carries no rule of its own anywhere in this file. */
.login-row{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px;margin:0 0 13px}
.login-l{font-size:13px;color:var(--dim)}
.login-row input{background:var(--sunk);border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--ink);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:9px 11px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;
 min-height:var(--tap);width:100%}
.login-row input:focus{border-color:var(--accent)}
/* THE STRONG PASSWORD SUGGESTION AND FORGOT PASSWORD LINK, ROUND MP: "the
   login needs the typical login stuff... I also want us to be able to
   suggest a strong password." .login-pw turns the password row into an
   input plus a button sharing one line; loginSuggestPassword() in
   ui/login.js fills the field and reveals the suggestion as plain text
   underneath, since a hidden suggestion is not one a person can copy. */
.login-pw{display:flex;gap:7px}
.login-pw input{flex:1 1 auto;min-width:0}
.login-pwsug{flex:0 0 auto;background:var(--sunk);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 color:var(--ink);border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:0 12px;font-family:var(--sans);
 font-size:13px;min-height:var(--tap);cursor:pointer}
.login-pwsug:hover{border-color:var(--accent)}
.login-hint{display:block;margin-top:6px;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);
 font-family:var(--num);word-break:break-all}
.login-forgot{display:block;background:none;border:none;color:var(--dim);
 font-size:13px;font-family:var(--sans);text-decoration:underline;cursor:pointer;
 padding:2px 0;margin:-6px 0 4px}
.login-forgot:hover{color:var(--ink)}
/* THE CARD'S OWN RESULT LINE, 30 September, when Log in started asking a real
   server. The status line sits in the top bar under this full screen card, so
   a refusal written only there could not be seen. Same size and same refusal
   colour as #status, so the two read as one message in two places. Empty, it
   takes no room, the way #status:empty does. */
.login-msg{margin:4px 0 0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--mid)}
.login-msg:empty{display:none}
.login-msg[data-kind="fail"]{color:var(--hot,#c0392b);font-weight:500}
/* DEVELOPER OPTIONS, DISCLOSED. A testing control, kept out of the way of
   a stranger meeting the funnel for the first time, round MP: "the login
   screen should give me the options, onboarding and tutorial." */
/* round OT: developer options sit in the lower right of the screen, not in
   the card. The switches open upward from the summary. */
.login-dev{position:fixed;right:18px;bottom:14px;z-index:96;margin:0;border:0;
 padding:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column-reverse;align-items:flex-end;opacity:.7}
.login-dev[open]{opacity:1}
#login{background:#06060a}
.login-dev summary{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);cursor:pointer;
 list-style:none}
.login-dev summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}
.login-dev summary::before{content:'▸ ';font-size:10px}
.login-dev[open] summary::before{content:'▾ '}
.login-sw{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;font-size:13.5px;
 color:var(--ink);margin-top:10px;cursor:pointer}
.login-sw-off{color:var(--dim);cursor:default}
.login-dim{color:var(--dim);font-size:11.5px;margin-left:4px}
.ob-dot{width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--edge-2)}
.ob-dot.on{background:var(--accent)}
/* THE FIGURE ON THE WELCOME. Centred, generous, and the only thing on the
   screen with any size to it. The seats fade up root to crown in a hundred and
   seventy millisecond step, which is the boot's own rhythm halved, so it reads
   as the same creature taking a breath rather than as a second animation.

   Under the type floor question: this carries no text, so it has none to be
   under. Height is capped in vmin as well as pixels because at 390 by 844 a
   300 pixel figure and three lines of copy do not both fit above the fold. */
.ob-fig{display:block;margin:2px auto 20px;width:min(34vmin,168px);
 height:auto;overflow:visible;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round}
.ob-fig-h{stroke:var(--au);stroke-width:1.4;opacity:.8}
.ob-fig-s{stroke:var(--edge-2);stroke-width:1.4}
.ob-fig-d{opacity:0;animation:obDot .52s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) both}
@keyframes obDot{0%{opacity:0;transform:translateY(3px)}100%{opacity:.92;transform:none}}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
 .ob-fig-d{animation:none;opacity:.92}}
.ob-grid{display:grid;gap:1px;background:var(--edge);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r);overflow:hidden;margin:4px 0 2px}
.ob-g{background:var(--panel-2);padding:10px 13px;display:flex;
 flex-direction:column;gap:2px}
.ob-g b{font-size:14px;color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
.ob-g span{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim)}
/* THE TWO WORDS. Large, alone, and with room around them, because the whole
   practice is reading one word slowly. A word in a paragraph is read at the
   speed of the paragraph. */
.ob-word{font-size:34px;line-height:1.1;text-align:center;letter-spacing:-.01em;
 padding:22px 12px;margin:6px 0 12px;border-radius:var(--r);
 background:var(--sunk);font-weight:500}
.ob-word-n{color:var(--dim)}
.ob-word-c{color:var(--ink)}
.ob-seats{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:7px;margin:10px 0 2px}
.ob-seat{min-height:var(--tap);padding:6px 13px;border-radius:var(--r-s);
 border:1px solid var(--edge);background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--mid);
 font:inherit;font-size:13px;cursor:pointer}
.ob-seat:hover{color:var(--ink);border-color:var(--edge-2)}
.ob-seat.on{background:var(--c,var(--accent));border-color:var(--c,var(--accent));
 color:var(--on-accent);font-weight:600}
@media(max-width:640px){
 .ob{padding:12px}.ob-card{padding:20px 18px 16px}.ob-h{font-size:20px}
 .ob-word{font-size:27px;padding:17px 10px}}
.s-lens{display:grid;gap:1px;background:var(--edge);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r);overflow:hidden}
.s-ln{background:var(--panel);padding:9px 12px 10px;display:flex;
 flex-direction:column;gap:1px}
.s-ln-t{font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.11em;color:var(--dim);font-weight:600}
.s-ln-a{font-size:14px;color:var(--ink);font-weight:600;line-height:1.3}
.s-ln-b{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--mid);line-height:1.4}
.s-ln-c{font-style:normal;font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:2px}
.s-axes{margin-top:18px;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r);
 background:var(--panel);padding:14px 18px 6px}
.s-axes .s-p{margin:0 0 12px}
/* INTEGRITY OVER TIME, FULL WIDTH, under both columns. Ruled. A line five
   years long inside a column is a scribble. */
.s-ig{margin-top:22px;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r);
 background:var(--panel);padding:14px 16px 12px}
.s-ig-h{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;
 gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:10px}
.s-ig-sp{display:flex;gap:6px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.s-ig-g{display:block;width:100%;height:120px}
.s-ig-f{display:flex;align-items:baseline;justify-content:space-between;
 margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;color:var(--dim)}
.s-ig-f b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:600}
.s-gl-s{display:block;font-style:normal;font-size:11px;font-weight:400;
 color:var(--dim);letter-spacing:.01em;margin-top:1px}
.ad-gl{width:14px;height:14px;flex:0 0 14px;display:inline-block;
 vertical-align:-2px;margin-right:6px;opacity:.82}
/* THE PANE IS THE CENTRE OF THREE, round OJ. A panel, the pane, a panel, as
   the Field is composed: the picture in the middle and a rail each side. The
   figure keeps the pane to itself, so the canvas gets no lane for the names
   and no padding to keep the drawing out from under them. */
.cone-fig{position:relative;display:grid;
 grid-template-columns:minmax(156px,188px) minmax(0,1fr) minmax(156px,188px);
 column-gap:10px;align-items:stretch}
.cone-fig>.cn-nms-l{grid-column:1;grid-row:1 / span 2}
.cone-fig>.cn-cvw{grid-column:2;grid-row:1}
.cone-fig>.cn-nms-r{grid-column:3;grid-row:1 / span 2}
.cone-fig>.cone-key{grid-column:2;grid-row:2}
/* A TALL FIGURE IN A NARROW PLACE: stacked. ui/cone.js coneFit puts .stk on
   the figure when the window is under 900 or the pane would be under 760
   once the panels had their share, which is a left column opened at 1280 as
   much as it is a phone. The overlays leave the canvas for the row above it,
   in flow and first, so they can never print over the figure or a name, the
   defect EP in TASKS.md was; the key follows the figure; and the panels go
   under both, in two columns each, the right one read the left's way. */
.cone-fig.stk{display:flex;flex-direction:column}
.cone-fig.stk .cn-cvw{display:flex;flex-direction:column;order:0}
.cone-fig.stk .cn-top{position:static;order:-1;margin:0 0 8px;flex-wrap:wrap}
/* the bar's groups and the capsule share one row where they fit: the fold,
   then the overlays, then the capsule at the far end; the seven seat circles
   are three hundred and forty four across and take the row under them */
.cone-fig.stk .cnbar{display:contents}
.cone-fig.stk .cnbar .fb-tog{order:0}
.cone-fig.stk .cnbar>.fb-grp{order:1}
.cone-fig.stk .cnview{order:2;margin-left:auto}
.cone-fig.stk .cnbar>.cn-seatgrp{order:3;flex:1 0 100%;justify-content:space-between}
.cone-fig.stk .cone-key{order:1}
.cone-fig.stk .cn-nms-l{order:2;margin-top:10px}
.cone-fig.stk .cn-nms-r{order:3;margin-top:10px}
.cone-fig.stk .cn-pan{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:6px}
.cone-fig.stk .cn-nms-r .cn-nr{flex-direction:row;text-align:left}
.cone-fig.stk .cn-nms-r .cn-tx{align-items:flex-start}
.cone-fig.stk .cn-nms-r .cn-or .fb-v{left:24px;right:auto}
/* the card's own title is the tab's name over again, and the Field and the
   Body carry none: the pane is the figure and what changes it */
#cone.tabmode .cone-hd{display:none}
.boot-wm{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:4px;opacity:0;
 animation:bootWm .92s cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1) 3.20s both}
@keyframes bootWm{0%{opacity:0;transform:translateY(8px)}100%{opacity:1;transform:none}}
.boot-wm .bn{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:23px;font-weight:400;
 letter-spacing:.075em;color:var(--sky);line-height:1;position:relative}
/* THE SAME DRAWN MARK AS THE BAR, at the study's boot card pin: 131.1 by 27.2
   pixels, the ascender at 26 against the 23px type it replaces. In em of
   that 23px, so the card's one size still decides it. */
.boot-wm .bn .wm{display:block;width:5.7em;height:1.1826em;overflow:visible}
.boot-wm .bn .wm-d{fill:var(--um)}
/* THE SUBTITLE IS NOW A PAIR, so it is laid out as one. "Powered by" is
   quieter and untracked, because tracking is what makes a name read as a
   logotype and this half is a sentence. The name keeps the tracking and takes
   the case from here rather than from the markup, for the reason written
   beside the wordmark above: the gate that bans all caps copy is correct, and
   a logotype answers it with styling rather than with an exception. */
/* AND IT IS TWO ROWS, NOT A ROW AND A HANGING QUALIFIER. Ruled 20 September,
   reversing the three column version directly above it: "put the word powered
   by above the word Source OS, and drop it down thirty pixels so it is
   standing on its own."

   Both rows centre on the wordmark, which is what the earlier ruling was
   protecting when it said centre the name: with the qualifier stacked above
   rather than beside, centring the pair and centring the name are the same
   thing, so the three columns are gone and nothing is lost by their going.

   Thirty pixels is taken literally, 8 to 38, because the instruction says what
   it is for: standing on its own means the gap to the wordmark is larger than
   the gap inside the pair, and 38 against 3 reads as two objects rather than
   one block of four words. */
.boot-wm .bs{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:9px;font-weight:500;
 line-height:1;margin-top:38px;
 display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:15px}
/* EQUALLY SPACED, FIFTEEN PIXELS APART. Round JZ, his words: "Powered by
   source OS needs to be equally spaced. But 323232 hex and space 15 pixels
   apart." Both lines carry the same tracking, and the gap between them is
   15px where it was 3, unchanged since; the trailing letter space is taken
   back on both, so each centres on its own glyphs.

   THE GREY RE-STATED, ROUND KM. #323232 on this card's black ground measured
   1.66 to 1, named to him as a near whisper when it shipped. He came back
   saying the line reads too tight and too pale and gave 343434 this time,
   a hex one shade off 323232 and still under 1.7 to 1, so this alone likely
   will not read as fixed. Taken exactly as given regardless: if it still
   reads pale against this ground, that is the contrast asked about at
   shipping and not yet ruled on, not a new defect in the 15px gap, which
   measures true above. */
.boot-wm .bs-by{letter-spacing:.19em;margin-right:-.19em;color:#343434;font-weight:400}
.boot-wm .bs-nm{letter-spacing:.19em;margin-right:-.19em;color:#343434;
 text-transform:uppercase}

/* THE BOOT CARD'S CSS DOTS ARE RETIRED WITH THE BAR'S. They carried their own
   centring correction, measured to .443 of the glyph against a .444 target;
   the drawn mark has its dots on the u by construction, which the study
   named as closing that defect rather than carrying it forward. */
/* THE WAY OUT, SAID QUIETLY AND LATE. It arrives at 1.5s, once the sequence
   has begun and long before the 5.2s it would otherwise run to, so a person
   who does not want the overture is never held by it without knowing. It is
   dim because it is an instruction and not an invitation, and it is not a
   button: the gesture is anything at all, and drawing a button would be
   narrower than the truth as well as another tap target on a first screen. */
/* THE RULE ITSELF IS GONE, 21 September. The line is not drawn any more, so
   the class, its keyframe and its reduced motion case came out with it rather
   than being left behind as dead style that the next reader has to prove is
   dead. */
/* THE OSCILLATING BAND'S MOVING PARTS. The marker, its ring and its number ride
   in one group so a frame to frame move is a single transform rather than four
   geometry writes. will-change lives here rather than inline because a class
   that appears in the markup and in no stylesheet is a class nobody can find. */
.pol2-mv{will-change:transform}
/* the swing pill and the crossbar riding inside it join the same rule. The
   pill sits over the button's own hit area, and a decoration that eats the
   press is the same defect as a control with no affordance. */
.pol2-mk,.pol2-dot,.pol2-ring{pointer-events:none}
@keyframes bootOut{to{opacity:0;visibility:hidden}}
/* the beat is motion, so somebody who asked the machine to stop moving does
   not get five of them. Same rule as everything else in this sheet: the end
   state, not a faster version. */
/* and every part of the arrival stops with it. .boot.bx is more specific
   than the reduced motion .boot rule below, so without this it would start
   animations nobody sees; panels.js removes the sheet at once in this case. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.boot.bx,.bx *{animation:none!important}}
/* THE TRADEMARK. Not registered yet and it goes in anyway, ruled. Superscript
   at a third of the name, tracked off the final d rather than after the
   tracking the logotype adds, or it floats away from the word. */
/* AGAINST THE DRAWING, NOT A BASELINE. The mark has no text baseline to ride
   on, so the superscript is placed: its top level with the top of the d, the
   tallest stroke, a small step off the d's right edge. Absolute, so it no
   longer takes width in the row and the mark centres on itself alone. */
.boot-wm .btm{position:absolute;left:100%;top:0;font-size:.34em;font-weight:500;
 line-height:1;letter-spacing:0;margin-left:.3em;color:var(--sky);opacity:.72}
/* the body says the boot is over. Nothing depends on it yet and it is not
   decoration: it is the hook a later surface uses to hold its own entrance
   until the instrument is actually on screen. */
body.booted{--boot-done:1}
/* REDUCED MOTION GETS THE END STATE, not a faster animation. Somebody who
   has asked the machine to stop moving has not asked it to move less. */
/* the tab entrance. six pixels, one context step. see setTab for why the
   class is removed and the layout read before it is put back. */
@keyframes tabIn{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(6px)}
 to{opacity:1;transform:none}}
.tabin{animation:tabIn var(--t-context) var(--ease-out) both}
/* PAINT TO SELECT. A region is nearly invisible until it is pointed at, which
   is what makes the figure a surface to paint on rather than a diagram with
   nine boxes drawn on it. The selected one holds a low wash so the person can
   see what they told it without the wash competing with the field underneath. */
.pm-pr{fill-opacity:0;stroke-opacity:0;stroke-width:.4;cursor:pointer;
 transition:fill-opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),
  stroke-opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.pm-pr:hover{fill-opacity:.10;stroke-opacity:.55}
.pm-pr.on{fill-opacity:.14;stroke-opacity:.78}
/* THE LADDER. The record, on the compass. The streak is the one number a
   person checks, so it is the one thing here at display size, and it is the
   only place in the product where a figure is allowed to be that large outside
   a reading. Everything under it is a row. */
.ld{margin-top:18px;padding-top:16px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.ld-streak{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:9px;margin:8px 0 2px}
.ld-n{font-family:var(--num);font-size:42px;font-weight:300;line-height:1;
 color:var(--dim);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.ld-streak.live .ld-n{color:var(--ink)}
.ld-u{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;color:var(--dim)}
.ld-p{margin:6px 0 0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--mid);max-width:62ch}
.ld-led{margin-top:14px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(190px,1fr));
 gap:6px 16px}
.ld-r{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;gap:12px;
 padding:7px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);font-family:var(--sans);
 font-size:13px;color:var(--mid)}
.ld-r b{font-family:var(--num);font-weight:500;color:var(--ink);
 font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.ld-mh{margin-top:18px}
.ld-marks{margin-top:8px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(232px,1fr));
 gap:8px}
.ld-m{display:grid;grid-template-columns:34px 1fr;grid-template-rows:auto auto;
 gap:2px 11px;align-items:center;padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid var(--edge);
 border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--panel-2)}
.ld-mi{grid-row:1/3;width:34px;height:34px;display:flex;align-items:center;
 justify-content:center;border:1.5px solid var(--c);border-radius:50%}
.ld-mi svg{width:19px;height:19px;fill:none;stroke:var(--c);stroke-width:1.7;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.ld-mn{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;color:var(--ink);align-self:end}
.ld-md{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--dim);
 align-self:start}
.ld-acc{margin-top:16px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.ld-on,.ld-off{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px}
.ld-on{color:var(--mid)}
.ld-off{color:var(--ink)}
.ld-next{margin:14px 0 0;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--mid);max-width:62ch}
.ld-next b{color:var(--ink);font-weight:500}
/* an inferred chip. The seat was read, the address was not named by the
   person's words, so the chip is quieter and its label is the child emotion. */
.ip.infer{border-style:dashed}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
 .boot{animation:none;opacity:0;visibility:hidden}}

/* THE NO SCRIPT NOTICE. It sits above the boot sheet, because the sheet fades
   itself on a CSS animation and would otherwise uncover an empty shell and let
   a person believe that is the product. Plain, high contrast, and it depends on
   no token a script might have been going to set. */
.nojs{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:9999;display:flex;flex-direction:column;
 align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:14px;padding:32px;
 background:#0C0D12;color:#E8E6E1;font-family:'Inter',system-ui,sans-serif;
 text-align:center}
.nojs h1{margin:0;font-size:22px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:-.01em}
.nojs p{margin:0;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.65;color:#94908A;max-width:54ch}
/* THE STORY ON THE SUMMARY. A word that named a behaviour is bold and carries
   its seat's colour, the same as it did in the box while it was typed. */
.s-told{margin-bottom:16px}
.s-told-e{padding:12px 14px;border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);
 background:var(--panel-2);margin-top:8px}
.s-told-e time{display:block;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:11px;
 color:var(--dim);margin-bottom:5px}
.s-told-e p{margin:0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ink);max-width:68ch}
/* A NAMED THING KEEPS ITS FAMILY'S COLOUR INSIDE A PARAGRAPH. .s-p b is
   (0,1,1) and .s-w was (0,1,0), so every bold in the reading lost its seat to
   the plain ink rule and the markup carrying --c was correct while nothing on
   screen showed it. Scoped to match, so it wins on order rather than on
   weight. */
.s-w,.s-p b.s-w,.s-told b.s-w{color:var(--c);font-weight:600}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{transition:none!important;animation:none!important}}

/* ============================================================
   THE ACCOUNT AREA
   ============================================================ */
/* THE RAILS DO NOT RENDER HERE. Two passes measured the same thing from
   opposite ends: 98 simultaneous choices of which 52 are the rail and 12 are
   settings, and 69 percent of the phone scroll spent on the instrument the
   person navigated away from. This is one rule and it is the largest single
   reduction available anywhere on this surface. */
body.tab-settings .mid{grid-template-columns:1fr}
body.tab-settings .mid .col{display:none}
.ac-wrap{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;width:100%;
 max-width:980px;margin:0 auto;padding:4px 2px 28px}
.ac-hd h2{margin:4px 0 0}
.ac-body{display:grid;grid-template-columns:228px 1fr;gap:18px;align-items:start}
/* 228 and not the 240 the precedent uses, because this product's own left rail
   is 302 and an index near that width reads as the instrument rail returning. */
.ac-ix{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;position:sticky;top:0}
.ac-ixb{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;width:100%;min-height:var(--tap);
 padding:10px 12px;background:transparent;border:1px solid transparent;
 border-radius:var(--r-s);color:var(--mid);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;
 font-weight:400;text-align:left;cursor:pointer;
 transition:background var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.ac-ixb:hover{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink)}
.ac-ixb.on{background:var(--panel-2);color:var(--ink);
 border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 40%,var(--edge))}
.ac-ixb.on .ac-gl{color:var(--c)}
.ac-gl{width:20px;height:20px;flex:0 0 auto;display:grid;place-items:center;color:var(--dim)}
.ac-gl svg{width:var(--icon-nav);height:var(--icon-nav);fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.5;
 stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.ac-pane{min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px}
.ac-ph{font-size:20px;font-weight:400;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:.005em;
 padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
body.punch .ac-ph{border-bottom-color:var(--panel-2)}
.ac-lead{margin:0;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ink);
 padding:13px 15px;border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--panel-2);
 border-left:3px solid var(--c)}
.ac-grp{border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-s);background:var(--panel);
 overflow:hidden}
body.punch .ac-grp{border-color:transparent;background:var(--panel-2)}
.ac-gh{padding:11px 15px 10px;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim);
 letter-spacing:.06em;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
body.punch .ac-gh{border-bottom-color:transparent}
/* A ROW IS 56 AND NOT 44. Forty four is the floor a finger needs, not a target
   for a full width list row, and the rows this replaced measured 35. */
.ac-row{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;
 min-height:56px;padding:9px 15px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge)}
.ac-row:last-child{border-bottom:0}
body.punch .ac-row{border-bottom-color:var(--panel)}
.ac-rl{font-size:14px;color:var(--ink);font-weight:400;min-width:0}
.ac-rl em{display:block;font-style:normal;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--dim);margin-top:3px}
.ac-rv{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--mid);text-align:right;min-width:0}
.ac-rv.num{font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink)}
/* A STUB IS NOT A DISABLED CONTROL. Full opacity, never dimmed, because dim
   reads as "you may not" and the truth is "not yet". It takes no press. */
.ac-stub{cursor:default}
.ac-stub .ac-rv{color:var(--dim);font-style:normal}
.ac-act{gap:12px}
.ac-edit input{background:var(--sunk);border:1px solid var(--edge);color:var(--ink);
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:9px 11px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;
 min-height:var(--tap);max-width:260px}
.ac-edit input:focus{border-color:var(--accent)}
.ac-sw{width:46px;min-width:46px;height:var(--tap);flex:0 0 auto;background:transparent;
 border:0;cursor:pointer;display:grid;place-items:center;padding:0}
.ac-sw::before{content:'';position:absolute;width:0;height:0}
.ac-sw i{display:block;width:42px;height:24px;border-radius:12px;background:var(--sunk);
 border:1px solid var(--edge-2);position:relative;
 transition:background var(--t-element) var(--ease-out),border-color var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.ac-sw i::after{content:'';position:absolute;top:2px;left:2px;width:18px;height:18px;
 border-radius:50%;background:var(--dim);
 transition:transform var(--t-element) var(--ease-land),background var(--t-element) var(--ease-out)}
.ac-sw.on i{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 34%,var(--sunk));border-color:var(--accent)}
.ac-sw.on i::after{transform:translateX(18px);background:var(--accent)}
.ac-gf{padding:11px 15px 13px;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--dim);
 background:var(--sunk);border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
body.punch .ac-gf{border-top-color:transparent;background:var(--panel)}
/* DESTRUCTIVE IS A DECISION, NOT AN ALARM. Deleting on purpose is not
   something being wrong, so it does not wear the alarm colour. */
.btn.dgr{color:var(--bad);border-color:color-mix(in srgb,var(--bad) 52%,var(--edge))}
.btn.dgr:hover{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--bad) 14%,transparent);color:var(--bad)}
.ac-seg{margin:11px 15px 13px}
.ac-grp .dens-list{margin:11px 15px 13px}
@media (max-width:1000px){
 .ac-body{grid-template-columns:1fr}
 .ac-ix{position:static;flex-direction:row;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px}
 .ac-ixb{width:auto;flex:1 1 140px}}

/* ---------- composing something to send ---------- */
.ob-qs{margin:14px 0 4px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:13px}
.ob-lens{font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim);letter-spacing:.06em;
 margin-top:6px;padding-top:11px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.ob-qs .ob-lens:first-child{margin-top:0;padding-top:0;border-top:0}
.ob-qt{font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--ink);margin-bottom:7px;font-weight:300}
.ob-as{display:flex;gap:5px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.ob-a{min-height:var(--tap);padding:8px 13px;border-radius:var(--r-xs);
 border:1px solid var(--edge);background:var(--sunk);color:var(--mid);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;cursor:pointer;
 transition:background var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out),color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.ob-a:hover{background:var(--panel);color:var(--ink)}
.ob-a.on{background:var(--accent);border-color:var(--accent);color:var(--on-accent);font-weight:500}
.ob-f{margin:16px 0 4px}
.ob-f label{display:block;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--dim);
 letter-spacing:.06em;margin-bottom:6px}
/* SAID BEFORE THEY TYPE. Always these words, and never below the field. */
.ob-warn{margin:0 0 8px;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--sacral);line-height:1.5}
.ob-f textarea{width:100%;background:var(--sunk);border:1px solid var(--edge);
 color:var(--ink);border-radius:var(--r-xs);padding:11px 12px;font-family:var(--sans);
 font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;resize:vertical;min-height:110px}
.ob-f textarea:focus{border-color:var(--accent);outline:none}

/* ============================================================
   THE PHONE, GF in TASKS.md, 27 September. His feedback from his own phone
   on the packed build, every item of it "on mobile by the way", so every
   rule here is under the phone width and the desktop is not touched.

   Last in the sheet on purpose. Several of these narrow a rule written
   earlier at the same specificity, .pol2's display and the dock's rings
   among them, and at equal weight the later rule is the one that holds.
   ============================================================ */
/* above the phone width the strip still fits, so the fold, the main menu's
   lighting and the loader circle do not exist there */
.navtog,.navlight,#pwrap{display:none}
.navth{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:4px}
.navth button{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;min-height:var(--tap);padding:0 11px;
 border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:var(--r-xs);background:transparent;color:var(--mid);
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;cursor:pointer;text-align:left}
.navth button svg{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.6;stroke-linecap:round;
 stroke-linejoin:round;flex:0 0 auto}
.navth button[aria-pressed=true]{background:var(--gold);border-color:transparent;
 color:var(--on-accent);font-weight:500}
/* THE NAMES ON ONE LINE. His words: "the names are really large on here too,
   they're wrapping onto a second line." The system picker set them in its own
   type across two lines; here each is the bar's 13 pixels on one line, and a
   name longer than the panel is cut with an ellipsis rather than given a
   second row. The row keeps the 44 pixel tap floor. */
.pload{max-height:min(62vh,480px);overflow:auto;padding:8px 8px 6px}
.pl-g{font-family:var(--num);font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);padding:9px 8px 3px}
.pl-i{display:block;width:100%;min-height:var(--tap);padding:0 10px;border:0;
 border-radius:var(--r-xs);background:transparent;color:var(--ink);text-align:left;
 font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;line-height:var(--tap);cursor:pointer;
 white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis}
.pl-i:hover{background:var(--panel-2)}
.pl-i[aria-pressed=true]{color:var(--accent);font-weight:500}
/* a held finger on a control opens its definition, ui/tip.js, and the
   system's own callout and selection would otherwise open over it */
@media (pointer:coarse){button,[role=button]{-webkit-touch-callout:none;
 -webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none}}
@media (max-width:720px){
 /* THE BAR IS TWO ROWS. The wordmark on the left, and on the right the
    loader, help and the person, together, as he placed them: "the profile on
    the upper right hand side, the kind of questionnaire help, whatever, right
    next to that", and the loader "on a button next to help, circular, small,
    just like the profile and the help button". Under them the folded tab. */
 .top{position:relative;gap:6px;row-gap:6px;padding:6px 10px}
 .top .brand{order:0}
 .top .spacer{order:1}
 .top #pwrap{display:block;order:2;position:static}
 .top .srch{order:3}
 .top #profbtn{order:4}
 .top #ploadbtn,.top #srchbtn,.top #profbtn{width:var(--tap);height:var(--tap);
  min-width:var(--tap);padding:0;border-radius:50%;justify-content:center}
 /* the loader's list hangs from the bar, under the first row, and is as wide
    as the bar, so a name has the whole width before it is cut */
 .top .pload{top:calc(var(--tap) + 12px);left:8px;right:8px;min-width:0}
 /* the picker and its label leave the bar for the circle above */
 .top .toplab,.top #psel{display:none}
 /* the strip is the main menu here, where the names are the point, so the
    fold's toggle leaves and a folded strip opens with its names */
 .top .tabnames-i{display:none}
 .top .tabnames-i:not(:checked)~#tabbar .tabtop .n{position:static;width:auto;height:auto;
  overflow:visible;clip:auto;clip-path:none}
 /* UNDO WAS HIDDEN ON A PHONE FIRST, on his words: "Undo, I've never used it,
    and I don't see the purpose of it right now, so let's hide that in the
    code." Round JZ carried it to every width, so the one rule is on
    .histpair itself, above, and not repeated here. */
 /* the lighting menu leaves the bar for the main menu's lighting section */
 .top #lightwrap{display:none}
 /* THE FOLDED BAR IS A TAB. The surface's name in the pressed tab's accent
    and weight, on the strip's hairline, with the chevron saying it opens. */
 .top .navtog{display:flex;order:5;flex:1 1 100%;justify-content:space-between;
  border:0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:0;background:transparent;
  color:var(--accent);font-weight:500;padding:8px 11px}
 /* .n is also the product's figure class and sets ink on itself, so the name
    takes the tab's accent explicitly or it reads as an unpressed tab */
 .top .navtog .n{color:inherit}
 .top .navtog .chev{width:12px;height:12px;opacity:.7;transition:transform var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
 .top.navopen .navtog .chev{transform:rotate(180deg)}
 /* the strip itself is the menu's tabs, folded away until the bar opens,
    then three to a row. !important because its display is written inline. */
 .top #tabbar{order:6;display:none!important}
 .top.navopen #tabbar{display:grid!important;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));
  flex:1 1 100%;overflow:visible;border-bottom:0;mask-image:none;-webkit-mask-image:none}
 .top.navopen #tabbar .tabtop{justify-content:center}
 /* the sections are the main menu's headings here, KC, so every tab is one
    press from anywhere; the first tier's own row leaves the phone bar */
 .top .secbar{display:none}
 .top.navopen #tabbar .tabgrp-hd{display:block;grid-column:1/-1;font-family:var(--num);
  font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);padding:8px 2px 0}
 .top.navopen #tabbar .tabgrp:first-child .tabgrp-hd{padding-top:0}
 /* the folded bar names the section, quietly, then the surface */
 .top .navtog .navsec{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:9px}
 .top .navtog #navsec{color:var(--dim);font-weight:400;font-size:13px}
 /* the search opens across the bar under the first row, where it has the
    width a phone does not have beside the circles */
 .top .srch{position:static}
 .top .srch.open .srch-q{position:absolute;left:10px;right:10px;top:calc(var(--tap) + 12px);
  width:auto;z-index:5}
 .top.navopen .navlight{display:block;order:7;flex:1 1 100%;padding:6px 2px 4px}
 .top #status{order:8}

 /* THE POLES GO INTO THE PICTURE. His words: "The heaven and hell, that's
    clearly causing a problem, on both of these right now it shows up in the
    very bottom of the square. I want you to move that inside of the circle,
    or CQDQ on the square one, and then make the CQDQ a little bit smaller."
    Measured at 390: the strip sat under the picture as a 190 pixel column and
    its pitchfork landed on the stage's bottom edge. The halo and the pitchfork
    are drawn inside the core now, above CQ and below DQ, by frCore in
    ui/rings.js on Frames and Dial and by solCore in ui/wheel.js on the wheel,
    the lit one being where the field is heading, and they are doors to the
    same two drills the strip's ends opened. So the strip goes on a phone. */
 .pol2{display:none}

 /* ROOT ENERGETICS' READINGS ARE SQUARE BUTTONS ON A PHONE. His words: "I
    want these to be square buttons, these circles are taking up too much
    space", and in the round after: "make those uniform and make those square
    ... flush left to right." Each reading keeps its row, DQ, CQ and accuracy
    over the four that move through a person, the two rows EZ ruled. Every
    button in a row is the same width, the rows run from the rail's left edge
    to its right, and all seven are one height, one edge and one radius. The
    ring goes, because the figure beside it says the same thing in a quarter
    of the room; the letters or the mark stay as the button's name.
    ACCURACY LEFT THE FIRST ROW FOR THE STAGE, round LR in TASKS.md, so that
    row is two columns, DQ and CQ, still flush left to right. On the stage it
    keeps its ring, as zoom beside it keeps its circles. */
 .fdock{gap:6px}
 .fdock .kb:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:2px}
}

/* ============================================================
   THE LIVE RAIL, round OJ. ui/railtiles.js says what is wired to what; this
   sheet says how it looks. One grammar, taken from the Field and not from
   the rail: a thing that is acting wears its seat's colour as a ring that
   breathes on the Field's own 4.2 second wave (--t-breath), a thing that is
   idle wears nothing, and the connection between them is a thread with a
   pulse on it, which is what every connection on the wheel already is.
   Ring, never fill, the rule the icons keep. Opacity and transform only: a
   still Field costs the page nothing it did not cost before.
   ============================================================ */
@keyframes rlring{0%,100%{opacity:.04}50%{opacity:1}}
@keyframes rlring2{0%,100%{opacity:0}50%{opacity:.62}}
/* THE TILES ARE THE FIELD'S CIRCLES. A disc, a ring round the rim carrying a
   real number (ui/railtiles.js, rlArcs), and the mark inside, which is what
   every orb on the glass bar is. The disc is 38 across inside the 44 target,
   so the floor holds and the circle does not touch its neighbour. What is
   chosen closes its ring in its own seat colour and breathes a second ring
   round it on the Field's wave; what is not chosen is the disc and the part
   of the ring the chosen one reaches into it, and nothing else. The primary
   breathes further than the rest of what is chosen. */
.gdom>.ib,.g6>.ib{border:0;border-radius:50%;background:transparent;box-shadow:none}
#ar1>.ib,#ar2>.ib{padding:0;border-width:0;background-clip:border-box}
.gdom>.ib::before,.g6>.ib::before{content:"";position:absolute;left:50%;top:50%;width:38px;height:38px;
 margin:-19px 0 0 -19px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--sunk);
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.06);
 transition:background-color var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.gdom>.ib:hover::before,.g6>.ib:hover::before{background:var(--panel-2)}
.gdom>.ib:hover,.g6>.ib:hover,.gdom>.ib[aria-pressed=true],.g6>.ib[aria-pressed=true]{background:transparent;
 border-color:transparent;box-shadow:none}
.ib svg:not(.ib-arc){position:relative;z-index:1;width:18px;height:18px}
.ib .ib-arc{position:absolute;left:50%;top:50%;width:38px;height:38px;margin:-19px 0 0 -19px;
 transform:rotate(-90deg);overflow:visible;stroke:none;transition:none}
.ib-arc circle{fill:none;stroke-width:2.4}
.ib-arc .trk{stroke:color-mix(in srgb,var(--ink) 13%,transparent)}
.ib-arc .val{stroke:var(--c);stroke-linecap:round;opacity:.5;stroke-dasharray:calc(var(--v,0) * 1) 100;
 transition:stroke-dasharray var(--t-surface) var(--ease-out),opacity var(--t-micro) var(--ease-out)}
.ib[aria-pressed=true]::before{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 12%,var(--panel-2))}
.ib[aria-pressed=true] .ib-arc .val{opacity:1}
.ib[aria-pressed=true] .ib-arc circle{stroke-width:3}
.ib[data-r="2"] .ib-arc .val{opacity:.88}
.ib[data-r="2"]::before{background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 6%,var(--sunk))}
.ib[aria-pressed=true]::after,.ib[data-r="2"]::after{content:"";position:absolute;left:50%;top:50%;
 width:44px;height:44px;margin:-22px 0 0 -22px;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid var(--c);
 pointer-events:none;animation:rlring var(--t-breath) var(--ease-breath) infinite;animation-delay:var(--bd,0s)}
.ib[data-r="2"]::after{animation-name:rlring2}
.ib:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:-3px;border-radius:50%}
/* ALIVE FROM THE MOMENT IT IS OPEN. A circle whose ring carries real weight,
   .18 and over, breathes its whole ring on the Field's wave at the phase of
   its seat, so a rail with a person in it moves on its own and a rail with
   nobody in it is still. The set is small and each is one opacity on one
   element, and it is set by rlArcs when a figure moves and never per frame. */
.ib.rl-hot .ib-arc,.aw-c.rl-hot .ib-arc{animation:rlarcb var(--t-breath) var(--ease-breath) infinite;animation-delay:var(--bd,0s)}
@keyframes rlarcb{0%,100%{opacity:.6}50%{opacity:1}}
body.quiet .ib.rl-hot .ib-arc,body.rm .ib.rl-hot .ib-arc,body.quiet .aw-c.rl-hot .ib-arc,body.rm .aw-c.rl-hot .ib-arc{animation:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.ib.rl-hot .ib-arc,.aw-c.rl-hot .ib-arc{animation:none}}
/* THE DISC IS ITS OWN GROUND IN EVERY LIGHTING. Glass, Punch and Lumen each
   paint the square cell (a pane, a solid, a solid) and each fills a chosen
   one with its seat colour. The cell is transparent in all three and the same
   three moves are made on the disc: Glass a pane, Punch and Lumen a solid
   that fills with the seat colour when chosen. Punch draws nothing as an
   outline, so it has no ring round the rim: the reach of the blueprint is
   the strength of the tint instead, which is the same number in its own
   material. */
body.glass .gdom>.ib,body.glass .g6>.ib,body.punch .gdom>.ib,body.punch .g6>.ib,
body.lumen .gdom>.ib,body.lumen .g6>.ib{background:transparent;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none;backdrop-filter:none}
body.glass .gdom>.ib:hover,body.glass .g6>.ib:hover,body.punch .ib[aria-pressed=true],body.punch .ib[data-r="2"],
body.lumen .ib[aria-pressed=true],body.lumen .ib[data-r="2"]{background:transparent}
body.glass .ib::before{background:rgba(255,255,255,.055)}
body.glass .ib:hover::before{background:rgba(255,255,255,.10)}
body.punch .ib::before,body.lumen .ib::before{background:var(--sunk)}
body.punch .ib[aria-pressed=true]::before,body.punch .ib[data-r="2"]::before,
body.lumen .ib[aria-pressed=true]::before,body.lumen .ib[data-r="2"]::before{background:var(--c,var(--accent))}
body.punch .ib:not([aria-pressed=true]):not([data-r="2"])::before{
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c,var(--accent)) calc(var(--v,0) * .32%),var(--sunk))}
body.punch .ib .ib-arc,body.punch .ib::after{display:none}
.rootb{position:relative}
.rootb[data-lit="1"]::after,.rootb[aria-pressed=true]::after{content:"";position:absolute;inset:-3px;
 border-radius:calc(var(--r-xs) + 3px);border:1.5px solid var(--rc);pointer-events:none;
 animation:rlring2 var(--t-breath) var(--ease-breath) infinite;animation-delay:var(--bd,0s)}
body.quiet .ib[aria-pressed=true]::after,body.rm .ib[aria-pressed=true]::after,
body.quiet .ib[data-r="2"]::after,body.rm .ib[data-r="2"]::after,
body.quiet .rootb::after,body.rm .rootb::after{animation:none;opacity:.7}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
 .ib[aria-pressed=true]::after,.ib[data-r="2"]::after,.rootb::after{animation:none;opacity:.7}}
/* the captions under the grids read as the right rail reads: ink for the name,
   the second ink for what it does, set from the left like every other line */
.cap{color:var(--mid);text-align:left}
.cap b{color:var(--ink)}
/* THE BLOCK HEADINGS CARRY A RULE, so Energy and Psyche read as two parts of
   one instrument and not as loose words between sections */
.rblk::after{content:"";flex:1;height:1px;background:var(--edge)}
.lsec .tier1{color:var(--dim)}

/* Nothing here is drawn on the stage. The first cut of this sheet carried a
   fixed layer of brackets and lines from the rail to the wheel, and it was
   ruled out twice, rounds OM and OO: whatever the left menu does, it does in
   the left menu. What is left is the circles, the map and the fold, and they
   are all in the rail. */
/* THE RAIL ANSWERS THE FIELD: what a pointer is over on the wheel is lit on
   the rail, in the colour of the thing, and let go when the pointer leaves */
.ib.rl-echo::before{box-shadow:0 0 0 2px var(--c),inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.06)}
.ib.rl-echo .ib-arc .val{opacity:1}
.rootb.rl-echo{border-color:var(--rc);color:var(--rc)}
/* THE FOUR SYSTEMS AND WHAT MEETS WHAT, ui/railtiles.js rlMeetHtml. Ink for the
   name, the accent for what is connected, the dim ink for what is idle: the
   same three tones the right rail reads in. */
.sp-map{margin:4px 0 8px}
.sp-mapsvg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;overflow:visible}
.sp-na{fill:none;stroke:var(--accent);stroke-linecap:round}
.sp-nr{fill:var(--panel);stroke:var(--edge-2);stroke-width:1.5}
.sp-sn{color:var(--dim)}
.sp-sn g{fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.sp-sn text{fill:var(--dim);stroke:none;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:11.5px}
.sp-sn.on{color:var(--accent)}
.sp-sn.on .sp-nr{stroke:var(--accent)}
.sp-sn.on text{fill:var(--ink)}
.sp-sn.off{opacity:.45}
.sp-nc circle{fill:var(--panel);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.5}
.sp-nc g{fill:none;stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:3.2;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.sp-pulse{fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 58%,var(--ink))}
body.quiet .sp-pulse,body.rm .sp-pulse{display:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.sp-pulse{display:none}}


/* ============================================================
   THE SIX READINGS ON ONE WIRE, ui/railwire.js. Layout and paint only; every
   number that moves is written by the script from a reading. The gutter
   belongs to the column that stands beside the stage: a stacked rail on a
   phone gives the bars the width back. The gutter itself is the left padding
   of the one .fdock rule above, 20px, because a class is declared once.
   ============================================================ */
.rw-bus{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;bottom:0;width:20px;pointer-events:none;opacity:0;
 transition:opacity var(--t-enter) var(--ease-enter) 200ms}
.rw-bus.in{opacity:1}
.rw-bus svg{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;overflow:visible}
.rw-w{fill:none;stroke:var(--edge-2);stroke-width:1.4}
.rw-ws{fill:none;stroke:var(--accent);stroke-linecap:round}
.rw-tap{fill:none;stroke:var(--edge-2);stroke-width:1}
.rw-n{fill:var(--panel);stroke-width:1.7}
.rw-halo{fill:var(--accent);transform-box:fill-box;transform-origin:center;animation:rwhalo var(--t-breath) var(--ease-breath) infinite}
@keyframes rwhalo{0%,100%{transform:scale(.88)}50%{transform:scale(1.14)}}
body.quiet .rw-halo,body.rm .rw-halo{animation:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.rw-halo{animation:none}}
.rw-rn{fill:var(--panel);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.5}
.rw-rc{fill:var(--accent)}
/* a pulse is a dash eight pixels long, the Field's own scale, in the accent
   whitened so it reads on every lighting, and it only ever has a transform
   and an opacity written */
.rw-p{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;width:2px;height:8px;margin:-4px 0 0 -1px;border-radius:2px;
 background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 55%,#fff);opacity:0;will-change:transform,opacity;pointer-events:none;
 box-shadow:0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 70%,transparent)}
body.quiet .rw-p,body.rm .rw-p{display:none}
body.quiet .rw-bus,body.rm .rw-bus{transition:none}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){.rw-p{display:none}.rw-bus{transition:none}}
/* RADIANCE, the junction the three vitals run into. Not a control: nothing
   here is pressed, so it takes no tap target. */
.rw-rad{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;height:24px;padding:0 12px;
 font-size:11.5px;color:var(--mid);letter-spacing:.01em}
.rw-rad b{font-family:var(--num);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-weight:600;color:var(--mid);font-size:12px}
@media (max-width:1180px){.fdock{padding-left:0}.rw-bus,.rw-rad{display:none}}


/* ORIENTATION AND BALANCE, UNDER THE READINGS, round OM. One instrument: the
   same left edge, the same width, the same 44px track on the same ground and
   the same hairline between the groups as the six rows above. The two
   headings are the quiet kind, a mark and a word, because the track under
   each already says what it is. */
.fdial{margin-top:6px;padding-top:6px;border-top:1px solid var(--edge)}
.fdl{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;height:24px;font-size:12px;font-weight:500;color:var(--mid);letter-spacing:.01em}
.fdl-ic{width:16px;height:16px;flex:0 0 auto;fill:none;stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.7;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
/* the dials' fills sit as quiet as the bars' own, so the green and the gold of a
   dial do not out shout the readings above it */
.fdial .ax-tr .fill{opacity:.52}
.fdial .polbar{margin-bottom:6px}
.fdial .fdl + .polbar + .fdl{margin-top:10px}
.fdial .bal{padding:0 0 2px;min-height:0;margin-bottom:2px}
.fdial .ax-pick{margin:8px 0 0}
@media (max-width:1180px){.fdial{margin-top:10px}}


/* THE AWARENESS FOLD, round OM, ui/railtiles.js awSum. Two lines of what was
   chosen, shown only while the grids above them are shut. Each line is one
   44px button and the circles in it are marks, drawn as the grid draws them:
   a disc, a ring carrying the reading, the glyph in the seat's colour, and
   the breath of the chosen. */
.aw-sum{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;padding-bottom:12px}
.lsec.open .aw-sum{display:none}
.aw-r{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;width:100%;min-height:var(--tap);padding:0 2px;background:transparent;
 border:0;border-radius:var(--r-xs);cursor:pointer;color:var(--ink);font:inherit;text-align:left}
.aw-r:hover{background:var(--sunk)}
.aw-r:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:-2px}
.aw-k{flex:0 0 62px;font-size:12px;color:var(--dim)}
.aw-cs{display:flex;gap:8px;flex:0 0 auto}
.aw-c{position:relative;display:block;width:38px;height:38px;flex:0 0 auto}
.aw-c::before{content:"";position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:50%;background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 12%,var(--panel-2));
 box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.06)}
.aw-c svg:not(.ib-arc){position:absolute;left:10px;top:10px;width:18px;height:18px;fill:none;stroke:var(--c);
 stroke-width:1.8;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round}
.aw-c .ib-arc{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;width:38px;height:38px;transform:rotate(-90deg);overflow:visible}
